{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 8666 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "aa3645828e485616dc45b0c16d2020058823d5a5a78281493caffc5c54823098" :edb/db-before-t 8665 :edb/db-after-t 8666 :edb/tx-data [[13194139541978 50 #inst "2026-10-27T03:20:21.793Z" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186054347 1074 "We need to find the point of intersection of the two lines.\n\nFrom the graph, we can see that the solution should lie between $x=13$ and $x=14$. So, we can disregard all values of $x$ that are not between $13$ and $14$.\n\nNow, we evaluate the table entries for $x=13.2$ and $x=13.6$, as follows:\n\n- Substituting $x=13.2$ into the expressions for $y_1$ and $y_2$, we get the following:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =0.43x+6 \\\\  & =0.43(13.2)+6 \\\\  & =11.676 \\\\ {y}_{2} & =0.65x+3 \\\\  & =0.65(13.2)+3 \\\\  & =11.58\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThen, we find the corresponding error:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}| & =|11.676-11.58| \\\\  & =|0.096| \\\\  & =0.096\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Substituting $x=13.6$ into the expressions for $y_1$ and $y_2$, we get the following:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =0.43x+6 \\\\  & =0.43(13.6)+6 \\\\  & =11.848 \\\\ {y}_{2} & =0.65x+3 \\\\  & =0.65(13.6)+3 \\\\  & =11.84\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThen, we find the corresponding error:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}| & =|11.848-11.84| \\\\  & =|0.008| \\\\  & =0.008\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe fill this information into the table:\n\n| $x$ | $13.2$ | $13.6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $y_1=0.43x+6$ | $11.676$ | $11.848$ |\n| $y_2=0.65x+3$ | $11.58$ | $11.84$ |\n| $\\mid y_1-y_2 \\mid$ | $0.096$ | $0.008$ |\n\nThe smallest error is $0.008$, which corresponds to $x=13.6$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nx\\approx 13.6\n$$\n\nis our approximate solution." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186054347 1074 "We need to find the point of intersection of the two lines.\n\nFrom the graph, we can see that the solution should lie between $x=13$ and $x=14$. So, we can disregard all values of $x$ that are not between $13$ and $14$.\n\nNow, we evaluate the table entries for $x=13.2$ and $x=13.6$, as follows:\n\n- Substituting $x=13.2$ into the expressions for $y_1$ and $y_2$, we get the following:\n$$\n\\displaystyle y_{1}|= 0.43x + 6; = 0.43(13.2) + 6; = 11.676; y_{2}|= 0.65x + 3; = 0.65(13.2) + 3; = 11.58\n$$\nThen, we find the corresponding error:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}| & =|11.676-11.58| \\\\  & =|0.096| \\\\  & =0.096\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Substituting $x=13.6$ into the expressions for $y_1$ and $y_2$, we get the following:\n$$\n\\displaystyle y_{1}|= 0.43x + 6; = 0.43(13.6) + 6; = 11.848; y_{2}|= 0.65x + 3; = 0.65(13.6) + 3; = 11.84\n$$\nThen, we find the corresponding error:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}| & =|11.848-11.84| \\\\  & =|0.008| \\\\  & =0.008\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe fill this information into the table:\n\n| $x$ | $13.2$ | $13.6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $y_1=0.43x+6$ | $11.676$ | $11.848$ |\n| $y_2=0.65x+3$ | $11.58$ | $11.84$ |\n| $\\mid y_1-y_2 \\mid$ | $0.096$ | $0.008$ |\n\nThe smallest error is $0.008$, which corresponds to $x=13.6$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nx\\approx 13.6\n$$\n\nis our approximate solution." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186054415 1074 "We factor in two steps:\n\n1. Separate the greatest common factor from each of the terms. In this case, $\\textrm{GCF}(6a,30) = 6$, so:\n$6a + 30|=; (6 \\cdot a) + (6 \\cdot 5)$\n2. Place the common factor in front of the parentheses, leaving whatever remains inside the parentheses:\n$(6 \\cdot a) + (6 \\cdot 5)|=; 6(a + 5)$\n\nThus, the factored form of the expression is $6(a+5)$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186054415 1074 "We factor in two steps:\n\n1. Separate the greatest common factor from each of the terms. In this case, $\\textrm{GCF}(6a,30) = 6$, so:\n$\\begin{aligned}6a+30 &= (6\\cdot a)+(6\\cdot 5)\\end{aligned}$\n2. Place the common factor in front of the parentheses, leaving whatever remains inside the parentheses:\n$\\begin{aligned}(6\\cdot a)+(6\\cdot 5) &= 6(a+5)\\end{aligned}$\n\nThus, the factored form of the expression is $6(a+5)$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186054416 1074 "Both terms are negative, so we begin by factoring out a negative sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5a - 35 &= - (5a + 35)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we continue factoring in two steps:\n\n1. Separate the greatest common factor from each of the terms. In this case, $\\textrm{GCF}(5a,35) = 5$, so\n$- (5a + 35)|=; - (5 \\cdot a + 5 \\cdot 7)$\n2. Place the common factor in front of the parentheses, leaving whatever remains inside the parentheses:\n$- (5 \\cdot a + 5 \\cdot 7)|=; - 5(a + 7)$\n\nThus, the factored form of the expression is $-5(a+7)$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186054416 1074 "Both terms are negative, so we begin by factoring out a negative sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5a - 35 &= - (5a + 35)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we continue factoring in two steps:\n\n1. Separate the greatest common factor from each of the terms. In this case, $\\textrm{GCF}(5a,35) = 5$, so\n$\\begin{aligned}-(5a+35) &= -(5\\cdot a+5\\cdot 7)\\end{aligned}$\n2. Place the common factor in front of the parentheses, leaving whatever remains inside the parentheses:\n$\\begin{aligned}-(5\\cdot a+5\\cdot 7) &= -5(a+7)\\end{aligned}$\n\nThus, the factored form of the expression is $-5(a+7)$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186054417 1074 "We factor in two steps:\n\n1. Separate the greatest common factor from each of the terms (ignoring any negative signs). In this case, $\\textrm{GCF}(27a, 33b, 30) = 3$, so:\n$27a + 33b - 30|=; 3 \\cdot 9a + 3 \\cdot 11b - 3 \\cdot 10$\n2. Place the common factor in front of the parentheses, leaving whatever remains inside the parentheses:\n$3 \\cdot 9a + 3 \\cdot 11b - 3 \\cdot 10|=; 3(9a + 11b - 10)$\n\nThus, the factored form of the expression is $3(9a+11b-10)$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186054417 1074 "We factor in two steps:\n\n1. Separate the greatest common factor from each of the terms (ignoring any negative signs). In this case, $\\textrm{GCF}(27a, 33b, 30) = 3$, so:\n$\\begin{aligned}27a+33b-30 &= 3\\cdot 9a+3\\cdot 11b-3\\cdot 10\\end{aligned}$\n2. Place the common factor in front of the parentheses, leaving whatever remains inside the parentheses:\n$\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 9a+3\\cdot 11b-3\\cdot 10 &= 3(9a+11b-10)\\end{aligned}$\n\nThus, the factored form of the expression is $3(9a+11b-10)$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186055961 1064 "Suppose that we want to calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pi/2}\\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{2\\cos{x}}\n$$\n\nIf we attempt to evaluate this limit by directly substituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nwe get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  π/2}\\frac{\\sin 2x}{2\\cos x} &= (\\sin (2 \\cdot \\frac{π}{2}))/(2\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right)) \\\\\n&= (\\sin (π))/(2\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we simplify the fraction before attempting to evaluate the limit, we obtain a more clear result.\n\nTo simplify the fraction in this particular case, the double angle formula for sine will be useful:\n\n$$\n\\sin{2x}=2\\sin{x}\\cos{x}\n$$\n\nUsing the double angle formula above, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to  \\pi  / 2} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sin   2 x}{2 \\cos   x}} & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  \\pi  / 2} {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 \\sin   x \\cos   x}{2 \\cos   x}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  \\pi  / 2} {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 \\sin   x \\cancel{\\cos   x}}{2 \\cancel{\\cos   x}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  \\pi  / 2} \\sin   x \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\sin   ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{2}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 1.\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nIn other cases, we may need to use a different trigonometric identity." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186055961 1064 "Suppose that we want to calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pi/2}\\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{2\\cos{x}}\n$$\n\nIf we attempt to evaluate this limit by directly substituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nwe get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  π/2}\\frac{\\sin 2x}{2\\cos x} &= (\\sin (2 \\cdot \\frac{π}{2}))/(2\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right)) \\\\\n&= (\\sin (π))/(2\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we simplify the fraction before attempting to evaluate the limit, we obtain a more clear result.\n\nTo simplify the fraction in this particular case, the double angle formula for sine will be useful:\n\n$$\n\\sin{2x}=2\\sin{x}\\cos{x}\n$$\n\nUsing the double angle formula above, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle lim_{x \\to  \\pi  / 2} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sin   2 x}{2 \\cos   x}} & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  \\pi  / 2} {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 \\sin   x \\cos   x}{2 \\cos   x}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  \\pi  / 2} {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 \\sin   x \\cancel{\\cos   x}}{2 \\cancel{\\cos   x}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  \\pi  / 2} \\sin   x \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\sin   ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{2}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 1.\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nIn other cases, we may need to use a different trigonometric identity." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186055964 1064 "It's possible to perform arithmetic with functions, just like with numbers. Shown below are the four arithmetic operations, addition $(+)$, subtraction $(-)$, multiplication $(\\cdot)$, and division $(/)$, applied to the functions\n\n$$\nf(x) = 4x + 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ng(x) = x - 2\n$$\n\n- **Addition**\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned} (f + g)(x) &= f(x) + g(x) \\\\ &= (4x + 1) + (x - 2) \\\\ &= 4x + 1 + x - 2 \\\\ &= 5x - 1 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n- **Subtraction**\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f-g)(x) & =f(x)-g(x) \\\\  & =(4x+1)-(x-2) \\\\  & =4x+1-x+2 \\\\  & =3x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- **Multiplication**\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(x) & =f(x)g(x) \\\\  & =(4x+1)(x-2) \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-8x+x-2 \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-7x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- **Division**\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f/g)(x) & =\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4x+1}{x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In the case of division, we must have that\n\n$$\ng(x)\\neq 0\n$$\n\nso that we avoid dividing by zero." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186055964 1064 "It's possible to perform arithmetic with functions, just like with numbers. Shown below are the four arithmetic operations, addition $(+)$, subtraction $(-)$, multiplication $(\\cdot)$, and division $(/)$, applied to the functions\n\n$$\nf(x) = 4x + 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ng(x) = x - 2\n$$\n\n- **Addition**\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned} (f + g)(x) &= f(x) + g(x) \\\\ &= (4x + 1) + (x - 2) \\\\ &= 4x + 1 + x - 2 \\\\ &= 5x - 1 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n- **Subtraction**\n$$\n\\displaystyle (f - g)(x)|= f(x) - g(x); = (4x + 1) - (x - 2); = 4x + 1 - x + 2; = 3x + 3\n$$\n- **Multiplication**\n$$\n\\displaystyle (fg)(x)|= f(x)g(x); = (4x + 1)(x - 2); = 4x^{2} - 8x + x - 2; = 4x^{2} - 7x - 2\n$$\n- **Division**\n$$\n\\displaystyle (f/g)(x)|= (f(x))/(g(x)); = \\frac{4x + 1}{x - 2}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In the case of division, we must have that\n\n$$\ng(x)\\neq 0\n$$\n\nso that we avoid dividing by zero." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186056027 1064 "**Factoring** is the opposite of distributing. To illustrate, consider the expression $4(a-2b)$. If you consider distributing as going forward,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(a - 2b) &= 4 \\cdot a - 4 \\cdot 2b \\\\ &= 4a - 8b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen factoring would be going in reverse:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4a - 8b &= (4 \\cdot a)-(4 \\cdot 2b) \\\\ &= 4(a - 2b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, you would say that $\\color{red}4$ was **factored out** of $4a-8b$.\n\nTo factor any expression, we just need to follow these two steps:\n\n1. Separate the greatest common factor from each of the terms (ignoring any negative signs). In this case, $\\textrm{GCF}(4a,8b) = {\\color{red}{4}}$, so:\n$4a - 8b|=; (4 \\cdot a) - (4 \\cdot 2b)$\n2. Place the common factor in front of the parentheses, leaving whatever remains inside the parentheses:\n$(4 \\cdot a) - (4 \\cdot 2b)|=; 4(a - 2b)$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186056027 1064 "**Factoring** is the opposite of distributing. To illustrate, consider the expression $4(a-2b)$. If you consider distributing as going forward,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(a - 2b) &= 4 \\cdot a - 4 \\cdot 2b \\\\ &= 4a - 8b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen factoring would be going in reverse:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4a - 8b &= (4 \\cdot a)-(4 \\cdot 2b) \\\\ &= 4(a - 2b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, you would say that $\\color{red}4$ was **factored out** of $4a-8b$.\n\nTo factor any expression, we just need to follow these two steps:\n\n1. Separate the greatest common factor from each of the terms (ignoring any negative signs). In this case, $\\textrm{GCF}(4a,8b) = {\\color{red}{4}}$, so:\n$\\begin{aligned}4a-8b &= ({\\color{red}{4}}\\cdot a)-({\\color{red}{4}}\\cdot 2b)\\end{aligned}$\n2. Place the common factor in front of the parentheses, leaving whatever remains inside the parentheses:\n$\\begin{aligned}({\\color{red}{4}}\\cdot a)-({\\color{red}{4}}\\cdot 2b) &= {\\color{red}{4}}(a-2b)\\end{aligned}$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186056069 1064 "Let's think about how to factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 7x + 12\n$$\n\nThis trinomial is neither a perfect square nor a difference of squares. In such cases, the factoring process requires a little trial and error.\n\nWe can factor this trinomial in four steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the factor pairs of the constant term $(12)$:The factor pairs of $12$ are\n$(1, 12), (2, 6), (3, 4)$.\n- **Step 2:** Determine which of these factor pairs add up to the coefficient of the linear term $(7)$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+12 & =13 & {\\color{red}{\\times }} \\\\ 2+6 & =8 & {\\color{red}{\\times }} \\\\ 3+4 & =7 & {\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- **Step 3:** Using the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $7x=3x+4x$:\n$\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+7x+12 &= {x}^{2}+\\underbrace{3x+4x}_{7x}+12\\end{aligned}$\n- **Step 4:** Factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3x+4x+12 &= ({x}^{2}+3x)+(4x+12) \\\\ &= x(x+3)+4(x+3) \\\\ &= (x+4)(x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nBefore we continue, note the following:\n\n- The process carried out in step 4 is sometimes called **factoring by grouping.** This is because we group two sets of terms and factor them individually before factoring the entire expression.\n- We can check that our answer is correct by multiplying out the factors:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)(x+3) & =x(x+3)+4(x+3) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+3x+4x+12 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+7x+12\\hspace{2em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186056069 1064 "Let's think about how to factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 7x + 12\n$$\n\nThis trinomial is neither a perfect square nor a difference of squares. In such cases, the factoring process requires a little trial and error.\n\nWe can factor this trinomial in four steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the factor pairs of the constant term $(12)$:The factor pairs of $12$ are\n$(1, 12), (2, 6), (3, 4)$.\n- **Step 2:** Determine which of these factor pairs add up to the coefficient of the linear term $(7)$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+12 & =13 & {\\color{red}{\\times }} \\\\ 2+6 & =8 & {\\color{red}{\\times }} \\\\ 3+4 & =7 & {\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- **Step 3:** Using the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $7x=3x+4x$:\n$x^{2} + 7x + 12|=; x^{2} + \\underbrace{3x + 4x}_{7x} + 12$\n- **Step 4:** Factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n$$\n\\displaystyle x^{2} + 3x + 4x + 12|=; (x^{2} + 3x) + (4x + 12)|=; x(x + 3) + 4(x + 3)|=; (x + 4)(x + 3)\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nBefore we continue, note the following:\n\n- The process carried out in step 4 is sometimes called **factoring by grouping.** This is because we group two sets of terms and factor them individually before factoring the entire expression.\n- We can check that our answer is correct by multiplying out the factors:\n$$\n\\displaystyle (x + 4)(x + 3)|= x(x + 3) + 4(x + 3); = x^{2} + 3x + 4x + 12; = x^{2} + 7x + 12✓\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186059611 1074 "We start by using the definition of congruence modulo $n$. Recall that\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: n) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad n \\mid (a-b) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad a-b = nk\n$$\n\nfor some integer $k$.\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n$a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 7) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad a-4 \\equiv b-4 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 7)$\n\nSo, we can reason as follows:\n\n> Since $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 7)$, by the definition of modular congruence,\n> $7 \\mid (a-b) \\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad a-b = 7k$\n> for some integer $k$.\n\nNow, we express the difference between $a-4$ and $b-4$ through the difference between $a$ and $b{:}$\n\n> Now, notice that\n> $(a - 4) - (b - 4)|= a - 4 - b + 4; = a - b; = 7k$.\n\nFinally, we use the definition of congruence in reverse:\n\n> Therefore, according to the definition of congruence, we have\n> $a-4 \\equiv b-4 \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 7)$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186059611 1074 "We start by using the definition of congruence modulo $n$. Recall that\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: n) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad n \\mid (a-b) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad a-b = nk\n$$\n\nfor some integer $k$.\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n$a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 7) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad a-4 \\equiv b-4 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 7)$\n\nSo, we can reason as follows:\n\n> Since $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 7)$, by the definition of modular congruence,\n> $7 \\mid (a-b) \\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad a-b = 7k$\n> for some integer $k$.\n\nNow, we express the difference between $a-4$ and $b-4$ through the difference between $a$ and $b{:}$\n\n> Now, notice that\n> $\\begin{aligned}(a-4)-(b-4) & =a-4-b+4 \\\\  & =a-b \\\\  & =7k.\\end{aligned}$.\n\nFinally, we use the definition of congruence in reverse:\n\n> Therefore, according to the definition of congruence, we have\n> $a-4 \\equiv b-4 \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 7)$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186059612 1074 "We start by using the definition of congruent numbers. Recall that\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: n) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad n \\mid (a-b) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad a-b = nk\n$$\n\nfor some integer $k$.\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n$a \\equiv b \\: \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 8a \\equiv 8b \\quad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 10)$\n\nSo, we can reason as follows:\n\n> Since $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 10)$, by the definition of modular congruence,\n> $10 \\mid (a-b) \\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad a - b = 10k$\n> for some integer $k$.\n\nThe equation above involves the difference between $a$ and $b$. But we want a relationship between $8a$ and $8b$. To get an expression involving $8a$ and $8b$, we can multiply the above equation by $8{:}$\n\n> Now, multiplying both sides of $a - b = 10k$ by $8$, we get\n> $8 \\cdot (a - b)|= 8 \\cdot 10k; 8a - 8b|= 10(8k); 8a - 8b|= 10m$,\n> where $m = 8k$ is an integer.\n\nFinally, we use the definition of modular congruence in reverse:\n\n> According to the definition of modular congruence, the final equation implies that\n> $8a \\equiv 8b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 10)$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186059612 1074 "We start by using the definition of congruent numbers. Recall that\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: n) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad n \\mid (a-b) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad a-b = nk\n$$\n\nfor some integer $k$.\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n$a \\equiv b \\: \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 8a \\equiv 8b \\quad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 10)$\n\nSo, we can reason as follows:\n\n> Since $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 10)$, by the definition of modular congruence,\n> $10 \\mid (a-b) \\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad a - b = 10k$\n> for some integer $k$.\n\nThe equation above involves the difference between $a$ and $b$. But we want a relationship between $8a$ and $8b$. To get an expression involving $8a$ and $8b$, we can multiply the above equation by $8{:}$\n\n> Now, multiplying both sides of $a - b = 10k$ by $8$, we get\n> $\\begin{aligned}8\\cdot (a-b) & =8\\cdot 10k \\\\ 8a-8b & =10(8k) \\\\ 8a-8b & =10m,\\end{aligned}$,\n> where $m = 8k$ is an integer.\n\nFinally, we use the definition of modular congruence in reverse:\n\n> According to the definition of modular congruence, the final equation implies that\n> $8a \\equiv 8b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 10)$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186060900 1064 "The following identities are called the **cofunction identities**. We can split them into three main groups.\n\n- Sine and **co**sine:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}x & =\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}x & =\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Tangent and **co**tangent:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}x & =\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}x & =\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secant and **co**secant:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}x & =\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x) \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}x & =\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541978 17