displaystyle (λa) \\cdot b|= λ(a \\cdot b); a \\cdot (λb)|= λ(a \\cdot b)\n$$\n- The dot product is commutative:\n$\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = \\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{a}$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186060981 1064 "Let's construct a direct proof of the following statement:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{a \\equiv b}_{P(a,b)} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{a+2 \\equiv b+2}_{Q(a,b)} \\qquad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 5)\n$$\n\nWe start by assuming the antecedent $P$ is true.\n\n> *Assume that* $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 5)$.\n\nNext, we state what this means using the definition of congruence.\n\n> *By the definition of modular congruence,*\n> $5 \\mid (a-b) \\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad a-b = 5k$\n> *for some integer $k$.*\n\nNow, the goal is to show that\n\n$$\n(a+2) \\equiv (b+2) \\quad \\Leftrightarrow\\quad 5 \\mid \\big((a+2) - (b+2)\\big)\n$$\n\nSo, we write down the difference between $(a+2)$ and $(b+2)$ and show that it is a multiple of $5$ using our assumption regarding the difference between $a$ and $b{:}$\n\n> *Hence, we get*\n> $(a + 2) - (b + 2)|= a + 2 - b - 2; = a - b; = 5k$.\n\nSince $(a+2) - (b+2)$ is a multiple of $5$, this shows that $(a+2) \\equiv (b+2)$ modulo $5$. So, our proof is complete, and we write our conclusion.\n\n> *Therefore, according to the definition of congruence, we have*\n> $a+2 \\equiv b+2 \\quad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 5)$.\n\nNow that we've discussed all the details, let's write down our final proof." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186060981 1064 "Let's construct a direct proof of the following statement:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{a \\equiv b}_{P(a,b)} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{a+2 \\equiv b+2}_{Q(a,b)} \\qquad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 5)\n$$\n\nWe start by assuming the antecedent $P$ is true.\n\n> *Assume that* $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 5)$.\n\nNext, we state what this means using the definition of congruence.\n\n> *By the definition of modular congruence,*\n> $5 \\mid (a-b) \\qquad\\Le