105 1074 "First, we find the intersections of the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= g(x) \\\\\nx^{3} + x^{2} + 1 &= x^{2} + x + 1 \\\\\nx^{3} - x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x^{2} - 1) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, ± 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area we want consists of two regions: $A_1$ and $A_2$.\n\n![](images/03/03ec1d82eb7375ce3f39a6e55d2271125d55db3fe957ac2c6a97f4dc6754de8d.png)\n\n- For the area $A_1$, we have $x \\in [-1,0]$, and the upper function is $f(x)$ while the lower function is $g(x)$. So, the area is given by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(f(x)-g(x))\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(({x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+1)-({x}^{2}+x+1))\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}){|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =0-\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the area $A_2$, we have $x \\in [0,1]$, and the upper function is $g(x)$ while the lower function is $f(x)$. So, the area is given by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(g(x)-f(x))\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(({x}^{2}+x+1)-({x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+1))\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}-x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= A_{1} + A_{2} = \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186064105 1074 "First, we find the intersections of the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= g(x) \\\\\nx^{3} + x^{2} + 1 &= x^{2} + x + 1 \\\\\nx^{3} - x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x^{2} - 1) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, ± 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area we want consists of two regions: $A_1$ and $A_2$.\n\n![](images/03/03ec1d82eb7375ce3f39a6e55d2271125d55db