 statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle DEF$ are congruent by ASA since we have the following pairs of congruent angles and included sides:$\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{DE}$$\\angle A \\cong \\angle D$$\\angle B \\cong \\angle E$\n- Statement II is false. Since the triangles are congruent, all the corresponding sides must be congruent. In particular,\n$$\n\\displaystyle BC|= EF; 2x - 5|= 5; 2x|= 10; x|= 5 \\ne 1.\n$$\n- Statement III is true. Since the triangles are congruent, all the corresponding sides must be congruent. In particular,\n$$\n\\displaystyle AC|= DF; 3|= 4y - 1; 4y|= 4; y|= 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186073652 1074 "Partitioning a line segment $\\overline{AB}$ into a ratio $a:b$ means we must\n\n- divide the segment into $a+b$ equal parts, and\n- find the point $C$ that is $a$ equal parts from $A$.\n\nIn our case, the ratio $1:3$ implies\n\n$$\nAC:CB = 1:3\n$$\n\nThis means\n\n- there are $1+3 = 4$ equal parts, and\n- we want to find the point $C$ that is $1$ equal part from $A$. So, we have\n$AC= \\dfrac{1}{4}AB$.\n\nNext, consider another point $X$ that is horizontal with $A$ and directly below $C$, as shown in the diagram.\n\n![](images/29/29b01b5cc8cca113a14c16f95626faadb70993bbb42fab7fd77f011ecf0de734.png)\n\nTo get from point $A$ to point $C$, we can \"run\" horizontally from $A$ to $X$ and then \"rise\" vertically from $X$ to $C$:\n\n- The $x$-coordinate of $C$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{C} & ={x}_{A}+{\\color{black}{\\frac{1}{4}({x}_{B}-{x}_{A})}} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{4}(6-2) \\\\  & =2+1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The $y$-coordinate of $C$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{C} & ={y}_{A}+{\\color{black}{\\frac{1}{4}({y}_{B}-{y}_{A})}} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{4}(9-1) \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $C$ has coordinates\n\n$$\n\\left(3, 3 \\right)\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186073652 1074 "Partitioning a line segment $\\overline{AB}$ into a ratio $a:b$ means we must\n\n- divide the segment into $a+b$ equal parts, and\n- find the point $C$ that is $a$ equal parts from $A$.\n\nIn our case, the ratio $1:3$ implies\n\n$$\nAC:CB = 1:3\n$$\n\nThis means\n\n- there are $1+3 = 4$ equal parts, and\n- we want to find the point $C$ that is $1$ equal part from $A$. So, we have\n$AC= \\dfrac{1}{4}AB$.\n\nNext, consider another point $X$ that is horizontal with $A$ and directly below $C$, as shown in the diagram.\n\n![](images/29/29b01b5cc8cca113a14c16f95626faadb70993bbb42fab7fd77f011ecf0de734.png)\n\nTo get from point $A$ to point $C$, we can \"run\" horizontally from $A$ to $X$ and then \"rise\" vertically from $X$ to $C$:\n\n- The $x$-coordinate of $C$ is\n$$\n\\displaystyle x_{C}|= x_{A} + \\frac{1}{4}(x_{B} - x_{A}); = 2 + \\frac{1}{4}(6 - 2); = 2 + 1; = 3.\n$$\n- The $y$-coordinate of $C$ is\n$$\n\\displaystyle y_{C}|= y_{A} + \\frac{1}{4}(y_{B} - y_{A}); = 1 + \\frac{1}{4}(9 - 1); = 1 + 2; = 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $C$ has coordinates\n\n$$\n\\left(3, 3 \\right)\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186073662 1074 "First, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠DAB &= m∠DAE + m∠EAB \\\\\n&= 21^{∘} + 45^{∘} \\\\\n&= 66^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\triangle{ADB}$ and $\\triangle{ACB}$ are inscribed in the circle, where the side $\\overline{AB}$ is a diameter. Therefore,\n\n$$\nm\\angle{ADB}=m\\angle{ACB}=90^\\circ\n$$\n\n- For $\\triangle{ADB}$, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\\mathrm{∠}ABD & ={90}^{∘}-m\\mathrm{∠}DAB \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}-{66}^{∘} \\\\  & ={24}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For $\\triangle{ACB}$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\\mathrm{∠}ABC & ={90}^{∘}-m\\mathrm{∠}CAB \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠EBC &= m∠ABC - m∠ABD \\\\\n&= 45^{∘} - 24^{∘} \\\\\n&= 21^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186073662 1074 "First, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠DAB &= m∠DAE + m∠EAB \\\\\n&= 21^{∘} + 45^{∘} \\\\\n&= 66^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\triangle{ADB}$ and $\\triangle{ACB}$ are inscribed in the circle, where the side $\\overline{AB}$ is a diameter. Therefore,\n\n$$\nm\\angle{ADB}=m\\angle{ACB}=90^\\circ\n$$\n\n- For $\\triangle{ADB}$, we obtain\n$$\n\\displaystyle m∠ABD|= 90^{∘} - m∠DAB; = 90^{∘} - 66^{∘}; = 24^{∘}.\n$$\n- For $\\triangle{ACB}$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle m∠ABC|= 90^{∘} - m∠CAB; = 90^{∘} - 45^{∘}; = 45^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠EBC &= m∠ABC - m∠ABD \\\\\n&= 45^{∘} - 24^{∘} \\\\\n&= 21^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186074936 1064 "![](images/8f/8f850ea0f0b0cdd54995561870cd8d896f290b9fd6b07de11ca0674c1a8a412b.png)\n\nOur goal is to prove the following:\n\n> $m\\angle{IHG} = m\\angle{F} + m\\angle{G}$\n\nTo prove this theorem, we construct a ray from the point $H$ that's parallel to $\\overline{FG}$, and then apply the corresponding angles postulate and alternate interior angles theorem.\n\nWe complete the proof as follows:\n\n- **Step 1**: We start by constructing a line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{HJ}$ through the point $H$ and parallel to $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{FG}$.![](images/ca/ca53ee7d8d2f6390e9aa8692bc0f1400aa78f84cf5afa0457b13f86f0921199e.png)\n- **Step 2**: Since $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{FG} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{HJ}$, we have that the corresponding angles $\\angle{IHJ}$ and $\\angle{F}$ are congruent by the *corresponding angles postulate*.Let's add this to our diagram.![](images/18/185672f4600e86a4e6e92e49d46cf966a46206128b4113ec4e070ff987cb5a7b.png)\n- **Step 3**: Since $\\angle{IHJ} \\cong \\angle{F}$ are congruent, we have\n$m\\angle{IHJ} = m\\angle{F}$\nby the *definition of congruent angles*.\n- **Step 4**: Since $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{FG}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{HJ}$ are parallel, we have that the alternate interior angles $\\angle{JHG}$ and $\\angle{G}$ are congruent by the *alternate interior angles theorem*.Let's add this to our diagram.![](images/f4/f441d13eb67dbbd6d301d479dd3ac0d6dd0ac79fd6fbc56c8fc90521f5c7cd24.png)\n- **Step 5**: Since $\\angle{JHG}$ and $\\angle{G}$ are congruent, we have\n$m\\angle{JHG} = m\\angle{G}$\nby the *definition of congruent angles*.\n- **Step 6**: In steps 3 and 5, we showed that $m\\angle{IHJ}=m\\angle{F}$ and $m\\angle{JHG}=m\\angle{G}$, respectively. So, by the *addition principle*, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\\mathrm{∠}IHJ & =m\\mathrm{∠}F \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}IHJ+m\\mathrm{∠}JHG & =m\\mathrm{∠}F+m\\mathrm{∠}JHG \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}IHJ+m\\mathrm{∠}JHG & =m\\mathrm{∠}F+m\\mathrm{∠}G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- **Step 7**: Notice that $\\overline{HJ}$ is the common side of the angles $\\angle{IHJ}$ and $\\angle{JHG}$. Therefore, by the *angle addition postulate*, we have that\n$m\\angle{IHG} = m\\angle{IHJ} + m\\angle{JHG}$.\n- **Step 8**: In steps 7 and 6, respectively, we showed that\n${\\color{blue}m\\angle{IHG}} = m\\angle{IHJ} + m\\angle{JHG}$,\nand\n$m\\angle{IHJ} + m\\angle{JHG} = {\\color{blue}m\\angle{F} + m\\angle{G}}$.\nTherefore, by the *transitive property of equality*, we get\n${\\color{blue}m\\angle{IHG}} = {\\color{blue}m\\angle{F} + m\\angle{G}}$,\nas required.\n\nAgain, it's **highly recommended** that you practice reconstructing this proof." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186074936 1064 "![](images/8f/8f850ea0f0b0cdd54995561870cd8d896f290b9fd6b07de11ca0674c1a8a412b.png)\n\nOur goal is to prove the following:\n\n> $m\\angle{IHG} = m\\angle{F} + m\\angle{G}$\n\nTo prove this theorem, we construct a ray from the point $H$ that's parallel to $\\overline{FG}$, and then apply the corresponding angles postulate and alternate interior angles theorem.\n\nWe complete the proof as follows:\n\n- **Step 1**: We start by constructing a line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{HJ}$ through the point $H$ and parallel to $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{FG}$.![](images/ca/ca53ee7d8d2f6390e9aa8692bc0f1400aa78f84cf5afa0457b13f86f0921199e.png)\n- **Step 2**: Since $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{FG} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{HJ}$, we have that the corresponding angles $\\angle{IHJ}$ and $\\angle{F}$ are congruent by the *corresponding angles postulate*.Let's add this to our diagram.![](images/18/185672f4600e86a4e6e92e49d46cf966a46206128b4113ec4e070ff987cb5a7b.png)\n- **Step 3**: Since $\\angle{IHJ} \\cong \\angle{F}$ are congruent, we have\n$m\\angle{IHJ} = m\\angle{F}$\nby the *definition of congruent angles*.\n- **Step 4**: Since $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{FG}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{HJ}$ are parallel, we have that the alternate interior angles $\\angle{JHG}$ and $\\angle{G}$ are congruent by the *alternate interior angles theorem*.Let's add this to our diagram.![](images/f4/f441d13eb67dbbd6d301d479dd3ac0d6dd0ac79fd6fbc56c8fc90521f5c7cd24.png)\n- **Step 5**: Since $\\angle{JHG}$ and $\\angle{G}$ are congruent, we have\n$m\\angle{JHG} = m\\angle{G}$\nby the *definition of congruent angles*.\n- **Step 6**: In steps 3 and 5, we showed that $m\\angle{IHJ}=m\\angle{F}$ and $m\\angle{JHG}=m\\angle{G}$, respectively. So, by the *addition principle*, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle m∠IHJ|= m∠F; m∠IHJ + m∠JHG|= m∠F + m∠JHG; m∠IHJ + m∠JHG|= m∠F + m∠G.\n$$\n- **Step 7**: Notice that $\\overline{HJ}$ is the common side of the angles $\\angle{IHJ}$ and $\\angle{JHG}$. Therefore, by the *angle addition postulate*, we have that\n$m\\angle{IHG} = m\\angle{IHJ} + m\\angle{JHG}$.\n- **Step 8**: In steps 7 and 6, respectively, we showed that\n${\\color{blue}m\\angle{IHG}} = m\\angle{IHJ} + m\\angle{JHG}$,\nand\n$m\\angle{IHJ} + m\\angle{JHG} = {\\color{blue}m\\angle{F} + m\\angle{G}}$.\nTherefore, by the *transitive property of equality*, we get\n${\\color{blue}m\\angle{IHG}} = {\\color{blue}m\\angle{F} + m\\angle{G}}$,\nas required.\n\nAgain, it's **highly recommended** that you practice reconstructing this proof." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186075008 1064 "We can partition any segment in a given ratio using techniques like those discussed for horizontal and vertical segments.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to find the point $C$ on the segment from the point $A(-1,0)$ to the point $B(7,4)$ that divides the segment into the ratio $1:1$.\n\n![](images/8e/8e67f8d03c2238fa342b98a58fd2527419f6f52ea98543eb85247d4c3706352a.png)\n\nPartitioning a line segment $\\overline{AB}$ into a ratio $a:b$ means we must\n\n- divide the segment into $a+b$ equal parts, and\n- find the point $C$ that is $a$ equal parts from $A$.\n\nIn our case, the ratio $1:1$ implies\n\n$$\nAC:CB = 1:1\n$$\n\nThis means\n\n- there are $1+1 = 2$ equal parts, and\n- we want to find the point $C$ that is $1$ equal part from $A$. So, we have\n$AC= \\dfrac{1}{2}AB$.\n\nNext, consider another point $X$ that is horizontal with $A$ and directly above $C$, as shown in the diagram.\n\n![](images/1e/1e2d40e0648ba95a734dd9166852907759c8f50af06653d7b1a34785e1944d6a.png)\n\nTo go from point $A$ to point $C$, we can \"run\" horizontally from $A$ to $X$ and then \"rise\" vertically from $X$ to $C$.\n\nSo, we compute the coordinates of $C$ as follows:\n\n- The $x$-coordinate of $C$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{C} & ={x}_{A}+{\\color{red}{\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{B}-{x}_{A})}} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2}(7-(-1)) \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{8}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The $y$-coordinate of $C$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{C} & ={y}_{A}+{\\color{blue}{\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{B}-{y}_{A})}} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2}(4-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $C$ has coordinates\n\n$$\n\\left(3, 2 \\right)\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186075008 1064 "We can partition any segment in a given ratio using techniques like those discussed for horizontal and vertical segments.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to find the point $C$ on the segment from the point $A(-1,0)$ to 