 \\frac{x - 1}{\\sqrt{x - 1}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sqrt{x - 1}}{\\sqrt{x - 1}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  {1}^{+}} {\\displaystyle \\frac{( x - 1 ) \\sqrt{x - 1}}{x - 1}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  {1}^{+}} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{( x - 1 )} \\sqrt{x - 1}}{\\cancel{x - 1}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  {1}^{+}} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sqrt{x - 1}}{1}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  {1}^{+}} \\sqrt{x - 1} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\sqrt{1 - 1} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\sqrt{0} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0\\end{array}}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186078344 1074 "First, let's attempt to evaluate this limit by direct substitution. Substituting $x=1$ into the limit, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\frac{|x-1|}{\\sqrt{x-1}} & =\\frac{|1-1|}{\\sqrt{1-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|0|}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nNotice, however, that we only require the right-sided limit, i.e., $x\\to1^{+}$. If $x > 1$, then\n\n$$\n\\mid x-1 \\mid = x-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write this limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  1^{+}}\\frac{\\mid x - 1 \\mid}{\\sqrt{x - 1}} &= \\lim_{x \\to  1^{+}}\\frac{x - 1}{\\sqrt{x - 1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDirect substitution into the above still gives an indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 0 0\n$$\n\nHowever, we can rationalize the expression and then evaluate the limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle lim_{x \\to  {1}^{+}} {\\displaystyle \\frac{x - 1}{\\sqrt{x - 1}}} & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  {1}^{+}} {\\displaystyle \\frac{x - 1}{\\sqrt{x - 1}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sqrt{x - 1}}{\\sqrt{x - 1}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  {1}^{+}} {\\displaystyle \\frac{( x - 1 ) \\sqrt{x - 1}}{x - 1}} \\\\ \\displa