}\n$= 5$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186083609 1071 "Consider the following system of equations.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 x + 4 y = 2 \\\\ x + 3 y = - 1\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe variable $x$ can be eliminated from the system using the following two steps:\n\n1. Multiply the second equation by $-3$ and obtain\n$\\begin{aligned}-3x-9y & =3.\\end{aligned}$.\n2. Add the multiplied equation to the first equation to get\n\n{{field-1}}\n$= 5$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186083779 1071 "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\displaystyle 2 v - 10 w = 4 \\\\ \\displaystyle - v + 6 w = 7\\end{array} \\right. \\end{array}\n$$\n\nThe variable $v$ can be eliminated from the system using the following two steps:\n\n1. Multiply the second equation by $2$ and obtain\n$-2v + 12w|= 14$.\n2. Add the multiplied equation to the first equation to get\n$⋯?⋯ = 18$.\n\nWhat's the missing expression?" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186083779 1071 "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\displaystyle 2 v - 10 w = 4 \\\\ \\displaystyle - v + 6 w = 7\\end{array} \\right. \\end{array}\n$$\n\nThe variable $v$ can be eliminated from the system using the following two steps:\n\n1. Multiply the second equation by $2$ and obtain\n$\\begin{aligned}-2v+12w & =14.\\end{aligned}$.\n2. Add the multiplied equation to the first equation to get\n$⋯?⋯ = 18$.\n\nWhat's the missing expression?" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186083780 1071 "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}12 v - 32 w = - 8 \\\\ - 2 v + 6 w = 2\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe variable $v$ can be eliminated from the system using the following two steps:\n\n1. Multiply the second equation by $6$ and obtain\n$-12v + 36w|= 12$.\n2. Add the multiplied equation to the first equation to get\n$⋯?⋯ = 4$.\n\nFind the missing expression, and hence find the solution $(v, w)$ of the system." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186083780 1071 "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}12 v - 32 w = - 8 \\\\ - 2 v + 6 w = 2\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe variable $v$ can be eliminated from the system using the following two steps:\n\n1. Multiply the second equation by $6$ and obtain\n$\\begin{aligned}-12v+36w & =12.\\end{aligned}$.\n2. Add the multiplied equation to the first equation to get\n$⋯?⋯ = 4$.\n\nFind the missing expression, and hence find the solution $(v, w)$ of the system." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186084504 1064 "In this lesson, we will learn how to identify a *possible* equation of a rational function from its graph.\n\nConsider the rational function $y=f(x)$ below. What can we figure out about $f(x)$ based on its graph?\n\n![](images/f8/f89d70391ba43423e3600ab8a5eff6421ee4828da82e36239334116c5af76fc9.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we deduce the following:\n\n- The function has a vertical asymptote at $x = -1$. Therefore, the function's denominator must be zero at $x = -1$, but the numerator should *not* be zero at this point. So we're expecting a factor like $(x+1)$ in the denominator:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{?}{x+1}\n$$\n\n- The function has an $x$-intercept at $x = -2$. Therefore, the function's numerator must be zero at $x = -2$, but the denominator should *not* be zero at this point. So we're expecting a factor like $(x+2)$ in the numerator. This suggests that the function $f(x)$ could be given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{x+2}{x+1}\n$$\n\nAs it turns out, this function does indeed give the above graph. However, to be sure, let's make a few additional checks:\n\n- The $y$-intercept shown on the graph is $(0,2)$, and so we must have $f(0) = 2$. This is indeed true of $f(x)$, since\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{0+2}{0+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The horizontal asymptote shown on the graph is $y=1$. This means that $y\\to 1$ as $\\mid x \\mid \\to\\infty$. This is indeed true of $f(x)$, since for large $\\mid x \\mid$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+2}{x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{x}{x}+\\frac{2}{x})}{(\\frac{x}{x}+\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1+\\frac{2}{x})}{(1+\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & \\approx \\frac{1+0}{1+0} \\\\  & =1.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{x+2}{x+1}\n$$\n\ncould be the function shown in the graph." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186084504 1064 "In this lesson, we will learn how to identify a *possible* equation of a rational function from its graph.\n\nConsider the rational function $y=f(x)$ below. What can we figure out about $f(x)$ based on its graph?\n\n![](images/f8/f89d70391ba43423e3600ab8a5eff6421ee4828da82e36239334116c5af76fc9.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we deduce the following:\n\n- The function has a vertical asymptote at $x = -1$. Therefore, the function's denominator must be zero at $x = -1$, but the numerator should *not* be zero at this point. So we're expecting a factor like $(x+1)$ in the denominator:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{?}{x+1}\n$$\n\n- The function has an $x$-intercept at $x = -2$. Therefore, the function's numerator must be zero at $x = -2$, but the denominator should *not* be zero at this point. So we're expecting a factor like $(x+2)$ in the numerator. This suggests that the function $f(x)$ could be given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{x+2}{x+1}\n$$\n\nAs it turns out, this function does indeed give the above graph. However, to be sure, let's make a few additional checks:\n\n- The $y$-intercept shown on the graph is $(0,2)$, and so we must have $f(0) = 2$. This is indeed true of $f(x)$, since\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(0)|= \\frac{0 + 2}{0 + 1}; = \\frac{2}{1}; = 2.✓\n$$\n- The horizontal asymptote shown on the graph is $y=1$. This means that $y\\to 1$ as $\\mid x \\mid \\to\\infty$. This is indeed true of $f(x)$, since for large $\\mid x \\mid$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(x)|= \\frac{x + 2}{x + 1}; = ((\\frac{x}{x} + \\frac{2}{x}))/((\\frac{x}{x} + \\frac{1}{x})); = ((1 + \\frac{2}{x}))/((1 + \\frac{1}{x})); \\approx \\frac{1 + 0}{1 + 0}; = 1.✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{x+2}{x+1}\n$$\n\ncould be the function shown in the graph." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186087559 1074 "To find the coordinates of the $x$-intercepts, we set $y=0$ and solve for $t$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\sqrt{(t - 2)(3t - 1)} \\\\\n0 &= \\sqrt{(t - 2)(3t - 1)} \\\\\n0 &= (t - 2)(3t - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side equals zero if one of the factors is zero.\n\n- For the factor $(t-2)$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-2) & =0 \\\\ t & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the factor $(3t-1)$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3t-1) & =0 \\\\ 3t & =1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our solutions for $t$ into the equation\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac 1t\n$$\n\n- Substituting $t=2$ gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Substituting $t=\\dfrac{1}{3}$ gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2},0\\right)\n$$\n\nand $(3,0)$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186087559 1074 "To find the coordinates of the $x$-intercepts, we set $y=0$ and solve for $t$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\sqrt{(t - 2)(3t - 1)} \\\\\n0 &= \\sqrt{(t - 2)(3t - 1)} \\\\\n0 &= (t - 2)(3t - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side equals zero if one of the factors is zero.\n\n- For the factor $(t-2)$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle (t - 2)|= 0; t|= 2.\n$$\n- For the factor $(3t-1)$, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle (3t - 1)|= 0; 3t|= 1; t|= \\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our solutions for $t$ into the equation\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac 1t\n$$\n\n- Substituting $t=2$ gives\n$$\n\\displaystyle x|= \\frac{1}{t}; = \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n- Substituting $t=\\dfrac{1}{3}$ gives\n$$\n\\displaystyle x|= \\frac{1}{t}; = (1)/(\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)); = 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2},0\\right)\n$$\n\nand $(3,0)$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186087619 1074 "We analyze each series in turn.\n\n- First, we look at $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^2\\sqrt n}.\n$$ Rewriting this series gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}\\sqrt{n}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}\\cdot {n}^{1/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}\\frac{1}{{n}^{5/2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\dfrac 5 2$. Since $p > 1$, the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n- For the second series, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{5}{n^2} = 5\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{{n^2}},\n$$\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=2$. Since $p>1$, the series is convergent by the $p$-series test.\n- For the third series, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}\\frac{{n}^{1/2}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}\\frac{1}{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=1$ (the harmonic series). Since $p \\leq 1$, the series diverges by the $p$-series test.\n\nTherefore, only series I and II are convergent." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186087619 1074 "We analyze each series in turn.\n\n- First, we look at $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^2\\sqrt n}.\n$$ Rewriting this series gives\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{n^{2}\\sqrt{n}}|= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{n^{2} \\cdot n^{1/2}}; = ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{n^{5/2}},\n$$\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\dfrac 5 2$. Since $p > 1$, the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n- For the second series, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{5}{n^2} = 5\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{{n^2}},\n$$\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=2$. Since $p>1$, the series is convergent by the $p$-series test.\n- For the third series, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt{n^{3}}}|= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{n^{1/2}}{n^{3/2}}; = ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{n},\n$$\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=1$ (the harmonic series). Since $p \\leq 1$, the series diverges by the $p$-series test.\n\nTherefore, only series I and II are convergent." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186087646 1074 "To find the greatest common factor of two numbers, we first find the prime factorization of both numbers.\n\n- Since $3$ is a prime number, we immediately obtain the prime decomposition:\n\n$$\n3^3 = 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3\n$$\n\n- Since $21= 3\\cdot 7$, the prime decomposition $21^2$ can be obtained as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{21}^{2} & =21\\cdot 21 \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 7)\\cdot (3\\cdot 7) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3\\cdot 7\\cdot 7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the decompositions side-by-side, we pair off the factors as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3^{3} &= 3_(―) \\cdot 3_(―) \\cdot 3 \\\\\n21^{2} &= 3_(―) \\cdot 3_(―) \\cdot 7 \\cdot 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{GCF}(3^{3}, 21^{2}) = 3^{2}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186087646 1074 "To find the greatest common factor of two numbers, we first find the prime factorization of both numbers.\n\n- Since $3$ is a prime number, we immediately obtain the prime decomposition:\n\n$$\n3^3 = 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3\n$$\n\n- Since $21= 3\\cdot 7$, the prime decomposition $21^2$ can be obtained as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 21^{2}|= 21 \\cdot 21; = (3 \\cdot 7) \\cdot (3 \\cdot 7); = 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 7.\n$$\n\nComparing the decompositions side-by-side, we pair off the factors as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3^{3} &= 3_(―) \\cdot 3_(―) \\cdot 3 \\\\\n21^{2} &= 3_(―) \\cdot 3_(―) \\cdot 7 \\cdot 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{GCF}(3^{3}, 21^{2}) = 3^{2}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186088922 1064 "Consider the ellipse\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{12} + \\dfrac{y^2}{24} = 1\n$$\n\nand line $y = 2x$, sketched below. We can see from the diagram that the line and the ellipse intersect at $(2,4)$ and $(-2,-4)$.\n\n![](images/b0/b0a3676e298879fb74c0488ded0ad6fd469592eeba21f4d96dc8e5555886b641.png)\n\nBut how can we determine this mathematically, without relying on the graph?\n\nTo find the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we can substitute $y = 2x$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{12} + \\frac{y^{2}}{24} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{12} + ((2x)^{2})/(24) &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{12} + \\frac{4x^{2}}{24} &= 1 \\\\\n2x^{2} + 4x^{2} &= 24 \\\\\n6x^{2} &= 24 \\\\\nx^{2} &= 4 \\\\\nx &= ± 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, to find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we can substitute $x = \\pm 2$ into the equation of the line and compute the $y$-values.\n\n- Substituting $x = -2$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x \\\\ y & =2\\cdot (-2) \\\\ y & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis tells us that one intersection point is $(-2, -4)$.\n- Substituting $x = 2$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x \\\\ y & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\ y & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis tells us that the other intersection point is $(2, 4)$.\n\nSo we find that the two intersection points are $(-2,-4)$ and $(2,4)$, which matches up perfectly with what we see in the graph." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186088922 1064 "Consider the ellipse\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{12} + \\dfrac{y^2}{24} = 1\n$$\n\nand line $y = 2x$, sketched below. We can see from the diagram that the line and the ellipse intersect at $(2,4)$ and $(-2,-4)$.\n\n![](images/b0/b0a3676e298879fb74c0488ded0ad6fd469592eeba21f4d96dc8e5555886b641.png)\n\nBut how can we determine this mathematically, without relying on the graph?\n\nTo find the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we can substitute $y = 2x$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{12} + \\frac{y^{2}}{24} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{12} + ((2x)^{2})/(24) &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{12} + \\frac{4x^{2}}{24} &= 1 \\\\\n2x^{2} + 4x^{2} &= 24 \\\\\n6x^{2} &= 24 \\\\\nx^{2} &= 4 \\\\\nx &= ± 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, to find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we can substitute $x = \\pm 2$ into the equation of the line and compute the $y$-values.\n\n- Substituting $x = -2$, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle y|= 2x; y|= 2 \\cdot (-2); y|=-4.\n$$\nThis tells us that one intersection point is $(-2, -4)$.\n- Substituting $x = 2$, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle y|= 2x; y|= 2 \\cdot 2; y|= 4.\n$$\nThis tells us that the other intersection point is $(2, 4)$.\n\nSo we find that the two intersection points are $(-2,-4)$ and $(2,4)$, which matches up perfectly with what we see in the graph." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186092147 1074 "We can find the volume of a pyramid using the formula\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac{1}{3}Ah\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is the area of the base and $h$ is the altitude of the pyramid.\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- We are told that $\\overline{SH}$ is the altitude of the pyramid. So, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle h=SH=8\\,\\textrm{in}.\n$$\n- The line segment $\\overline{CL}$ is the height of $\\triangle{BCD}$ corresponding to the side $\\overline{BD}$. So, we compute the area of $\\triangle{BCD}$ as\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{BD\\cdot CL}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\text{in}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting\n\n$$\nA= \\dfrac{63}{2}\\,\\textrm{in}^2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nh=8\\,\\textrm{in}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we get\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\left(\\dfrac{63}{2}\\right) (8) = 84 \\: \\text{in}^3\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186092147 1074 "We can find the volume of a pyramid using the formula\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac{1}{3}Ah\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is the area of the base and $h$ is the altitude of the pyramid.\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- We are told that $\\overline{SH}$ is the altitude of the pyramid. So, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle h=SH=8\\,\\textrm{in}.\n$$\n- The line segment $\\overline{CL}$ is the height of $\\triangle{BCD}$ corresponding to the side $\\overline{BD}$. So, we compute the area of $\\triangle{BCD}$ as\n$$\n\\displaystyle A|= \\frac{BD \\cdot CL}{2}; = \\frac{9 \\cdot 7}{2}; = \\frac{63}{2}in^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting\n\n$$\nA= \\dfrac{63}{2}\\,\\textrm{in}^2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nh=8\\,\\textrm{in}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we get\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\left(\\dfrac{63}{2}\\right) (8) = 84 \\: \\text{in}^3\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186096405 1074 "We want to find the best model for the scatterplot data shown, in the quadratic form\n\n$$\ny = ax^2+bx+c\n$$\n\nwhere $a$, $b$, and $c$ are constants, with\n\n$$\na \\neq 0\n$$\n\n- The data in the scatterplot appear to form a downward-opening parabola. Therefore, the leading coefficient $a$ must be negative.\n- The points look symmetric about the line $x=4$. Using the axis of symmetry formula $x=-\\dfrac{b}{2a}$ and solving for $b$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{b}{2a} \\\\ 4 & =-\\frac{b}{2a} \\\\ b & =-8a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $a$ is negative, the coefficient of the linear term should be positive.\n- The $y$-intercept of the quadratic model is near $(0,650)$.\n\nTherefore, of the given choices, only choice\n\n$$\ny = -2.28x^{2} + 20.2x+652.76\n$$\n\nis the best quadratic model for the data in the scatterplot." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186096405 1074 "We want to find the best model for the scatterplot data shown, in the quadratic form\n\n$$\ny = ax^2+bx+c\n$$\n\nwhere $a$, $b$, and $c$ are constants, with\n\n$$\na \\neq 0\n$$\n\n- The data in the scatterplot appear to form a downward-opening parabola. Therefore, the leading coefficient $a$ must be negative.\n- The points look symmetric about the line $x=4$. Using the axis of symmetry formula $x=-\\dfrac{b}{2a}$ and solving for $b$, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle x|=-\\frac{b}{2a}; 4|=-\\frac{b}{2a}; b|=-8a.\n$$\nSince $a$ is negative, the coefficient of the linear term should be positive.\n- The $y$-intercept of the quadratic model is near $(0,650)$.\n\nTherefore, of the given choices, only choice\n\n$$\ny = -2.28x^{2} + 20.2x+652.76\n$$\n\nis the best quadratic model for the data in the scatterplot." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186096539 1074 "Let's inspect each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since there were $25$ total students, and only $5$ of them passed both tests, we have that\n$$\n\\displaystyle P(A)=\\dfrac{5}{25}=0.2.\n$$\n- Statement II is true. The complement $A'$ is the opposite of the event $A$, \"the student passed both tests.\" So $A'$ is the event \"the student failed at least one test.\"\n- Statement III is true. Since we know that $P(A) = 0.2$, we can calculate $P\\left(A'\\right)$ using the formula\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A)+P({A}^{'}) & =1 \\\\ P({A}^{'}) & =1-P(A) \\\\  & =1-0.2 \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186096539 1074 "Let's inspect each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since there were $25$ total students, and only $5$ of them passed both tests, we have that\n$$\n\\displaystyle P(A)=\\dfrac{5}{25}=0.2.\n$$\n- Statement II is true. The complement $A'$ is the opposite of the event $A$, \"the student passed both tests.\" So $A'$ is the event \"the student failed at least one test.\"\n- Statement III is true. Since we know that $P(A) = 0.2$, we can calculate $P\\left(A'\\right)$ using the formula\n$$\n\\displaystyle P(A) + P(A^{′})|= 1; P(A^{′})|= 1 - P(A); = 1 - 0.2; = 0.8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186096557 1074 "Suppose the sequence $a_n$ is defined for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nTo determine whether $a_n$ is increasing or decreasing, we can use the following test:\n\n- If a sequence $a_n$ is *increasing,* then for all $n\\geq 1$, we have\n$a_{n+1} \\geq a_{n} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad a_{n+1} - a_n \\geq 0$.\n- If a sequence $a_n$ is *decreasing,* then for all $n\\geq 1$, we have\n$a_{n+1} \\leq a_{n} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad a_{n+1} - a_n \\leq 0$.\n- If neither of these conditions holds, the sequence is *neither increasing nor decreasing*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the difference $a_{n+1} - a_n$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n} & =3-2(n+1)-(n+1)^{2}-(3-2n-{n}^{2}) \\\\  & =3-2n-2-({n}^{2}+2n+1)-3+2n+{n}^{2} \\\\  & =3-\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{2n}-2-\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{n}^{2}}-2n-1-3+\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{2n}+\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{n}^{2}} \\\\  & =-3-2n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhich is *negative* for all $n\\geq 1$. To see this concretely, set $b_n = -3-2n$. Computing the first few terms of $b_n$ we get\n$-5, \\quad -7, \\quad -9, \\quad -11, \\quad \\ldots\\,$.\n\n- Statement II is false, whereas statement III is true. Since $a_{n+1} - a_n \\leq 0$ for all $n\\geq 1$, we can conclude that $a_n$ is a decreasing sequence.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186096557 1074 "Suppose the sequence $a_n$ is defined for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nTo determine whether $a_n$ is increasing or decreasing, we can use the following test:\n\n- If a sequence $a_n$ is *increasing,* then for all $n\\geq 1$, we have\n$a_{n+1} \\geq a_{n} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad a_{n+1} - a_n \\geq 0$.\n- If a sequence $a_n$ is *decreasing,* then for all $n\\geq 1$, we have\n$a_{n+1} \\leq a_{n} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad a_{n+1} - a_n \\leq 0$.\n- If neither of these conditions holds, the sequence is *neither increasing nor decreasing*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the difference $a_{n+1} - a_n$, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle a_{n + 1} - a_{n}|= 3 - 2(n + 1) - (n + 1)^{2} - (3 - 2n - n^{2}); = 3 - 2n - 2 - (n^{2} + 2n + 1) - 3 + 2n + n^{2}; = 3 - 2n - 2 - n^{2} - 2n - 1 - 3 + 2n + n^{2}; =-3 - 2n\n$$\nwhich is *negative* for all $n\\geq 1$. To see this concretely, set $b_n = -3-2n$. Computing the first few terms of $b_n$ we get\n$-5, \\quad -7, \\quad -9, \\quad -11, \\quad \\ldots\\,$.\n\n- Statement II is false, whereas statement III is true. Since $a_{n+1} - a_n \\leq 0$ for all $n\\geq 1$, we can conclude that $a_n$ is a decreasing sequence.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186096600 1074 "The total surface area $(S)$ of a pyramid equals the sum of the lateral surface area $(S_L)$ and the base area $(S_B)$ of the pyramid:\n\n$$\nS = S_L + S_B\n$$\n\nWith this in mind, let's find $S_L$ and $S_B$ in turn.\n\n- We can find the lateral surface area of a regular pyramid using the formula\n$S_L = \\dfrac{1}{2} p l$,\nwhere $p$ is the base's perimeter and $l$ is the pyramid's slant height.Given the side $s = 7 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ of the regular (equilateral) triangular base, its perimeter is equal to\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =3s=3(7)=21\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{yd}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nNow, substituting $p = 21 \\, \\textrm{yd}$ and $l = 12 \\, \\textrm{yd}$ into the formula, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_L = \\dfrac{1}{2}(21)(12) = 126 \\, \\textrm{yd}^2.\n$$\n- The base of the triangular pyramid is an equilateral triangle. In our cases, it's a triangle with side length $s = 7 \\, \\textrm{yd}$. So, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_B = \\dfrac{s^2\\sqrt{3}}{4} = \\dfrac{7^2\\sqrt{3}}{4} = \\dfrac{49\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\, \\textrm{yd}^2.\n$$\n\nFinally, the total surface area of the pyramid is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= S_{L} + S_{B} \\\\\n&= (126 + \\frac{49\\sqrt{3}}{4})yd^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186096600 1074 "The total surface area $(S)$ of a pyramid equals the sum of the lateral surface area $(S_L)$ and the base area $(S_B)$ of the pyramid:\n\n$$\nS = S_L + S_B\n$$\n\nWith this in mind, let's find $S_L$ and $S_B$ in turn.\n\n- We can find the lateral surface area of a regular pyramid using the formula\n$S_L = \\dfrac{1}{2} p l$,\nwhere $p$ is the base's perimeter and $l$ is the pyramid's slant height.Given the side $s = 7 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ of the regular (equilateral) triangular base, its perimeter is equal to\n$$\n\\displaystyle p|= 3s = 3(7) = 21yd.\n$$\nNow, substituting $p = 21 \\, \\textrm{yd}$ and $l = 12 \\, \\textrm{yd}$ into the formula, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_L = \\dfrac{1}{2}(21)(12) = 126 \\, \\textrm{yd}^2.\n$$\n- The base of the triangular pyramid is an equilateral triangle. In our cases, it's a triangle with side length $s = 7 \\, \\textrm{yd}$. So, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_B = \\dfrac{s^2\\sqrt{3}}{4} = \\dfrac{7^2\\sqrt{3}}{4} = \\dfrac{49\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\, \\textrm{yd}^2.\n$$\n\nFinally, the total surface area of the pyramid is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= S_{L} + S_{B} \\\\\n&= (126 + \\frac{49\\sqrt{3}}{4})yd^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186097856 1064 "A line may intersect a parabola at one or two points. It is also possible that there is no point of intersection.\n\nIn the figure below, we see that the parabola\n\n$$\n(x-1)^2 =y-1\n$$\n\nintersects the line $y = 2x - 1$ at two places, $A$ and $B$. Let's compute the coordinates of these points.\n\n![](images/23/2312bbdd5cecf1c3523acfb5a6f8fc83eaf73334966b4f946d46b22facfb3162.png)\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we substitute 