aystyle \\frac{( r + n - 1 ) !}{r ! ( n - 1 ) !}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\nSo, the number of ways to choose $r=8$ chocolates from $n=4$ categories is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(8+4-1)!}{8!(4-1)!} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{11}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11!}{8!\\cdot (11-8)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{11!}{8!\\cdot 3!} \\\\  & =165.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The number of ways of choosing $4$ nougats from $5$ possible options without repetition is given by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4}) & =\\frac{5!}{4!\\cdot (5-4)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5!}{4!\\cdot 1!} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the multiplication principle, we conclude that we can obtain\n\n$$\n165\\cdot 5 =825\n$$\n\ncombinations in total." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186105670 1074 "We will first compute the number of ways of selecting $8$ chocolates. Then, we will compute the number of ways of selecting $4$ nougats. Finally, we will apply the multiplication principle to compute the total number of combinations that can be obtained.\n\n- The problem of selecting $8$ chocolates is analogous to placing $r$ identical objects into $n$ categories. We are choosing $r=8$ chocolates from $n=4$ categories. The number of ways to place $r$ identical objects into $n$ categories is given by the formula\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{r + n - 1}{r} \\right) = {\\displaystyle \\frac{( r + n - 1 ) !}{r ! ( n - 1 ) !}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\nSo, the number of ways to choose $r=8$ chocolates from $n=4$ categories is\n$$\n\\displaystyle ((8 + 4 - 1)!)/(8!(4 - 1)!)|= \\left(\\frac{11}{8}\\right); = (11!)/(8! \\cdot (11 - 8)!); = \\frac{11!}{8! \\cdot 3!}; = 165.\n$$\n- The number of ways of choosing $4$ nougats from $5$ possible options without repetition is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)|= (5!)/(4! \\cdot (5 - 4)!); = \\frac{5!}{4! \\cdot 1!}; = 5.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the multiplication principle, we conclude that we can obtain\n\n$$\n165\\cdot 5 =825\n$$\n\ncombinations in total." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186105682 1074 "If the diagonals of a quadrilateral bisect each other, then the quadrilateral is a parallelogram. Thus, $ABCD$ is a parallelogram if $AO=CO$ and $BO=DO$.\n\n- Using the first equality, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AO & =CO \\\\ 4x+3 & =y+2 \\\\ y & =4x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Using the second equality, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BO & =DO \\\\ 5-x & =y-1 \\\\ x & =6-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\ny = 4x+1\n$$\n\nfrom the first equation into the above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 6 - y \\\\\nx &= 6 - (4x + 1) \\\\\nx &= 5 - 4x \\\\\n5x &= 5 \\\\\nx &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x$ back into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 4x + 1 \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 1 + 1 \\\\\n&= 4 + 1 \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ and $y=5$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186105682 1074 "If the diagonals of a quadrilateral bisect each other, then the quadrilateral is a parallelogram. Thus, $ABCD$ is a parallelogram if $AO=CO$ and $BO=DO$.\n\n- Using the first equality, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle AO|= CO; 4x + 3|= y + 2; y|= 4x + 1.\n$$\n- Using the second equality, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle BO|= DO; 5 - x|= y - 1; x|= 6 - y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\ny = 4x+1\n$$\n\nfrom the first equation into the above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 6 - y \\\\\nx &= 6 - (4x + 1) \\\\\nx &= 5 - 4x \\\\\n5x &= 5 \\\\\nx &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x$ back into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 4x + 1 \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 1 + 1 \\\\\n&= 4 + 1 \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ and $y=5$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186107002 1064 "![](images/a9/a9a087086d2653ff95b039c1a05ea0dcf310df72243cfee248c8544f179adf37.png)\n\nConsider the parallelogram $ABCD$ above, where\n\n$$\nm\\angle A = 70^\\circ\n$$\n\nLet's find the measures of the other angles.\n\n- First, we know that opposite angles in a parallelogram are congruent. Therefore, $m\\angle C = 70^\\circ$.![](images/3a/3a32408673bb4a42be19f48323476339ed7aaaf6c91243b29d8fc4fcb9f0818a.png)\n\n- Next, we know that consecutive angles in a parallelogram are supplementary. Therefore,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\\mathrm{∠}B & ={180}^{∘}-m\\mathrm{∠}A \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{70}^{∘} \\\\  & ={110}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nLet's add this to our diagram.![](images/f9/f92d9f2354b52d90074aaca802937468645749d09aa314f0fa915947ee3d4682.png)\n\n- Finally, we apply the fact that opposite angles are congruent to conclude that $m\\angle D = 110^\\circ$.![](images/d9/d91adf574a75ce2095129724e5eb241e0aebb6f0b3c8182c41972c36e5f876ef.png)" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186107002 1064 "![](images/a9/a9a087086d2653ff95b039c1a05ea0dcf310df72243cfee248c8544f179adf37.png)\n\nConsider the parallelogram $ABCD$ above, where\n\n$$\nm\\angle A = 70^\\circ\n$$\n\nLet's find the measures of the other angles.\n\n- First, we know that opposite angles in a parallelogram are congruent. Therefore, $m\\angle C = 70^\\circ$.![](images/3a/3a32408673bb4a42be19f48323476339ed7aaaf6c91243b29d8fc4fcb9f0818a.png)\n\n- Next, we know that consecutive angles in a parallelogram are supplementary. Therefore,\n$$\n\\displaystyle m∠B|= 180^{∘} - m∠A; = 180^{∘} - 70^{∘}; = 110^{∘}.\n$$\nLet's add this to our diagram.![](images/f9/f92d9f2354b52d90074aaca802937468645749d09aa314f0fa915947ee3d4682.png)\n\n- Finally, we apply the fact that opposite angles are congruent to conclude that $m\\angle D = 110^\\circ$.![](images/d9/d91adf574a75ce2095129724e5eb241e0aebb6f0b3c8182c41972c36e5f876ef.png)" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186107110 1064 "Here, we derive the three useful triangle formulas that we have used throughout the lesson.\n\n- For an equilateral triangle with side length $a$, the area $A$ is given by the formula\n$A = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{4}a^2$.\nTo see why this is true, consider the equilateral triangle below.![](images/85/85b54af73ee6951c83aa675e7e34c6403c91d3f8d11ca198c1a4f91510e8d976.png)\nFor this triangle, we see that we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{base} & =a, \\\\ \\text{height} & =a\\operatorname{sin}{60}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSubstituting this into the formula for the area of a triangle, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\dfrac 1 2 \\cdot a \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}a = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{4}a^2.\n$$\n\n![](images/20/20b7814be68e26f3e4b0426f6726d571a2dc0c112705dc6cad178bf0042d23a3.png)\n\n- For an isosceles right triangle with leg $b$ and hypotenuse $c$, the area $A$ of the triangle is\n$A = \\dfrac12 b^2$.\nThis is a straightforward application of the formula for the area of a triangle. Clearly, we have $\\textrm{base} = \\textrm{height} = b$, and therefore, the formula follows immediately.We also have the formula\n$A = \\dfrac14 c^2$.\nTo see why this is true, recall that, for an isosceles right triangle, the base $b$ and hypotenuse $c$ are related by the equation $c = \\sqrt 2 b$. Now, substituting for $b$ into the formula $A=\\dfrac12 b^2$, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\dfrac12\\left(\\dfrac{c}{\\sqrt 2}\\right)^2 = \\dfrac14 c^2.\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186107110 1064 "Here, we derive the three useful triangle formulas that we have used throughout the lesson.\n\n- For an equilateral triangle with side length $a$, the area $A$ is given by the formula\n$A = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{4}a^2$.\nTo see why this is true, consider the equilateral triangle below.![](images/85/85b54af73ee6951c83aa675e7e34c6403c91d3f8d11ca198c1a4f91510e8d976.png)\nFor this triangle, we see that we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle base|= a,; height|= a\\sin 60^{∘} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}a.\n$$\nSubstituting this into the formula for the area of a triangle, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\dfrac 1 2 \\cdot a \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}a = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{4}a^2.\n$$\n\n![](images/20/20b7814be68e26f3e4b0426f6726d571a2dc0c112705dc6cad178bf0042d23a3.png)\n\n- For an isosceles right triangle with leg $b$ and hypotenuse $c$, the area $A$ of the triangle is\n$A = \\dfrac12 b^2$.\nThis is a straightforward application of the formula for the area of a triangle. Clearly, we have $\\textrm{base} = \\textrm{height} = b$, and therefore, the formula follows immediately.We also have the formula\n$A = \\dfrac14 c^2$.\nTo see why this is true, recall that, for an isosceles right triangle, the base $b$ and hypotenuse $c$ are related by the equation $c = \\sqrt 2 b$. Now, substituting for $b$ into the formula $A=\\dfrac12 b^2$, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\dfrac12\\left(\\dfrac{c}{\\sqrt 2}\\right)^2 = \\dfrac14 c^2.\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186110424 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle DEF$ are congruent by the SAS criterion since we have the following pairs of congruent sides and included angles:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}BA^(―) & ≅ED^(―) \\\\ AC^(―) & ≅DF^(―) \\\\ ∠A & ≅∠D\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Statement II is false. Since the two triangles are congruent, the corresponding angles must be congruent too. Therefore, we obtain\n$\\angle{B} \\cong \\angle{E}$.\n- Statement III is true. Since the two triangles are congruent, the corresponding sides must be congruent too. Hence, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =EF \\\\ 2x & =x+2 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186110424 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle DEF$ are congruent by the SAS criterion since we have the following pairs of congruent sides and included angles:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}BA^(―) & ≅ED^(―) \\\\ AC^(―) & ≅DF^(―) \\\\ ∠A & ≅∠D\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Statement II is false. Since the two triangles are congruent, the corresponding angles must be congruent too. Therefore, we obtain\n$\\angle{B} \\cong \\angle{E}$.\n- Statement III is true. Since the two triangles are congruent, the corresponding sides must be congruent too. Hence, we obtain\n$$\n\\displaystyle BC|= EF; 2x|= x + 2; x|= 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186110428 1074 "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle DEF$ are congruent by HL (hypotenuse-leg) since we have the following congruent legs, hypotenuses, and right angles:$m\\angle{B}=m\\angle{E}=90^\\circ$$\\overline{AB}\\cong \\overline{ED}$$\\overline{AC}\\cong \\overline{DF}$\n- Statement II is true. Since the triangles are congruent, all the corresponding angles must be congruent. In particular,\n$\\angle C \\cong \\angle F$\n- Statement III is true. Since the triangles are congruent, all the corresponding sides must be congruent. In particular, $\\overline{BC} \\cong \\overline{EF}$. Thus,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =EF \\\\ 3y-8 & =2y-4 \\\\ y & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186110428 1074 "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle DEF$ are congruent by HL (hypotenuse-leg) since we have the following congruent legs, hypotenuses, and right angles:$m\\angle{B}=m\\angle{E}=90^\\circ$$\\overline{AB}\\cong \\overline{ED}$$\\overline{AC}\\cong \\overline{DF}$\n- Statement II is true. Since the triangles are congruent, all the corresponding angles must be congruent. In particular,\n$\\angle C \\cong \\angle F$\n- Statement III is true. Since the triangles are congruent, all the corresponding sides must be congruent. In particular, $\\overline{BC} \\cong \\overline{EF}$. Thus,\n$$\n\\displaystyle BC|= EF; 3y - 8|= 2y - 4; y|= 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186110544 1074 "For each row/column in a joint frequency table:\n\n- the sum of the joint frequencies equals the marginal frequency (or total) for that row/column, and\n- the sum of the marginal frequencies in the totals row/column equals the total $(85)$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each row/column:\n\n- Looking at the first row, there are $44$ boys in total.\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\"></td>\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">German</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Spanish</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">French</th>\n<td data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Totals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-fill=\"#FCF3CF\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Boys</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FCF3CF\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FCF3CF\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FCF3CF\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#FCF3CF\">$44$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-fill=\"#F2F4F4\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Girls</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$z$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Totals </th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$t$ </td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$w$ </td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$29$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$85$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8+x+14 & =44 \\\\ 22+x & =44 \\\\