aystyle \\text{Smallest} = 1 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\text{Greatest} = 15\n$$\nSo, the range of the dataset is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Range} & =\\text{Greatest}-\\text{Smallest} \\\\  & =15-1 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $9 < 14$, a comparison of the ranges suggests that the dataset corresponding to the second week is more spread out." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186147193 1074 "Recall that on a box plot,\n\n- the smallest value in the data corresponds to the left whisker, and\n- the greatest value corresponds to the right whisker.\n\nThe range of a dataset is the difference between the greatest and the smallest values.\n\nWith that in mind, let's find the ranges of the given datasets.\n\n- Consider the box plot corresponding to the first week.![](images/12/12a2d26dfb33ddaae846ca332f5a684dca70720af005543aedc7e501192890e8.png)\nHere, we have the following:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Smallest} = 2 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\text{Greatest} = 11\n$$\nSo, the range of the dataset is\n$$\n\\displaystyle Range|= Greatest - Smallest; = 11 - 2; = 9.\n$$\n- Consider the box plot corresponding to the second week.![](images/01/01d1ab0ff1e0aa7533e1dd0b7af5efaae56f0034b20c075e6d8c7e9367beb0c6.png)\nHere, we have the following:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Smallest} = 1 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\text{Greatest} = 15\n$$\nSo, the range of the dataset is\n$$\n\\displaystyle Range|= Greatest - Smallest; = 15 - 1; = 14.\n$$\n\nSince $9 < 14$, a comparison of the ranges suggests that the dataset corresponding to the second week is more spread out." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186147194 1074 "The interquartile range (IQR) of a dataset is the difference between the upper and the lower quartiles.\n\nWith that in mind, let's find the interquartile ranges of the given datasets.\n\n- Consider the dot plot corresponding to the first assistant.Notice that the number of data points ($15$) is odd. As a result, we can separate the data into its lower and upper parts, as illustrated in the diagram below.![](images/e0/e0966bd1467619b0235b410adf7bed82b6ea862cd128242b6f584dedf1c6e192.png)\nHere, we have the following:The lower quartile is the median of the lower part. In this case, it's just the middle number. So, the lower quartile is\n$\\text{Lower Quartile} = 21$.The upper quartile is the median of the upper part. In this case, it's just the middle number. So, the upper quartile is\n$\\text{Upper Quartile} = 23$.Thus, the interquartile range is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{IQR} & =\\text{Upper Quartile}-\\text{Lower Quartile} \\\\  & =23-21 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Consider the dot plot corresponding to the second assistant.Notice that the number of data points ($12$) is even. As a result, we can separate the data into its lower and upper parts, as illustrated in the diagram below.![](images/cf/cf9f14b1ec079ae949177dec31c679a10a8dc44c07dc4e6309d5d5065df1871f.png)\nHere, we have the following:The lower quartile is the median of the lower part. In this case, it's the average of the two middle numbers. So, the lower quartile is\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Lower Quartile} = \\dfrac{21+22}{2} = \\dfrac{43}{2} = 21.5.\n$$The upper quartile is the median of the upper part. In this case, it's the average of the two middle numbers. So, the upper quartile is\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Upper Quartile} = \\dfrac{23+23}{2} = \\dfrac{46}{2} = 23.\n$$Thus, the interquartile range is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{IQR} & =\\text{Upper Quartile}-\\text{Lower Quartile} \\\\  & =23-21.5 \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2 > 1.5$, a comparison of the interquartile ranges suggests that the dataset corresponding to the first assistant is more spread out." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186147194 1074 "The interquartile range (IQR) of a dataset is the difference between the upper and the lower quartiles.\n\nWith that in mind, let's find the interquartile ranges of the given datasets.\n\n- Consider the dot plot corresponding to the first assistant.Notice that the number of data points ($15$) is odd. As a result, we can separate the data into its lower and upper parts, as illustrated in the diagram below.![](images/e0/e0966bd1467619b0235b410adf7bed82b6ea862cd128242b6f584dedf1c6e192.png)\nHere, we have the following:The lower quartile is the median of the lower part. In this case, it's just the middle number. So, the lower quartile is\n$\\text{Lower Quartile} = 21$.The upper quartile is the median of the upper part. In this case, it's just the middle number. So, the upper quartile is\n$\\text{Upper Quartile} = 23$.Thus, the interquartile range is\n$$\n\\displaystyle IQR|= Upper Quartile - Lower Quartile; = 23 - 21; = 2.\n$$\n- Consider the dot plot corresponding to the second assistant.Notice that the number of data points ($12$) is even. As a result, we can separate the data into its lower and upper parts, as illustrated in the diagram below.![](images/cf/cf9f14b1ec079ae949177dec31c679a10a8dc44c07dc4e6309d5d5065df1871f.png)\nHere, we have the following:The lower quartile is the median of the lower part. In this case, it's the average of the two middle numbers. So, the lower quartile is\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Lower Quartile} = \\dfrac{21+22}{2} = \\dfrac{43}{2} = 21.5.\n$$The upper quartile is the median of the upper part. In this case, it's the average of the two middle numbers. So, the upper quartile is\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Upper Quartile} = \\dfrac{23+23}{2} = \\dfrac{46}{2} = 23.\n$$Thus, the interquartile range is\n$$\n\\displaystyle IQR|= Upper Quartile - Lower Quartile; = 23 - 21.5; = 1.5.\n$$\n\nSince $2 > 1.5$, a comparison of the interquartile ranges suggests that the dataset corresponding to the first assistant is more spread out." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186147792 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$8x^{2}y^{″} + 2x(4x + 1)y^{′} + y = 0$\nWhich of the following is a Frobenius series solution to the differential equation given above, where $a_{n}$ for $n = 0, 1, 2, …$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: Substituting a trial Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain*\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{2}{y}^{''} & =8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+8{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 2x(4x+1){y}^{'} & =2\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}[8(\\lambda +m-1){a}_{n-1}+2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ y & ={a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186147792 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$8x^{2}y^{″} + 2x(4x + 1)y^{′} + y = 0$\nWhich of the following is a Frobenius series solution to the differential equation given above, where $a_{n}$ for $n = 0, 1, 2, …$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: Substituting a trial Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain*\n$$\n\\displaystyle 8x^{2}y^{″}|= 8(λ^{2} - λ)a_{0}x^{λ} + 8∑_(n = 1)^(∞)((λ + n)^{2} - (λ + n))a_{n}x^{λ + n},; 2x(4x + 1)y^{′}|= 2λa_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)[8(λ + m - 1)a_{n - 1} + 2(λ + n)a_{n}]x^{λ + n},; y|= a_{0}x^{λ} + x^{λ}∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}x^{λ + n}.\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186151884 1074 "Let's recall the general terminology for sinusoidal curves:\n\n![](images/f6/f693f83ce4198b52d8b6dbca791cdf2855c7879dcf230193e824c23c10ede995.png)\n\nIf\n\n$$\nF(t) = A \\cos\\left(B t \\right) + C\n$$\n\nthen\n\n- $\\mid A \\mid$ is the *amplitude*,\n- $\\dfrac{\\mid B \\mid}{2\\pi}$ is the *frequency,* $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\mid B \\mid}$ is the *period*, and\n- $C$ is the *midline* (vertical shift).\n\nWe'll answer each part of the question in turn.\n\n- For the first part, we are interested in the time difference between the highest and lowest tides (i.e., *half* the period of the function).Now, we have\n$B = \\frac{π}{6}$.\nSo, the period is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\pi }{B} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =12\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{hr}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe time between the lowest and highest tides is *half* the period. Therefore, it takes $6$ hours for the tide to transition from its lowest to highest level.\n- For the second part, we're interested in the maximum value of the function. This is given by the amplitude plus the midline. Now, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mid A \\mid = 6\\,\\textrm{ft}, \\qquad C = 8\\,\\textrm{ft}.\n$$\nTherefore,\n$$\n\\displaystyle H_{\\textrm{max}} = 6\\,\\textrm{ft} + 8\\,\\textrm{ft} = 14\\,\\textrm{ft}.\n$$\nTherefore, the maximum height of the tide is $14\\,\\textrm{ft}$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186151884 1074 "Let's recall the general terminology for sinusoidal curves:\n\n![](images/f6/f693f83ce4198b52d8b6dbca791cdf2855c7879dcf230193e824c23c10ede995.png)\n\nIf\n\n$$\nF(t) = A \\cos\\left(B t \\right) + C\n$$\n\nthen\n\n- $\\mid A \\mid$ is the *amplitude*,\n- $\\dfrac{\\mid B \\mid}{2\\pi}$ is the *frequency,* $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\mid B \\mid}$ is the *period*, and\n- $C$ is the *midline* (vertical shift).\n\nWe'll answer each part of the question in turn.\n\n- For the first part, we are interested in the time difference between the highest and lowest tides (i.e., *half* the period of the function).Now, we have\n$B = \\frac{π}{6}$.\nSo, the period is\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2π}{B}|= (2π)/(\\left(\\frac{π}{6}\\right)); = 12hr.\n$$\nThe time between the lowest and highest tides is *half* the period. Therefore, it takes $6$ hours for the tide to transition from its lowest to highest level.\n- For the second part, we're interested in the maximum value of the function. This is given by the amplitude plus the midline. Now, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mid A \\mid = 6\\,\\textrm{ft}, \\qquad C = 8\\,\\textrm{ft}.\n$$\nTherefore,\n$$\n\\displaystyle H_{\\textrm{max}} = 6\\,\\textrm{ft} + 8\\,\\textrm{ft} = 14\\,\\textrm{ft}.\n$$\nTherefore, the maximum height of the tide is $14\\,\\textrm{ft}$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186151988 1074 "To decipher a message encrypted using the linear cipher with key $a=15$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the ciphertext in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}25$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}20$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Find the inverse to $a=15$ modulo $26$. To do that, we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{ccccc}26 & = & 15\\cdot 1 & + & 11 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 15 & = & 11\\cdot 1 & + & 4 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 11 & = & 4\\cdot 2 & + & 3 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 4 & = & 3\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11 & =26-15 \\\\ 4 & =15-11 \\\\ 3 & =11-4\\cdot 2 \\\\ 1 & =4-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThen, we back-substitute:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =4-3 \\\\  & =4-(11-4\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3-11 \\\\  & =(15-11)\\cdot 3-11 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 3-11\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 3-(26-15)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 7-26\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 7+26\\cdot (-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWe can write this result in modulo $26$, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}15 \\cdot 7 + 26 \\cdot (-4) & ≡1 & (mod26) \\\\ 15 \\cdot 7 & ≡1 & (mod26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nSo we have $15 \\cdot 7 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:26)$, which means that the inverse of $a=15$ modulo $26$ is $a^{-1}=7$.\n- Multiply each index by $a^{-1}=7$ modulo $n=26$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\cdot 25≡175≡19 & (mod26) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 0≡0≡0 & (mod26) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 9≡63≡11 & (mod26) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 20≡140≡10 & (mod26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indices:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the answer is $\\text{TALK}$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186151988 1074 "To decipher a message encrypted using the linear cipher with key $a=15$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the ciphertext in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}25$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}20$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Find the inverse to $a=15$ modulo $26$. To do that, we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{ccccc}26 & = & 15\\cdot 1 & + & 11 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 15 & = & 11\\cdot 1 & + & 4 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 11 & = & 4\\cdot 2 & + & 3 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 4 & = & 3\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle 11|= 26 - 15; 4|= 15 - 11; 3|= 11 - 4 \\cdot 2; 1|= 4 - 3\n$$\nThen, we back-substitute:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 1|= 4 - 3; = 4 - (11 - 4 \\cdot 2); = 4 \\cdot 3 - 11; = (15 - 11) \\cdot 3 - 11; = 15 \\cdot 3 - 11 \\cdot 4; = 15 \\cdot 3 - (26 - 15) \\cdot 4; = 15 \\cdot 7 - 26 \\cdot 4; = 15 \\cdot 7 + 26 \\cdot (-4)\n$$\nWe can write this result in modulo $26$, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}15 \\cdot 7 + 26 \\cdot (-4) & ≡1 & (mod26) \\\\ 15 \\cdot 7 & ≡1 & (mod26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nSo we have $15 \\cdot 7 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:26)$, which means that the inverse of $a=15$ modulo $26$ is $a^{-1}=7$.\n- Multiply each index by $a^{-1}=7$ modulo $n=26$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\cdot 25≡175≡19 & (mod26) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 0≡0≡0 & (mod26) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 9≡63≡11 & (mod26) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 20≡140≡10 & (mod26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indices:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the answer is $\\text{TALK}$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186152017 1074 "Euler's theorem states that if $a$ and $n$ are coprime, then\n\n$$\na^{\\phi(n)} \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:n)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\phi$ is Euler's totient function.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\text{gcd}(5,39)=1$.\n- Statement II is true. First, notice that\n$39 = 3 \\cdot 13$,\nwhere $3$ and $13$ are distinct prime numbers. As a result,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ(39) & =ϕ(3)\\cdot ϕ(13) \\\\  & =(3-1)\\cdot (13-1) \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Statement III is true. By Euler's theorem, we get the following:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}a^{ϕ(n)} & ≡1 & (modn) \\\\ 5^{ϕ(39)} & ≡1 & (mod39) \\\\ 5^{24} & ≡1 & (mod39)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186152017 1074 "Euler's theorem states that if $a$ and $n$ are coprime, then\n\n$$\na^{\\phi(n)} \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:n)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\phi$ is Euler's totient function.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\text{gcd}(5,39)=1$.\n- Statement II is true. First, notice that\n$39 = 3 \\cdot 13$,\nwhere $3$ and $13$ are distinct prime numbers. As a result,\n$$\n\\displaystyle ϕ(39)|= ϕ(3) \\cdot ϕ(13); = (3 - 1) \\cdot (13 - 1); = 24.\n$$\n- Statement III is true. By Euler's theorem, we get the following:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}a^{ϕ(n)} & ≡1 & (modn) \\\\ 5^{ϕ(39)} & ≡1 & (mod39) \\\\ 5^{24} & ≡1 & (mod39)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186152022 1074 "Given a natural number $n$, Euler's totient function $\\phi(n)$ returns the total number of positive integers that are less than $n$ and are coprime to $n$.\n\nRecall that Euler's totient function is a multiplicative function, meaning that if two positive integers $m$ and $n$ are coprime, then\n\n$$\n\\phi(mn) = \\phi(m)\\phi(n)\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n210 = 5\\cdot 6 \\cdot 7\n$$\n\n- Since $5$ and $7$ are distinct prime numbers, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ(5) & =5-1=4, \\\\ ϕ(7) & =7-1=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- To compute $\\phi(6)$, let's write down all positive integers that are less than $6$ and cancel out the numbers that are not coprime to $6$:\n$1, \\: \\bcancel{2}, \\: \\bcancel{3}, \\bcancel{4},\\: 5$\nSo, $\\phi(6) = 2$.\n\nFinally, since $\\phi$ is multiplicative and\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(5,6,7)=1\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nϕ(210) &= ϕ(5) \\cdot ϕ(6) \\cdot ϕ(7) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 6 \\\\\n&= 48\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186152022 1074 "Given a natural number $n$, Euler's totient function $\\phi(n)$ returns the total number of positive integers that are less than $n$ and are coprime to $n$.\n\nRecall that Euler's totient function is a multiplicative function, meaning that if two positive integers $m$ and $n$ are coprime, then\n\n$$\n\\phi(mn) = \\phi(m)\\phi(n)\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n210 = 5\\cdot 6 \\cdot 7\n$$\n\n- Since $5$ and $7$ are distinct prime numbers, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle ϕ(5)|= 5 - 1 = 4,; ϕ(7)|= 7 - 1 = 6.\n$$\n- To compute $\\phi(6)$, let's write down all positive integers that are less than $6$ and cancel out the numbers that are not coprime to $6$:\n$1, \\: \\bcancel{2}, \\: \\bcancel{3}, \\bcancel{4},\\: 5$\nSo, $\\phi(6) = 2$.\n\nFinally, since $\\phi$ is multiplicative and\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(5,6,7)=1\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nϕ(210) &= ϕ(5) \\cdot ϕ(6) \\cdot ϕ(7) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 6 \\\\\n&= 48\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186153388 1064 "Earlier, we encrypted a message using the linear cipher with key $a=3$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters. The resulting encrypted message was $\\textrm{VMU}$.\n\nTo decrypt this message, all we have to do is multiply by the inverse of the key:\n\n$$\na^{-1} \\cdot i_\\textrm{cipher} \\equiv i_\\textrm{plain} \\: (\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nLet's recall the indices of the English alphabet and then decipher the message.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">D</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">F</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">G</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">I</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">N</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">O</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">P</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Q</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">R</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">S</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">V</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">W</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">X</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$23$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo decipher a message encrypted using the linear cipher with key $a=3$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the ciphertext in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">V</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}20$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Find the inverse of $a=3$ modulo $26$. To do that, we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{ccccc}26 & = & 3\\cdot 8 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 3 & = & 2\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =26-3\\cdot 8 \\\\ 1 & =3-2\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThen, we back-substitute:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =3-2 \\\\  & =3-(26-3\\cdot 8) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 9-26 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 9+26\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWe can write this result in modulo $26$, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\cdot 9 + 26 \\cdot (-1) & ≡1 & (mod26) \\\\ 3 \\cdot 9 & ≡1 & (mod26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nSo we have $3 \\cdot 9 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:26)$, which means that the inverse of $a=3$ modulo $26$ is $a^{-1}=9$.\n- Multiply each index by $a^{-1}=9$ modulo $n=26$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}9 \\cdot 21≡189≡7 & (mod26) \\\\ 9 \\cdot 12≡108≡4 & (mod26) \\\\ 9 \\cdot 20≡180≡24 & (mod26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indices:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}24$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nWe successfully recovered the original message, which was $\\text{HEY}$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186153388 1064 "Earlier, we encrypted a message using the linear cipher with key $a=3$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters. The resulting encrypted message was $\\textrm{VMU}$.\n\nTo decrypt this message, all we have to do is multiply by the inverse of the key:\n\n$$\na^{-1} \\cdot i_\\textrm{cipher} \\equiv i_\\textrm{plain} \\: (\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nLet's recall the indices of the English alphabet and then decipher the message.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center