\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Q</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">R</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">S</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">V</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">W</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">X</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$23$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo decipher a message encrypted using the linear cipher with key $a=3$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the ciphertext in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">V</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}20$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Find the inverse of $a=3$ modulo $26$. To do that, we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{ccccc}26 & = & 3\\cdot 8 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 3 & = & 2\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle 2|= 26 - 3 \\cdot 8; 1|= 3 - 2 \\cdot 1\n$$\nThen, we back-substitute:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 1|= 3 - 2; = 3 - (26 - 3 \\cdot 8); = 3 \\cdot 9 - 26; = 3 \\cdot 9 + 26 \\cdot (-1)\n$$\nWe can write this result in modulo $26$, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\cdot 9 + 26 \\cdot (-1) & ≡1 & (mod26) \\\\ 3 \\cdot 9 & ≡1 & (mod26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nSo we have $3 \\cdot 9 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:26)$, which means that the inverse of $a=3$ modulo $26$ is $a^{-1}=9$.\n- Multiply each index by $a^{-1}=9$ modulo $n=26$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}9 \\cdot 21≡189≡7 & (mod26) \\\\ 9 \\cdot 12≡108≡4 & (mod26) \\\\ 9 \\cdot 20≡180≡24 & (mod26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indices:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}24$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nWe successfully recovered the original message, which was $\\text{HEY}$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186153475 1064 "Any decimal containing *repeating* digits is rational. This means that every decimal containing repeating digits can be written as the ratio of two integers (i.e., as a fraction).\n\nLet's see how to convert the decimal $0.3333\\ldots$ to a fraction. We proceed in four steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** We define our number as $x$:\n$x = 0.3333 \\ldots$\nNotice that the *repeating digit* is $3$.\n- **Step 2:** We multiply our number by $10$ to create a number with the same digits after the decimal point as $x$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\times x & =10\\times 0.3333… \\\\ 10x & =3.3333…\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, we now have the following two numbers:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x & =3.{\\color{blue}{3333}}… \\\\ x & =0.{\\color{blue}{3333}}…\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThese two numbers have the same repeating digit after the decimal point. In other words, they have the same \"tail.\" This is crucial for the next step.\n\n- **Step 3**: We subtract $x$ from $10x$:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}10x & =3.3333… \\\\ -\\hspace{2em}x & =0.3333… \\\\ \\hline 9x & =3\\end{array}\n$$\nNotice that the tails canceled out, leaving us with the equation $9x=3$ that contains only integers.\n\n- **Step 4**: Finally, we solve the equation for $x$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9x & =3 \\\\ \\frac{9x}{9} & =\\frac{3}{9} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n0.3333 \\ldots = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186153475 1064 "Any decimal containing *repeating* digits is rational. This means that every decimal containing repeating digits can be written as the ratio of two integers (i.e., as a fraction).\n\nLet's see how to convert the decimal $0.3333\\ldots$ to a fraction. We proceed in four steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** We define our number as $x$:\n$x = 0.3333 \\ldots$\nNotice that the *repeating digit* is $3$.\n- **Step 2:** We multiply our number by $10$ to create a number with the same digits after the decimal point as $x$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 10 \\cdot x|= 10 \\cdot 0.3333…; 10x|= 3.3333…\n$$\nSo, we now have the following two numbers:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 10x|= 3.3333…; x|= 0.3333…\n$$\nThese two numbers have the same repeating digit after the decimal point. In other words, they have the same \"tail.\" This is crucial for the next step.\n\n- **Step 3**: We subtract $x$ from $10x$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 10x|= 3.3333…; - x|= 0.3333…; 9x|= 3\n$$\nNotice that the tails canceled out, leaving us with the equation $9x=3$ that contains only integers.\n\n- **Step 4**: Finally, we solve the equation for $x$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 9x|= 3; \\frac{9x}{9}|= \\frac{3}{9}; x|= \\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n0.3333 \\ldots = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186153476 1064 "Consider the following decimal:\n\n$$\n0.67777\\ldots\n$$\n\nThis decimal contains a *non-repeating* digit $(6)$ followed by a *repeating digit* $(7)$.\n\nTo express this decimal as a fraction, we need to follow five steps. The extra step deals with the non-repeating digit.\n\n- **Step 1:** We define our number as $x$:\n$x = 0.67777 \\ldots$\n\n- **Step 2:** Since we have one *non-repeating* digit, we multiply $x$ by $10^1 = 10$ to create a number containing only repeating digits after the decimal point. This gives\n$10x|= 6.777…$.\n\n- **Step 3**. Since we have one *repeating* digit, we multiply $10x$ by $10^{1}=10$ to create a number with the same digits after the decimal point as $10x$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 10 \\cdot 10x|= 10 \\cdot 6.77777…; 100x|= 67.777…\n$$\n\n- **Step 4:** We subtract $10x$ from $100x$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 100x|= 67.7777…; - 10x|= 6.7777…; 90x|= 61\n$$\n- **Step 5:** Finally, we solve the equation for $x$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 90x|= 61; \\frac{90x}{90}|= \\frac{61}{90}; x|= \\frac{61}{90}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n0.67777 \\ldots = \\dfrac{61}{90}\n$$\n\nLet's look at an example of a decimal containing two non-repeating digits." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186153476 1064 "Consider the following decimal:\n\n$$\n0.67777\\ldots\n$$\n\nThis decimal contains a *non-repeating* digit $(6)$ followed by a *repeating digit* $(7)$.\n\nTo express this decimal as a fraction, we need to follow five steps. The extra step deals with the non-repeating digit.\n\n- **Step 1:** We define our number as $x$:\n$x = 0.67777 \\ldots$\n\n- **Step 2:** Since we have one *non-repeating* digit, we multiply $x$ by $10^1 = 10$ to create a number containing only repeating digits after the decimal point. This gives\n$\\begin{aligned}10x & =6.777…\\hspace{0.222em}.\\end{aligned}$.\n\n- **Step 3**. Since we have one *repeating* digit, we multiply $10x$ by $10^{1}=10$ to create a number with the same digits after the decimal point as $10x$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\times 10x & =10\\times 6.77777… \\\\ 100x & =67.777…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 4:** We subtract $10x$ from $100x$:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}100x & =67.{\\color{blue}{7777}}… \\\\ -\\hspace{2em}10x & =6.{\\color{blue}{7777}}… \\\\ \\hline 90x & =61\\end{array}\n$$\n- **Step 5:** Finally, we solve the equation for $x$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}90x & =61 \\\\ \\frac{90x}{90} & =\\frac{61}{90} \\\\ x & =\\frac{61}{90}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n0.67777 \\ldots = \\dfrac{61}{90}\n$$\n\nLet's look at an example of a decimal containing two non-repeating digits." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186157017 1074 "In this case, we need to show the following:\n$(20 \\mid a) \\: \\land\\: (21 \\mid a) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 35 \\mid a$\n\nFirst, we recall the following fact:\n$(d \\mid a) \\:\\land\\: (b \\mid d) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad b \\mid a$\nTherefore, we start our proof as follows:\n\n> Because $5 \\mid 20$ and $7 \\mid 21$, we have\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}20∣a & ⇒5∣a, \\\\ 21∣a & ⇒7∣a\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nNotice that $5$ and $7$ are coprime, which means their greatest common divisor is $1{:}$\n\n> Since $5$ and $7$ are coprime, we have $\\gcd(5, 7) = 1$.\n\nWe now use Bézout’s identity:\n\n> By Bézout’s identity, there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that\n> $5u+7v = 1$.\n\nSince we want to relate $a$ with $5$ and $7$, we multiply both sides by $a{:}$\n\n> Multiplying both sides by $a$, we get\n> $5ua+7va = a$.\n\nWe now use the fact that $5\\mid a$ and $7 \\mid a$, along with the definition of divisibility.\n\n> Since $5 \\mid a$ and $7 \\mid a$, there exist integers $k$ and $m$ such that $a = 5k$ and $a = 7m$.\n\nWe use these expressions for $a$ to rewrite the left-hand side of the equality above, obtaining an expression relating $35$ and $a{:}$\n\n> Substituting these for $a$ into the left-hand side of the expression above, we obtain\n> $\\begin{aligned}a & =5ua+7va \\\\  & =5u(7m)+7v(5k) \\\\  & =35um+35vk \\\\  & =35(um+vk).\\end{aligned}$.\n\nBy the definition of divisibility once more, we have that $35$ divides $a{:}$\n\n> Since $um+vk$ is an integer, we conclude that $35 \\mid a$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186157017 1074 "In this case, we need to show the following:\n$(20 \\mid a) \\: \\land\\: (21 \\mid a) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 35 \\mid a$\n\nFirst, we recall the following fact:\n$(d \\mid a) \\:\\land\\: (b \\mid d) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad b \\mid a$\nTherefore, we start our proof as follows:\n\n> Because $5 \\mid 20$ and $7 \\mid 21$, we have\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}20∣a & ⇒5∣a, \\\\ 21∣a & ⇒7∣a\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nNotice that $5$ and $7$ are coprime, which means their greatest common divisor is $1{:}$\n\n> Since $5$ and $7$ are coprime, we have $\\gcd(5, 7) = 1$.\n\nWe now use Bézout’s identity:\n\n> By Bézout’s identity, there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that\n> $5u+7v = 1$.\n\nSince we want to relate $a$ with $5$ and $7$, we multiply both sides by $a{:}$\n\n> Multiplying both sides by $a$, we get\n> $5ua+7va = a$.\n\nWe now use the fact that $5\\mid a$ and $7 \\mid a$, along with the definition of divisibility.\n\n> Since $5 \\mid a$ and $7 \\mid a$, there exist integers $k$ and $m$ such that $a = 5k$ and $a = 7m$.\n\nWe use these expressions for $a$ to rewrite the left-hand side of the equality above, obtaining an expression relating $35$ and $a{:}$\n\n> Substituting these for $a$ into the left-hand side of the expression above, we obtain\n> $a|= 5ua + 7va; = 5u(7m) + 7v(5k); = 35um + 35vk; = 35(um + vk)$.\n\nBy the definition of divisibility once more, we have that $35$ divides $a{:}$\n\n> Since $um+vk$ is an integer, we conclude that $35 \\mid a$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186157056 1074 "In this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{a is divisible by 10} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\textrm{a is divisible by 2}\n$$\n\nRecall that if an integer $a$ is divisible by $d$, then there exists an integer $k$ such that\n\n$$\na = d \\cdot k\n$$\n\nUsing this definition, we begin our proof as follows:\n\n> By the definition of integer divisibility, if $a$ is divisible by $10$, then\n> $a = 10k$\n> for some integer $k$.\n\nNow, we'll rewrite our expression to explicitly show that $2$ is a factor of $a{:}$\n\n> Now, note that\n> $\\begin{aligned}a & =10k \\\\  & =2\\cdot 5\\cdot k \\\\  & =2(5k) \\\\  & =2m\\end{aligned}$\n> where $m=5k$ is an integer.\n\nFinally, we use the definition of integer divisibility once more:\n\n> Therefore, by the definition of integer divisibility, since $a=2m$, we conclude that $a$ is divisible by $2$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186157056 1074 "In this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{a is divisible by 10} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\textrm{a is divisible by 2}\n$$\n\nRecall that if an integer $a$ is divisible by $d$, then there exists an integer $k$ such that\n\n$$\na = d \\cdot k\n$$\n\nUsing this definition, we begin our proof as follows:\n\n> By the definition of integer divisibility, if $a$ is divisible by $10$, then\n> $a = 10k$\n> for some integer $k$.\n\nNow, we'll rewrite our expression to explicitly show that $2$ is a factor of $a{:}$\n\n> Now, note that\n> $a|= 10k; = 2 \\cdot 5 \\cdot k; = 2(5k); = 2m$\n> where $m=5k$ is an integer.\n\nFinally, we use the definition of integer divisibility once more:\n\n> Therefore, by the definition of integer divisibility, since $a=2m$, we conclude that $a$ is divisible by $2$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186157057 1074 "In this case, we need to show the following:\n$(4 \\mid a) \\: \\land\\: (15 \\mid a) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 60 \\mid a$\n\nNotice that $4$ and $15$ are coprime, which means their greatest common divisor is $1{:}$\n\n> Since $4$ and $15$ are coprime, we have $\\gcd(4, 15) = 1$.\n\nWe now use Bézout’s identity:\n\n> By Bézout’s identity, there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that\n> $4u+15v = 1$.\n\nSince we want to relate $a$ with $4$ and $15$, we multiply both sides by $a{:}$\n\n> Multiplying both sides by $a$, we get\n> $4ua+15va = a$.\n\nWe use now the fact that $4\\mid a$ and $15 \\mid a$, along with the definition of divisibility.\n\n> Since $4 \\mid a$ and $15 \\mid a$, there exists integers $k$ and $m$ such that $a = 4k$ and $a = 15m$.\n\nWe use these expressions for $a$ to rewrite the left-hand side of the equality above, obtaining an expression relating $60$ and $a{:}$\n\n> Substituting these expressions for $a$ into the left-hand side of the expression above, we obtain\n> $\\begin{aligned}a & =4ua+15va \\\\  & =4u(15m)+15v(4k) \\\\  & =60um+60vk \\\\  & =60(um+vk).\\end{aligned}$.\n\nBy the definition of divisibility once more, we have that $60$ divides $a{:}$\n\n> Since $um+vk$ is an integer, we conclude that $60 \\mid a$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186157057 1074 "In this case, we need to show the following:\n$(4 \\mid a) \\: \\land\\: (15 \\mid a) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 60 \\mid a$\n\nNotice that $4$ and $15$ are coprime, which means their greatest common divisor is $1{:}$\n\n> Since $4$ and $15$ are coprime, we have $\\gcd(4, 15) = 1$.\n\nWe now use Bézout’s identity:\n\n> By Bézout’s identity, there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that\n> $4u+15v = 1$.\n\nSince we want to relate $a$ with $4$ and $15$, we multiply both sides by $a{:}$\n\n> Multiplying both sides by $a$, we get\n> $4ua+15va = a$.\n\nWe use now the fact that $4\\mid a$ and $15 \\mid a$, along with the definition of divisibility.\n\n> Since $4 \\mid a$ and $15 \\mid a$, there exists integers $k$ and $m$ such that $a = 4k$ and $a = 15m$.\n\nWe use these expressions for $a$ to rewrite the left-hand side of the equality above, obtaining an expression relating $60$ and $a{:}$\n\n> Substituting these expressions for $a$ into the left-hand side of the expression above, we obtain\n> $a|= 4ua + 15va; = 4u(15m) + 15v(4k); = 60um + 60vk; = 60(um + vk)$.\n\nBy the definition of divisibility once more, we have that $60$ divides $a{:}$\n\n> Since $um+vk$ is an integer, we conclude that $60 \\mid a$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186157058 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p$.\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1$.\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n\n$$\n3n-1 \\text{is even} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad n \\text{is odd}\n$$\n\nTo prove $P \\Rightarrow Q$, it suffices to prove the contrapositive\n\n$$\n\\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\nSo, let's write down the contrapositive of our statement.\n\n> The contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:\n> $n \\text{is even} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 3n-1 \\text{is odd}$\n> We'll prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is even.\n\n> If $n$ is even, then $n=2a$ for some integer $a$.\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into the expression $3n-1$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2$, plus $1$.\n\n> There