ion as follows:\n\n> Therefore, $45x+27y \\neq 1$ for any $x,y \\in \\mathbb{Z}$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186171791 1074 "Suppose we have a contingency table with $n$ rows and $m$ columns. Then, the random variable\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i} \\sum_{j} \\frac{(O_{ij} - E_{ij})^2}{E_{ij}}\n$$\n\napproximately follows a chi-square distribution with $\\nu$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $O_{ij}$ is the observed frequency for row $i$ and column $j$, and\n- $E_{ij}$ is the expected frequency for row $i$ and column $j$, as predicted under the null hypothesis that the row and column factors are independent.\n\nThis approximation holds when all expected frequencies $E_{ij}$ are sufficiently large (typically\n\n$$\nE_{ij} \\geq 5\n$$\n\nfor each cell), and the observations are independent.\n\nNotice that all of the expected frequencies are greater than or equal to $5$.\n\nFirst, we calculate the term\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(O_{ij} - E_{ij})^2}{E_{ij}}\n$$\n\nfor each combination of values of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((O_{11} - E_{11})^{2})/(E_{11}) &= ((37 - 33.2)^{2})/(33.2) \\approx 0.4349 \\\\\n((O_{12} - E_{12})^{2})/(E_{12}) &= ((3 - 6.8)^{2})/(6.8) \\approx 2.1235 \\\\\n((O_{21} - E_{21})^{2})/(E_{21}) &= ((46 - 49.8)^{2})/(49.8) \\approx 0.2900 \\\\\n((O_{22} - E_{22})^{2})/(E_{22}) &= ((14 - 10.2)^{2})/(10.2) \\approx 1.4157\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we rounded to four decimal places.\n\nFirst, we compute our test statistic by summing the above results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX &= ∑_(i)∑_(j)((O_{ij} - E_{ij})^{2})/(E_{ij}) \\\\\n&= 0.4349 + 2.1235 + 0.2900 + 1.4157 \\\\\n&\\approx 4.3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the test statistic is approximately $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}4.3}$.\n\nWe now compute the number of degrees of freedom:\n\n- Given a contingency table with $n$ rows and $m$ columns: the number of constraints is $1 + (m-1) + (n-1)$ the total number of observations must equal $N$, and the estimates $\\hat p (A_1), \\hat p(A_2),\\ldots \\hat p(A_{n-1})$ and $\\hat p(B_1), \\hat p(B_2), \\ldots \\hat p(B_{m-1})$ set a constraint each. Notice that the $\\hat p(A_n)$ and $\\hat p(B_m)$ can be deduced by the others, so they don't create a constraint. Therefore, the number of degrees of freedom $\\nu$ is calculated as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ν & =\\text{number of cells}-\\text{number of constraints} \\\\  & =nm-1-(n-1)-(m-1) \\\\  & =nm-n-m+1 \\\\  & =(n-1)(m-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, in our case there are $\\nu = (2-1)(2-1)=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ degree of freedom.\n\nFrom the given chi-square table, the critical value for $\\nu=1$ at a $5\\%$ significance level is\n\n$$\n\\chi^2_{\\textrm{critical}}=3.841\n$$\n\nand the critical region is\n\n$$\nX\\geq 3.841\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/f1/f17c7174d7df5c99801af4db09cdb840b0471f6254e757575f4d59110369926d.png)\n\nOur test statistic $(4.3)$ lies inside the critical region. Therefore, there is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{sufficient}}$ evidence to reject the null hypothesis." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186171791 1074 "Suppose we have a contingency table with $n$ rows and $m$ columns. Then, the random variable\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i} \\sum_{j} \\frac{(O_{ij} - E_{ij})^2}{E_{ij}}\n$$\n\napproximately follows a chi-square distribution with $\\nu$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $O_{ij}$ is the observed frequency for row $i$ and column $j$, and\n- $E_{ij}$ is the expected frequency for row $i$ and column $j$, as predicted under the null hypothesis that the row and column factors are independent.\n\nThis approximation holds when all expected frequencies $E_{ij}$ are sufficiently large (typically\n\n$$\nE_{ij} \\geq 5\n$$\n\nfor each cell), and the observations are independent.\n\nNotice that all of the expected frequencies are greater than or equal to $5$.\n\nFirst, we calculate the term\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(O_{ij} - E_{ij})^2}{E_{ij}}\n$$\n\nfor each combination of values of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((O_{11} - E_{11})^{2})/(E_{11}) &= ((37 - 33.2)^{2})/(33.2) \\approx 0.4349 \\\\\n((O_{12} - E_{12})^{2})/(E_{12}) &= ((3 - 6.8)^{2})/(6.8) \\approx 2.1235 \\\\\n((O_{21} - E_{21})^{2})/(E_{21}) &= ((46 - 49.8)^{2})/(49.8) \\approx 0.2900 \\\\\n((O_{22} - E_{22})^{2})/(E_{22}) &= ((14 - 10.2)^{2})/(10.2) \\approx 1.4157\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we rounded to four decimal places.\n\nFirst, we compute our test statistic by summing the above results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX &= ∑_(i)∑_(j)((O_{ij} - E_{ij})^{2})/(E_{ij}) \\\\\n&= 0.4349 + 2.1235 + 0.2900 + 1.4157 \\\\\n&\\approx 4.3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the test statistic is approximately $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}4.3}$.\n\nWe now compute the number of degrees of freedom:\n\n- Given a contingency table with $n$ rows and $m$ columns: the number of constraints is $1 + (m-1) + (n-1)$ the total number of observations must equal $N$, and the estimates $\\hat p (A_1), \\hat p(A_2),\\ldots \\hat p(A_{n-1})$ and $\\hat p(B_1), \\hat p(B_2), \\ldots \\hat p(B_{m-1})$ set a constraint each. Notice that the $\\hat p(A_n)$ and $\\hat p(B_m)$ can be deduced by the others, so they don't create a constraint. Therefore, the number of degrees of freedom $\\nu$ is calculated as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle ν|= number of cells - number of constraints; = nm - 1 - (n - 1) - (m - 1); = nm - n - m + 1; = (n - 1)(m - 1)\n$$\nSo, in our case there are $\\nu = (2-1)(2-1)=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ degree of freedom.\n\nFrom the given chi-square table, the critical value for $\\nu=1$ at a $5\\%$ significance level is\n\n$$\n\\chi^2_{\\textrm{critical}}=3.841\n$$\n\nand the critical region is\n\n$$\nX\\geq 3.841\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/f1/f17c7174d7df5c99801af4db09cdb840b0471f6254e757575f4d59110369926d.png)\n\nOur test statistic $(4.3)$ lies inside the critical region. Therefore, there is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{sufficient}}$ evidence to reject the null hypothesis." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186171802 1074 "Let's inspect each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. In general, if $Y = X_1 + X_2$, where $X_1$ and $X_2$ are independent random variables with MGF's $M_{X_1}(t)$ and $M_{X_2}(t)$, respectively, then the MGF of $Y$ is\n$M_Y(t) = M_{X_1}(t) M_{X_2}(t)$.\n- Statement II is true. Since $Y = X_1 + X_2$, we know that $Y$ and $X_1+X_2$ have the same probability distribution. We represent this as $Y \\stackrel{d}{=} X_1 + X_2$.\n- Statement III is true. A normally distributed random variable $X \\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)$ has the following MGF:\n$M(t) = e^{\\mu t + \\sigma^2 t^2/2}, \\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R$\nSo the MGF's for $X_1 \\sim N(5, 4)$ and $X_2 \\sim N(3, 2)$ are\n$$\n\\displaystyle M_{X_1}(t) = e^{5t + 2t^2}, \\qquad M_{X_2}(t) = e^{3t + t^2},\n$$\nand we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{Y}(t) & ={M}_{{X}_{1}}(t){M}_{{X}_{2}}(t) \\\\  & ={e}^{5t+2{t}^{2}}\\cdot {e}^{3t+{t}^{2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{8t+3{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $M_Y(t)$ is the MGF for the distribution $N(8, 6)$, we conclude that $Y \\sim N(8, 6)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186171802 1074 "Let's inspect each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. In general, if $Y = X_1 + X_2$, where $X_1$ and $X_2$ are independent random variables with MGF's $M_{X_1}(t)$ and $M_{X_2}(t)$, respectively, then the MGF of $Y$ is\n$M_Y(t) = M_{X_1}(t) M_{X_2}(t)$.\n- Statement II is true. Since $Y = X_1 + X_2$, we know that $Y$ and $X_1+X_2$ have the same probability distribution. We represent this as $Y \\stackrel{d}{=} X_1 + X_2$.\n- Statement III is true. A normally distributed random variable $X \\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)$ has the following MGF:\n$M(t) = e^{\\mu t + \\sigma^2 t^2/2}, \\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R$\nSo the MGF's for $X_1 \\sim N(5, 4)$ and $X_2 \\sim N(3, 2)$ are\n$$\n\\displaystyle M_{X_1}(t) = e^{5t + 2t^2}, \\qquad M_{X_2}(t) = e^{3t + t^2},\n$$\nand we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle M_{Y}(t)|= M_{X_{1}}(t)M_{X_{2}}(t); = e^{5t + 2t^{2}} \\cdot e^{3t + t^{2}}; = e^{8t + 3t^{2}}.\n$$\nSince $M_Y(t)$ is the MGF for the distribution $N(8, 6)$, we conclude that $Y \\sim N(8, 6)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186173132 1064 "Let's return to the example of the distribution of the daily incomes of three shops.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Group 1</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Group 2</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Group 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10.8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9.2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We will denote the number of groups as $K$. In our case, $K=3$.\n- Each group will be identified with a number $k$ between $1$ and $K$.\n- For each group, we will denote the number of data points in the group as $n_k$.\nIn our case, we reported the incomes of $4$ days for each shop. So, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle n_1 = 4, \\quad n_2 = 4, \\quad n_3 = 4.\n$$\n- We denote $x_{jk}$ the $j$th data point from the $k$th group. So, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11} & =15,\\phantom{.5}\\hspace{2em}{x}_{21}=17,\\phantom{.5}\\hspace{2em}{x}_{31}=16,\\phantom{.5}\\hspace{2em}{x}_{41}=14, \\\\ {x}_{12} & =12.5,\\hspace{2em}{x}_{22}=11.5,\\hspace{2em}{x}_{32}=13,\\phantom{.5}\\hspace{2em}{x}_{42}=11, \\\\ {x}_{13} & =9,\\phantom{5.5}\\hspace{2em}{x}_{23}=11,\\phantom{.5}\\hspace{2em}{x}_{33}=10.8,\\hspace{2em}{x}_{43}=9.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- We denote $\\overline{x}_{k}$ the sample mean of the $k$th group:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\overline{x}_{1} = 15.5, \\qquad \\overline{x}_{2} = 12, \\qquad \\overline{x}_{3} = 10\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186173132 1064 "Let's return to the example of the distribution of the daily incomes of three shops.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Group 1</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Group 2</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Group 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10.8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9.2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We will denote the number of groups as $K$. In our case, $K=3$.\n- Each group will be identified with a number $k$ between $1$ and $K$.\n- For each group, we will denote the number of data points in the group as $n_k$.\nIn our case, we reported the incomes of $4$ days for each shop. So, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle n_1 = 4, \\quad n_2 = 4, \\quad n_3 = 4.\n$$\n- We denote $x_{jk}$ the $j$th data point from the $k$th group. So, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle x_{11}|= 15,.5x_{21} = 17,.5x_{31} = 16,.5x_{41} = 14,; x_{12}|= 12.5, x_{22} = 11.5, x_{32} = 13,.5x_{42} = 11,; x_{13}|= 9, 5.5x_{23} = 11,.5x_{33} = 10.8, x_{43} = 9.2.\n$$\n- We denote $\\overline{x}_{k}$ the sample mean of the $k$th group:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\overline{x}_{1} = 15.5, \\qquad \\overline{x}_{2} = 12, \\qquad \\overline{x}_{3} = 10\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186173134 1064 "Let's return to the following contingency table, which includes the observed and expected frequencies for a sample of $N=60$ students who sat an exam from two schools.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pass</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Fail</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">School 1</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$O_{11}=18$<br>$E_{11} =16.8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$O_{12}=6$<br>$E_{12} =7.2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">School 2</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$O_{18}=24$<br>$E_{21} =25.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$O_{12}=12$<br>$E_{22} =10.8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nRecall that for a chi-square goodness-of-fit test, the number of degrees of freedom is the number of independent sample categories (cells). The case for independence tests is similar:\n\n- In this case, we have a contingency table with $2\\cdot 2 = 4$ cells.\n- In total, we have $3$ constraints:The sum of the observations must equal $N$.We also formed two estimates, which both set an additional constraint:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\hat p (\\textrm{Students is from School 1}) = 0.4, \\qquad \\hat p (\\textrm{Student Passes the Exam}) = 0.7\n$$\nWe figured these out earlier in the lesson. Note that the probabilities\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\hat p (\\textrm{Students is from School 2}) = 0.6, \\qquad \\hat p (\\textrm{Student Passes the Exam}) = 0.3\n$$\ndo not form additional constraints because they can be derived using the rule of complements. Similarly, the probabilities of the various intersections do not form additional constraints.\n\nTherefore, the number of degrees of freedom for this test is\n\n$$\n\\nu = 4 - 3 = 1\n$$\n\nMore generally, given a contingency table with $n$ rows and $m$ columns:\n\n- The total number of observations must equal $N$, and the estimates $\\hat p (A_1), \\hat p(A_2),\\ldots \\hat p(A_{n-1})$ and $\\hat p(B_1), \\hat p(B_2), \\ldots \\hat p(B_{m-1})$ set a constraint each. The estimates $\\hat p(A_n)$ and $\\hat p(B_m)$ can be deduced by the others, so they don't create a constraint.\n\n- Therefore, the number of degrees of freedom $\\nu$ is calculated as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ν & =\\text{number of cells}-\\text{number of constraints} \\\\  & =nm-1-(n-1)-(m-1) \\\\  & =nm-n-m+1 \\\\  & =(n-1)(m-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186173134 1064 "Let's return to the following contingency table, which includes the observed and expected frequencies for a sample of $N=60$ students who sat an exam from two schools.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pass</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Fail</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">School 1</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\