n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle M ( t ) & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4 \\cdot  \\mathrm{\\Gamma } ( 2 )}} {\\int }_{0}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }} x \\cdot  {e}^{x ( t - 1 / 2 )} \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{d} x \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4 \\cdot  \\mathrm{\\Gamma } ( 2 )}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{( 1 / 2 - t {)}^{2}}} \\cdot  \\mathrm{\\Gamma } ( 2 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4 \\cdot  \\cancel{\\mathrm{\\Gamma } ( 2 )}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{( 1 / 2 - t {)}^{2}}} \\cdot  \\cancel{\\mathrm{\\Gamma } ( 2 )} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{ {2}^{2}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{( 1 / 2 - t {)}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\bbox[3pt,gainsboro]{ {\\color{blue}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{( 1 - 2 t {)}^{2}}}}} \\hspace{1em} \\text{for} \\hspace{1em} t < {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} ,\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nand $M(t)$ is undefined for\n\n$$\nt\\geq \\dfrac 12\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186176518 1071 "From the information given below, find all of the angles that are congruent.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\\mathrm{∠}A & ={60}^{∘}, \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}B & ={30}^{∘}, \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}C & ={20}^{∘}, \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}D & =m\\mathrm{∠}A, \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}E & ={90}^{∘}-m\\mathrm{∠}B, \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}F & ={180}^{∘}-m\\mathrm{∠}C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186176518 1071 "From the information given below, find all of the angles that are congruent.\n$$\n\\displaystyle m∠A|= 60^{∘},; m∠B|= 30^{∘},; m∠C|= 20^{∘},; m∠D|= m∠A,; m∠E|= 90^{∘} - m∠B,; m∠F|= 180^{∘} - m∠C.\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186176956 1064 "The table below gives the moment-generating functions $M(t)$ of some common continuous probability distributions.\n\n| Distribution | $M(t)$ | $t$-domain |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $U[a,b]$ | ${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{ {e}^{t b} - {e}^{t a}}{t ( b - a )}} , \\hspace{1em} & t \\ne  0 \\\\ 1 , \\hspace{2em} & t = 0\\end{array} \\right. }$ | $t\\in\\mathbb R$ |\n| $N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ | $\\exp\\left({\\mu t+\\dfrac12\\sigma^2t^2}\\right)$ | $\\,\\,\\,t\\in\\mathbb R\\,\\,\\,$ |\n| $\\chi^2(k)$ | $(1-2t)^{-k/2}$ | $t<\\dfrac12$ |\n| $\\text{Exp}(\\lambda)$ | $\\dfrac{\\lambda}{\\lambda-t}$ | $t<\\lambda$ |\n| $\\Gamma(\\alpha,\\lambda)$ | $\\left(\\dfrac{\\lambda}{\\lambda-t}\\right)^\\alpha$ | $t<\\lambda$ |\n\nFrom here, we can make some observations:\n\n- At the beginning of this lesson, we proved the formula for a continuous uniform random variable $X\\sim U[a,b]$ for some specific values of $a$ and $b$, where we assumed $t\\neq 0$. However, when $t=0$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M(0) & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{1}{b-a}\\cdot {e}^{0\\cdot x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{1}{b-a}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{b-a}{b-a} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Notice the similarity between the MGFs of the exponential and gamma random variables. This is reflective of the relationship between the distributions: $\\textrm{Exp}(\\lambda)$ gives the total time until the next event occurs in a Poisson process, while $\\Gamma(\\alpha, \\lambda)$ gives the total time for $\\alpha$ events to occur. In a future lesson, we will discuss the precise relationship between these two MGFs." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186176956 1064 "The table below gives the moment-generating functions $M(t)$ of some common continuous probability distributions.\n\n| Distribution | $M(t)$ | $t$-domain |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $U[a,b]$ | ${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{ {e}^{t b} - {e}^{t a}}{t ( b - a )}} , \\hspace{1em} & t \\ne  0 \\\\ 1 , \\hspace{2em} & t = 0\\end{array} \\right. }$ | $t\\in\\mathbb R$ |\n| $N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ | $\\exp\\left({\\mu t+\\dfrac12\\sigma^2t^2}\\right)$ | $\\,\\,\\,t\\in\\mathbb R\\,\\,\\,$ |\n| $\\chi^2(k)$ | $(1-2t)^{-k/2}$ | $t<\\dfrac12$ |\n| $\\text{Exp}(\\lambda)$ | $\\dfrac{\\lambda}{\\lambda-t}$ | $t<\\lambda$ |\n| $\\Gamma(\\alpha,\\lambda)$ | $\\left(\\dfrac{\\lambda}{\\lambda-t}\\right)^\\alpha$ | $t<\\lambda$ |\n\nFrom here, we can make some observations:\n\n- At the beginning of this lesson, we proved the formula for a continuous uniform random variable $X\\sim U[a,b]$ for some specific values of $a$ and $b$, where we assumed $t\\neq 0$. However, when $t=0$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle M(0)|= \\displaystyle \\int_{a}^{b}\\frac{1}{b - a} \\cdot e^{0 \\cdot x}dx; = \\displaystyle \\int_{a}^{b}\\frac{1}{b - a}dx; = \\frac{b - a}{b - a}; = 1.\n$$\n\n- Notice the similarity between the MGFs of the exponential and gamma random variables. This is reflective of the relationship between the distributions: $\\textrm{Exp}(\\lambda)$ gives the total time until the next event occurs in a Poisson process, while $\\Gamma(\\alpha, \\lambda)$ gives the total time for $\\alpha$ events to occur. In a future lesson, we will discuss the precise relationship between these two MGFs." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186177020 1064 "Suppose that Alice has a $20\\%$ chance of being late for work if it rains and a $5\\%$ chance of being late for work if it doesn't. Where Alice lives, the probability that it rains on a randomly selected day is $10\\%$.\n\nAlice's boss wants to determine the probability that Alice will be late on a randomly selected day.\n\nOne way of modeling this situation is to use a tree diagram.\n\nBefore we draw our tree diagram, let's summarize what we know:\n\n$$\nP(\\textrm{Rain}) = 0.1, \\qquad P(\\textrm{Late \\mid Rain}) = 0.2, \\qquad P(\\textrm{Late \\mid No Rain}) = 0.05\n$$\n\nWe're given probability data on Alice being late *given* whether or not it rains, which suggests that we set up our tree diagram as shown below.\n\n![](images/3a/3af417611214953e21920827706eb266e8c069414271980120f3f70a60e95fb5.png)\n\nWe now fill in the missing probabilities:\n\n- For the missing probability on the first level of our diagram, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\text{No Rain}) & =1-P(\\text{Rain}) \\\\  & =1-0.1 \\\\  & =0.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the missing probabilities on the second level, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\text{Not Late|Rain}) & =1-P(\\text{Late|Rain}) \\\\  & =1-0.2 \\\\  & =0.8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\text{Not Late|No Rain}) & =1-P(\\text{Late|No Rain}) \\\\  & =1-0.05 \\\\  & =0.95.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these probabilities to our diagram.\n\n![](images/f2/f2a896e33e582a384d8ed3f21a1b55f6aaf96641154c55e93d83f344122613af.png)\n\nTo calculate the probability that Alice will be late on a randomly selected day, we first highlight the relevant branches.\n\n![](images/ea/ead309fdf29fcf57a8d1ab6911650ac3cdc551207a9934d9c655469121fbdfd0.png)\n\nRemember that we multiply *across* the branches and add *between* them. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle P ( \\text{Late} ) & \\displaystyle  = P ( \\text{Rain} ) \\cdot  P ( \\text{Late|Rain} ) + P ( \\text{No Rain} ) \\cdot  P ( \\text{Late|No Rain} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 0.1 ) ( 0.2 ) + ( 0.9 ) ( 0.05 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.065 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 6.5 \\mathrm{\\% } .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is a $6.5\\%$ chance that Alice will be late on a randomly selected day." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186177020 1064 "Suppose that Alice has a $20\\%$ chance of being late for work if it rains and a $5\\%$ chance of being late for work if it doesn't. Where Alice lives, the probability that it rains on a randomly selected day is $10\\%$.\n\nAlice's boss wants to determine the probability that Alice will be late on a randomly selected day.\n\nOne way of modeling this situation is to use a tree diagram.\n\nBefore we draw our tree diagram, let's summarize what we know:\n\n$$\nP(\\textrm{Rain}) = 0.1, \\qquad P(\\textrm{Late \\mid Rain}) = 0.2, \\qquad P(\\textrm{Late \\mid No Rain}) = 0.05\n$$\n\nWe're given probability data on Alice being late *given* whether or not it rains, which suggests that we set up our tree diagram as shown below.\n\n![](images/3a/3af417611214953e21920827706eb266e8c069414271980120f3f70a60e95fb5.png)\n\nWe now fill in the missing probabilities:\n\n- For the missing probability on the first level of our diagram, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle P(No Rain)|= 1 - P(Rain); = 1 - 0.1; = 0.9.\n$$\n- For the missing probabilities on the second level, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle P(Not Late \\mid Rain)|= 1 - P(Late \\mid Rain); = 1 - 0.2; = 0.8,\n$$\n$$\n\\displaystyle P(Not Late \\mid No Rain)|= 1 - P(Late \\mid No Rain); = 1 - 0.05; = 0.95.\n$$\n\nWe add these probabilities to our diagram.\n\n![](images/f2/f2a896e33e582a384d8ed3f21a1b55f6aaf96641154c55e93d83f344122613af.png)\n\nTo calculate the probability that Alice will be late on a randomly selected day, we first highlight the relevant branches.\n\n![](images/ea/ead309fdf29fcf57a8d1ab6911650ac3cdc551207a9934d9c655469121fbdfd0.png)\n\nRemember that we multiply *across* the branches and add *between* them. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle P ( \\text{Late} ) & \\displaystyle  = P ( \\text{Rain} ) \\cdot  P ( \\text{Late|Rain} ) + P ( \\text{No Rain} ) \\cdot  P ( \\text{Late|No Rain} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 0.1 ) ( 0.2 ) + ( 0.9 ) ( 0.05 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.065 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 6.5 \\mathrm{\\% } .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is a $6.5\\%$ chance that Alice will be late on a randomly selected day." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186177025 1064 "A company manufactures canned mixed nuts. The price function for this company is given by\n\n$$\np(x) = 60-0.01x\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ represents the number of units produced and sold per month. Additionally, each unit costs $\\$25$ to make, and the company's fixed costs are $\\$8\\,800$ per month.\n\nLet's calculate the profit function $P(x)$ for this company.\n\nRecall that profit function $P(x)$ is equal to the revenue function $R(x)$ minus the cost function $C(x)$:\n\n$$\nP(x)=R(x)-C(x)\n$$\n\nSo, to calculate the profit function, we first need to construct $R(x)$ and $C(x)$.\n\n- We're given the price function $p(x)$, so the revenue function is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =(\\text{number of units})\\cdot (\\text{price per unit}) \\\\  & =x\\cdot p(x) \\\\  & =x(60-0.01x) \\\\  & =60x-0.01{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- We're also given that the fixed costs are $\\$8\\,800$ per month and that each unit costs $\\$25$ to make. Therefore, the cost function is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(x) & =(\\text{fixed cost})+(\\text{number of units})\\cdot (\\text{cost per unit}) \\\\  & =8\\hspace{0.167em}800+x\\cdot 25 \\\\  & =8\\hspace{0.167em}800+25x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now calculate the profit function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x) &= R(x) - C(x) \\\\\n&= (60x - 0.01x^{2}) - (8800 + 25x) \\\\\n&= 60x - 0.01x^{2} - 8800 - 25x \\\\\n&=-0.01x^{2} + 35x - 8800\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186177025 1064 "A company manufactures canned mixed nuts. The price function for this company is given by\n\n$$\np(x) = 60-0.01x\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ represents the number of units produced and sold per month. Additionally, each unit costs $\\$25$ to make, and the company's fixed costs are $\\$8\\,800$ per month.\n\nLet's calculate the profit function $P(x)$ for this company.\n\nRecall that profit function $P(x)$ is equal to the revenue function $R(x)$ minus the cost function $C(x)$:\n\n$$\nP(x)=R(x)-C(x)\n$$\n\nSo, to calculate the profit function, we first need to construct $R(x)$ and $C(x)$.\n\n- We're given the price function $p(x)$, so the revenue function is\n$$\n\\displaystyle R(x)|= (number of units) \\cdot (price per unit); = x \\cdot p(x); = x(60 - 0.01x); = 60x - 0.01x^{2}.\n$$\n- We're also given that the fixed costs are 