\theta \\to 0}\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{sin{\\color{blue}{2\\theta }}}{{\\color{blue}{2\\theta }}}\\end{aligned}\n$$Let $x = 2\\theta$. Then, since $x\\to0$ as $\\theta \\to 0$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle 2 \\cdot \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin \\color{blue}2\\theta}{\\color{blue}2\\theta} = 2 \\cdot \\lim_{x \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin \\color{blue}x}{\\color{blue}x} = 2 \\cdot 1 =2.\n$$\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0} \\: \\dfrac{1}{\\cos 2\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos 0} = \\dfrac11 = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{θ \\to  0}\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{θ} \\cdot lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{1}{\\cos 2θ} = 2 \\cdot 1 = 2\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186179609 1074 "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{θ \\to  0}\\frac{\\tan 2θ}{θ} &= \\lim_{θ \\to  0}(\\left(\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{\\cos 2θ}\\right))/(θ) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{θ \\to  0}(\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{θ} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cos 2θ}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{θ \\to  0}\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{θ} \\cdot lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{1}{\\cos 2θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0$, both numerator and denominator approach $0$. So, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin 2\\theta}{\\theta} = \\dfrac{0}{0},\n$$\nwhich is an indeterminate form. Instead, let's recall the following special limit:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin x}{x} =1\n$$We rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{θ}|= \\lim_{θ \\to  0}(2 \\cdot \\frac{\\sin 2θ}{2θ}); = 2 \\cdot lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{\\sin2θ}{2θ}\n$$Let $x = 2\\theta$. Then, since $x\\to0$ as $\\theta \\to 0$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle 2 \\cdot \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin \\color{blue}2\\theta}{\\color{blue}2\\