irc = {\\color{red}{-}}\\cos\\theta_R = {\\color{red}{-}}\\cos 70^\\circ.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\cos 970^\\circ = -\\cos 70^\\circ\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186184123 1074 "We wish to express $\\cos 970^\\circ$ in terms of $\\cos \\theta_R$, where $\\theta_R$ is the reference angle corresponding to the angle\n\n$$\n\\theta = 970^\\circ\n$$\n\nTo express $\\cos 970^\\circ$ in terms of $\\cos\\theta_R$, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1**: Find the corresponding coterminal angle that lies in the range $[0^\\circ, 360^\\circ)$. We do this by subtracting integer multiples of $360^\\circ$ until we get our desired angle.\n$$\n\\displaystyle 970^{∘} - 360^{∘}|= 610^{∘}; 970^{∘} - 2 \\cdot 360^{∘}|= 250^{∘}✓\n$$\nTherefore,\n$\\cos 970^\\circ = \\cos 250^\\circ$.\n\n![](images/33/331d0ec27c253f2c1712365d5519655555377433b82b8a60c4f736eb56dbd8b0.png)\n\n- **Step 2**: Compute the reference angle. Since $\\theta = 250^\\circ$ is in the $3$rd quadrant, the reference angle is\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 250^\\circ - 180^\\circ= 70^\\circ\n$$\n\n- **Step 3**: Determine the correct sign. The cosine function is negative $({\\color{red}{-}})$ in the third quadrant. Therefore,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\cos 970^\\circ = {\\color{red}{-}}\\cos\\theta_R = {\\color{red}{-}}\\cos 70^\\circ.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\cos 970^\\circ = -\\cos 70^\\circ\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186184128 1074 "Notice that the logarithms share the same base.\n\nFirst, we apply the power rule to the *first* term to make the coefficient in front of each logarithm the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\log_{3} (\\sqrt{2}x) - \\log_{3} (x - 1) &= \\log_{3} (3x) \\\\\n\\log_{3} (\\sqrt{2}x)^{2} - \\log_{3} (x - 1) &= \\log_{3} (3x) \\\\\n\\log_{3} (2x^{2}) - \\log_{3} (x - 1) &= \\log_{3} (3x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we combine the logarithms on the left-hand side using the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{3} \\left(\\frac{2x^{2}}{x - 1}\\right) &= \\log_{3} (3x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the equation for $x$ using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 {x}^{2}}{x - 1}} & \\displaystyle  = 3 x \\\\ \\displaystyle ( x - 1 ) \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 {x}^{2}}{x - 1}} & \\displaystyle  = ( x - 1 ) \\cdot  ( 3 x ) \\\\ \\displaystyle \\cancel{( x - 1 )} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 {x}^{2}}{\\cancel{x - 1}}} & \\displaystyle  = ( x - 1 ) \\cdot  ( 3 x ) \\\\ \\displaystyle 2 {x}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = 3 {x}^{2} - 3 x \\\\ \\displaystyle {x}^{2} - 3 x & \\displaystyle  = 0 \\\\ \\displaystyle x ( x - 3 ) & \\displaystyle  = 0\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSo, $x = 0$ and $x =3$.\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting them back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $x=0$ back into the original equation, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{log}_{3}(\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 0)-{log}_{3}(0-1) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{3}(3\\cdot 0) \\\\ 2{log}_{3}(0)-{log}_{3}(-1) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{3}(0).\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis statement is false because $\\log_3 (0)$ and $\\log_3 (-1)$ are undefined. Therefore, $x=0$ is not a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=3$ back into the original equation, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{log}_{3}(3\\sqrt{2})-{log}_{3}(3-1) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{3}(3\\cdot 3) \\\\ {log}_{3}{(3\\sqrt{2})}^{2}-{log}_{3}(2) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{3}(9) \\\\ {log}_{3}(18)-{log}_{3}(2) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{3}(9) \\\\ {log}_{3}\\left(\\frac{18}{2}\\right) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{3}(9) \\\\ {log}_{3}(9) & ={log}_{3}(9).\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore, $x=3$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$x=3$ only\"." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186184128 1074 "Notice that the logarithms share the same base.\n\nFirst, we apply the power rule to the *first* term to make the coefficient in front of each logarithm the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{a