17592186576411 false] [17592186184519 1071 "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\,-1/2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x))=\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186184519 1071 "$$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to \\,-1/2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x))=\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186185275 1064 "A nondeterministic finite automaton accepts a finite string if, after processing the entire string, the automation ends in one of its final states. Otherwise, the string is rejected. The set of all accepted finite strings is the **language** of the NFA.\n\nFor example, consider the three strings $100$, $0101$, and $0110$. Are any of these accepted by the nondeterministic automaton represented by the diagram below?\n\n![](images/e0/e057a1ecaad653f3d39e5fed6e71f95813da7d75f17e7a9e0f5529b1cf99dbfc.png)\n\nTo find out, let's apply the extended transition function to our strings.\n\n- Processing the string $100$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\delta (q,100) & ={⋃}_{s\\in \\delta (q,1)}\\delta (s,00) \\\\  & =\\mathrm{∅}\\end{aligned}\n$$![](images/5e/5eda3e914d7d967c036c89ab6ee47f5c702a1a5b38fae45740971853c3794af5.png)\nThe process is \"stuck\". So, the string $100$ is rejected.\n\n- Processing the string $0101$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\delta (q,0101) & ={⋃}_{s\\in \\delta (q,0)}\\delta (s,101) \\\\  & =\\delta (r,101) \\\\  & ={⋃}_{s\\in \\delta (r,1)}\\delta (s,01) \\\\  & =\\delta (r,01) \\\\  & ={⋃}_{s\\in \\delta (r,0)}\\delta (s,1) \\\\  & =\\delta (q,1)\\cup \\delta (r,1) \\\\  & =\\mathrm{∅}\\cup \\{r\\} \\\\  & =\\{r\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$![](images/55/55a552f32aa9838da2a8e6b1751c8a7635d98f8457627594eaeeeeca451bc73d.png)\nThe process terminates at the state $r$. Since $r$ is a final (accepting) state, the string $0101$ is accepted.\n\n- Processing the string $0110$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\delta (q,0110) & ={⋃}_{s\\in \\delta (q,0)}\\delta (s,110) \\\\  & =\\delta (r,110) \\\\  & ={⋃}_{s\\in \\delta (r,1)}\\delta (s,10) \\\\  & =\\delta (r,10) \\\\  & ={⋃}_{s\\in \\delta (r,1)}\\delta (s,0) \\\\  & =\\delta (r,0) \\\\  & =\\{q,r\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$![](images/a8/a8d2550901c202cd4699381ccb3f642fd7daf5efb84ba36cc5e1dc3b63576508.png)\nThe process is terminated at the states $q$ and $r$. Since $r$ is a final (accepting) state, the string $0110$ is accepted.\n\nThe language of this NFA consists of all finite strings of $0$'s and $1$'s that start with $0$.\n\nWe begin processing a string in state $q$. Notice that when the automaton initially reads a $1$, the automaton gets stuck. However, when it initially encounters a $0$, it switches to the accepting state $r$. If the automaton is in $r$ and processes another symbol, it remains in the accepting state.\n\nTherefore, the automaton will end in the accepting state $r$ if and only if the input starts with $0$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186185275 1064 "A nondeterministic finite automaton accepts a finite string if, after processing the entire string, the automation ends in one of its final states. Otherwise, the string is rejected. The set of all accepted finite strings is the **language** of the NFA.\n\nFor example, consider the three strings $100$, $0101$, and $0110$. Are any of these accepted by the nondeterministic automaton represented by the diagram below?\n\n![](images/e0/e057a1ecaad653f3d39e5fed6e71f95813da7d75f17e7a9e0f5529b1cf99dbfc.png)\n\nTo find out, let's apply the extended transition function to our strings.\n\n- Processing the string $100$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle δ(q, 100)|= ⋃_(s ∈ δ(q, 1))δ(s, 00); = ∅\n$$![](images/5e/5eda3e914d7d967c036c89ab6ee47f5c702a1a5b38fae45740971853c3794af5.png)\nThe process is \"stuck\". So, the string $100$ is rejected.\n\n- Processing the string $0101$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle δ(q, 0101)|= ⋃_(s ∈ δ(q, 0))δ(s, 101); = δ(r, 101); = ⋃_(s ∈ δ(r, 1))δ(s, 01); = δ(r, 01); = ⋃_(s ∈ δ(r, 0))δ(s, 1); = δ(q, 1)∪δ(r, 1); = ∅∪{r}; = {r}\n$$![](images/55/55a552f32aa9838da2a8e6b1751c8a7635d98f8457627594eaeeeeca451bc73d.png)\nThe process terminates at the state $r$. Since $r$ is a final (accepting) state, the string $0101$ is accepted.\n\n- Processing the string $0110$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle δ(q, 0110)|= ⋃_(s ∈ δ(q, 0))δ(s, 110); = δ(r, 110); = ⋃_(s ∈ δ(r, 1))δ(s, 10); = δ(r, 10); = ⋃_(s ∈ δ(r, 1))δ(s, 0); = δ(r, 0); = {q, r}\n$$![](images/a8/a8d2550901c202cd4699381ccb3f642fd7daf5efb84ba36cc5e1dc3b63576508.png)\nThe process is terminated at the states $q$ and $r$. Since $r$ is a final (accepting) state, the string $0110$ is accepted.\n\nThe language of this NFA consists of all finite strings of $0$'s and $1$'s that start with $0$.\n\nWe begin processing a string in state $q$. Notice that when the automaton initially reads a $1$, the automaton gets stuck. However, when it initially encounters a $0$, it switches to the accepting state $r$. If the automaton is in $r$ and processes another symbol, it remains in the accepting state.\n\nTherefore, the automaton will end in the accepting state $r$ if and only if the input starts with $0$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186185413 1064 "In formal language theory, we often construct new languages by applying operations to existing ones.\nThese operations allow us to build complex languages from simpler ones.\n\nLet $L$ and $M$ be two languages. Then, we define the following operations on languages:\n\n- The **union** $L \\cup M$ consists of all the strings that belong to either language:\n$L \\cup M = \\big\\{\\alpha: \\: \\alpha \\in L \\: \\lor \\alpha \\in M \\big\\}$\n- The **concatenation** $LM$ consists of all the strings that can be obtained by concatenating (i.e., joining) a string from the first language with a string from the second language (in this exact order):\n$LM = \\big\\{\\alpha \\beta \\:: \\: \\alpha \\in L, \\: \\beta \\in M \\big\\}$\n- The **Kleene closure** (or **Kleene star**) $L^\\ast$ consists of all the strings that can be obtained by concatenating an arbitrary (finite) number of strings from the language:\n$$\n\\displaystyle L^\\ast = \\big\\{\\alpha_1\\alpha_2\\ldots\\alpha_n \\:: \\: n \\geq 0, \\: \\alpha_i \\in L \\: \\text{for} \\: i =1,\\ldots,n \\big\\}\n$$\nNote that the empty string $\\epsilon$ also belongs to $L^\\ast$.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the following languages:\n\n$$\nL = \\big\\{11, a, ab \\big\\}, \\qquad M = \\big\\{11, 10, 00 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nFrom these two, we construct new languages as follows:\n\n- The union of $L$ and $M$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\cup M & =\\{\\alpha \\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\alpha \\in \\{11,a,ab\\},\\hspace{0.222em}\\alpha \\in \\{11,10,00\\}\\} \\\\  & =\\{a,\\hspace{0.222em}ab,\\hspace{0.222em}11,\\hspace{0.222em}10,\\hspace{0.222em}00\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The concatenation of $L$ and $M$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LM & =\\{\\alpha \\beta \\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\alpha \\in \\{{\\color{blue}{11}},{\\color{blue}{a}},{\\color{blue}{ab}}\\},\\hspace{0.222em}\\beta \\in \\{{\\color{red}{11}},{\\color{red}{10}},{\\color{red}{00}}\\}\\} \\\\  & =\\{{\\color{blue}{11}}{\\color{red}{11}},\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}{11}}{\\color{red}{10}},\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}{11}}{\\color{red}{00}},\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}{a}}{\\color{red}{11}},\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}{a}}{\\color{red}{10}},\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}{a}}{\\color{red}{00}},\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}{ab}}{\\color{red}{11}},\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}{ab}}{\\color{red}{10}},\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}{ab}}{\\color{red}{00}}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The Kleene closure of $L$ contains strings such as $1111$, $11ab$ and $aaba11$. Indeed, notice that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1111 & =11\\cdot 11, \\\\ 11ab & =11\\cdot ab, \\\\ aaba11 & =a\\cdot ab\\cdot a\\cdot 11,\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhere each individual component belongs to $L$. On the other hand, some strings *not* in the closure $L^\\ast$ are$a1a$, since $1$'s can onl