ve that $b$ is an integer.\n> So, $n^2-n+7 = 2b+1$, which means that it is odd.\n\nNow, we consider the case when $n$ is odd.\n\n> **Case 2**. If $n$ is odd, then $n = 2a+1$ for some integer $a$.\n\nThe idea is to substitute\n\n$$\nn = 2a + 1\n$$\n\ninto our expression $n^2-n+7$ and show it is odd by writing it as $1$ more than a multiple of $2$.\n\n> As a result, we obtain\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-n+7 & =(2a+1)^{2}-(2a+1)+7 \\\\  & =(4{a}^{2}+4a+1)-(2a+1)+7 \\\\  & =4{a}^{2}+2a+7 \\\\  & =(4{a}^{2}+2a+6)+1 \\\\  & =2(2{a}^{2}+a+3)+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n> Let $b = 2a^2 + a + 3$. Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n> So, $n^2-n+7 = 2b+1$, which means it is odd.\n\nFinally, we combine both cases:\n\n> Combining the cases above, we can conclude that $n^2-n+7$ is odd for any integer $n$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186198087 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- An integer is *even* if it can be written as $2p$, where $p$ is an integer.\n- An integer is *odd* if it can be written as $2q + 1$, where $q$ is an integer.\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nnis an integer⇒n^{2} - n + 7is odd\n$$\n\nThe integers split naturally into even and odd numbers, and these two cases cover all possible integers. Therefore, it suffices to prove the following:\n\n$$\n(nis even)∨(nis odd)⇒n^{2} - n + 7is odd\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof by assuming $n$ is an integer and state that we'll proceed using cases.\n\n> Let $n$ be an integer. We consider the following cases:\n\nFirst, we consider the case when $n$ is even.\n\n> **Case 1**. If $n$ is even, then $n = 2a$ for some integer $a$.\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into our expression $n^2-n+7$ and show it is odd by writing it as $1$ more than a multiple of $2$.\n\n> As a result, we obtain\n> $$\n\\displaystyle n^{2} - n + 7|= (2a)^{2} - (2a) + 7; = 4a^{2} - 2a + 7; = (4a^{2} - 2a + 6) + 1; = 2(2a^{2} - a + 3) + 1.\n$$\n> Let $b = 2a^2 - a + 3$. Since $a$ is an integer, we have that