41978 17592186576411 true] [17592186203728 1074 "Recall that $A = B$ if and only if $A\\subseteq B$ and $B\\subseteq A$.\n\nSo, we begin our proof as follows:\n\n> To prove that $A=B$, we must show that $A \\subseteq B$ and $B \\subseteq A$.\n\nFirst, let's prove that $A \\subseteq B$. We denote an arbitrary element of $A$ by $x$ and use the definition of $A$.\n\n> **Part 1.** Proving that $A \\subseteq B$.\n> Let $x \\in A$. Then, there exists $m \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ such that $x = 3m+1$.\n\nNext, we rewrite $x$ to match the definition of $B$. In other words, we need to write\n\n$$\nx=3n+7\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\n> Note that\n> $x|= 3m + 1; = 3m + (7 - 6); = 3m - 6 + 7; = 3(m - 2) + 7$.\n> Denoting $n=m-2$, we get that\n> $x=3n+7$,\n> where $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. So, by the definition of $B$, we have $x \\in B$. Thus, $A \\subseteq B$.\n\nNext, let's prove that $B \\subseteq A$.\n\n> **Part 2.** Proving that $B \\subseteq A$.\n> Let $x \\in B$. Then, there exists $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ such that $x = 3n+7$.\n\nNext, we rewrite $x$ to match the definition of $A$. In other words, we need to write\n\n$$\nx=3m+1\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is an integer.\n\n> Note that\n> $x|= 3n + 7; = 3n + (6 + 1); = 3(n + 2) + 1$.\n> Denoting $m=n+2$, we get that\n> $x=3m+1$,\n> where $m \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. So, by the definition of $A$, we have $x \\in A$. Thus, $B \\subseteq A$.\n\nFinally, we've shown that $A\\subseteq B$ and $B\\subseteq A$. Therefore, $A=B$, and we state our conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, $A = B$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186203730 1074 "The connector \"if and only if\" means we must prove an equivalence.\n\n$$\nP \\Leftrightarrow Q\n$$\n\nHowever, instead of proving an equivalence directly, we can instead prove two conditional statements.\n\n$$\nP\\Leftrightarrow Q \\equiv (P\\Rightarrow Q) \\land (Q\\Rightarrow P)\n$$\n\nSo, we start our proof as follows:\n\nWe need to prove the following:\n\n$$\n(x+y)^2 =x^2+y^2 \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad \\big(x=0 \\quad \\lor \\quad y=0 \\big)\n$$\n\nThis is the same as proving two implications.\n\nFirst, we consider the \"left to right\" implication.\n\n**Case $\\Rightarrow{:}$** We need to show that\n\n$$\n(x+y)^2 =x^2+y^2 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\big(x=0 \\quad \\lor \\quad y=0 \\big)\n$$\n\nBy expanding the left-hand side and simplifying, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 2xy + y^{2} &= x^{2} + y^{2} \\\\\n2xy &= 0 \\\\\nx \\cdot y &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the left-hand side equals zero if $x=0$ or $y=0$.\n\nNext, we consider the \"right to left\" implication.\n\n> **Case $\\Leftarrow{:}$** We need to show that\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\big(x=0 \\quad \\lor \\quad y=0 \\big) \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad (x+y)^2= x^2+y^2.\n$$\n\nHere, consider the two cases separately.\n\n> We have two subcases:\n> **Subcase 1.** Let $x=0$. Then, substituting this into our equation, we obtain\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ (0+y)^{2} & ={0}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n> which is always true.\n\nAnd the second case.\n\n> **Subcase 2.** Let $y=0$. Then, substituting this into our equation, we obtain\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ (x+0)^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n> which is always true.\n> Hence, if $x=0$ or $y=0$ then $(x+y)^2=x^2+y^2$.\n\nFinally, we write down the conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, $(x+y)^2=x^2+y^2$ if and only if $x=0$ or $y=0$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186203730 1074 "The connector \"if and only if\" means we must prove an equivalence.\n\n$$\nP \\Leftrightarrow Q\n$$\n\nHowever, instead of proving an equivalence directly, we can instead prove two conditional statements.\n\n$$\nP\\Leftrightarrow Q \\equiv (P\\Rightarrow Q) \\land (Q\\Rightarrow P)\n$$\n\nSo, we start our proof as follows:\n\nWe need to prove the following:\n\n$$\n(x+y)^2 =x^2+y^2 \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad \\big(x=0 \\quad \\lor \\quad y=0 \\big)\n$$\n\nThis is the same as proving two implications.\n\nFirst, we consider the \"left to right\" implication.\n\n**Case $\\Rightarrow{:}$** We need to show that\n\n$$\n(x+y)^2 =x^2+y^2 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\big(x=0 \\quad \\lor \\quad y=0 \\big)\n$$\n\nBy expanding the left-hand side and simplifying, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 2xy + y^{2} &= x^{2} + y^{2} \\\\\n2xy &= 0 \\\\\nx \\cdot y &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the left-hand side equals zero if $x=0$ or $y=0$.\n\nNext, we consider the \"right to left\" implication.\n\n> **Case $\\Leftarrow{:}$** We need to show that\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\big(x=0 \\quad \\lor \\quad y=0 \\big) \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad (x+y)^2= x^2+y^2.\n$$\n\nHere, consider the two cases separately.\n\n> We have two subcases:\n> **Subcase 1.** Let $x=0$. Then, substituting this into our equation, we obtain\n> $$\n\\displaystyle (x + y)^{2}|= x^{2} + y^{2}; (0 + y)^{2}|= 0^{2} + y^{2}; y^{2}|= y^{2},\n$$\n> which is always true.\n\nAnd the second case.\n\n> **Subcase 2.** Let $y=0$. Then, substituting this into our equation, we obtain\n> $$\n\\displaystyle (x + y)^{2}|= x^{2} + y^{2}; (x + 0)^{2}|= x^{2} + 0^{2}; x^{2}|= x^{2},\n$$\n> which is always true.\n> Hence, if $x=0$ or $y=0$ then $(x+y)^2=x^2+y^2$.\n\nFinally, we write down the conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, $(x+y)^2=x^2+y^2$ if and only if $x=0$ or $y=0$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186203731 1074 "To show that $A\\subseteq B$, we need to show that every element of $A$ is also an element of $B$.\n\nSo, we begin our proof as follows.\n\n> To prove that $A \\subseteq B$, we take an arbitrary element of $A$ and show that its is an element of $B$.\n\nNow, we denote an arbitrary element of $A$ by $x$ and use the definition of $A$.\n\n> So, let $x \\in A$. Then, according to the definition of $A$, there exists $m \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ such that\n> $\\begin{aligned}x-3 & =4m \\\\ x & =3+4m.\\end{aligned}$.\n\nThe key to unlocking the proof is to rewrite $x$ to match the definition of $B$. In other words, we need to write\n\n$$\nx = 1+2n\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\n> Note that\n> $\\begin{aligned}x & =3+4m \\\\  & =1+2+4m \\\\  & =1+2(1+2m)\\end{aligned}$\n> Denote $n=1+2m$. Since $m$ is an integer, $n$ is also an integer. Thus,\n> $x=1+2n\\qquad\\Leftrightarrow \\qquad x\\equiv 1\\:(\\textrm{mod} 2)$.\n> Therefore, by the definition of $B$, we have $x \\in B$.\n\nSo, we have shown that $x\\in B$, and we can state our conclusion.\n\n> So, we conclude that $A \\subseteq B$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186203731 1074 "To show that $A\\subseteq B$, we need to show that every element of $A$ is also an element of $B$.\n\nSo, we begin our proof as follows.\n\n> To prove that $A \\subseteq B$, we take an arbitrary element of $A$ and show that its is an element of $B$.\n\nNow, we denote an arbitrary element of $A$ by $x$ and use the definition of $A$.\n\n> So, let $x \\in A$. Then, according to the definition of $A$, there exists $m \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ such that\n> $x - 3|= 4m; x|= 3 + 4m$.\n\nThe key to unlocking the proof is to rewrite $x$ to match the definition of $B$. In other words, we need to write\n\n$$\nx = 1+2n\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\n> Note that\n> $x|= 3 + 4m; = 1 + 2 + 4m; = 1 + 2(1 + 2m)$\n> Denote $n=1+2m$. Since $m$ is an integer, $n$ is also an integer. Thus,\n> $x=1+2n\\qquad\\Leftrightarrow \\qquad x\\equiv 1\\:(\\textrm{mod} 2)$.\n> Therefore, by the definition of $B$, we have $x \\in B$.\n\nSo, we have shown that $x\\in B$, and we can state our conclusion.\n\n> So, we conclude that $A \\subseteq B$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186203761 1074 "The diagram corresponding to our automaton is shown below.\n\n![](images/0c/0c79f07e5e39ef11f5366fadd2ad37478a925c2296b498f647553c099e4572de.png)\n\nLet's apply the extended transition function to our strings.\n\n- Consider the string $0001{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\delta (p,0001) & =\\delta (\\delta (p,0),001) \\\\  & =\\delta (q,001) \\\\  & =\\delta (\\delta (q,0),01) \\\\  & =\\delta (r,01) \\\\  & =\\delta (\\delta (r,0),1) \\\\  & =\\delta (p,1) \\\\  & =p\\end{aligned}\n$$![](images/5b/5bdbfd194560c41eb304d9bf489623ab0f82e1bb28b24cac9573f599afe017b2.png)\nThe process is terminated at the state $p$. Since $p$ is not a final (accepting) state, the string $0001$ is rejected.\n- Consider the string $1010{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\delta (p,1010) & =\\delta (\\delta (p,1),010) \\\\  & =\\delta (p,010) \\\\  & =\\delta (\\delta (p,0),10) \\\\  & =\\delta (q,10) \\\\  & =\\delta (\\delta (q,1),0) \\\\  & =\\delta (q,0) \\\\  & =r\\end{aligned}\n$$![](images/7d/7d75e94dd273fc37034baefc52234aad4056f528ec5824ddf850e729d44931c0.png)\nThe process is terminated at the state $r$. Since $r$ is a final (accepting) state, the string $1010$ is accepted.\n- Consider the string $0011{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\delta (p,0011) & =\\delta (\\delta (p,0),011) \\\\  & =\\delta (q,011) \\\\  & =\\delta (\\delta (q,0),11) \\\\  & =\\delta (r,11) \\\\  & =\\delta (\\delta (r,1),1) \\\\  & =\\delta (q,1) \\\\  & =q\\end{aligned}\n$$![](images/a4/a497ef18d08c2cbefcf7dc7164d3073f2b64851c1124fb4f22e573804570d6d2.png)\nThe process is terminated at the state $q$. Since $q$ is a final (accepting) state, the string $0011$ is accepted.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186203761 1074 "The diagram corresponding to our automaton is shown below.\n\n![](images/0c/0c79f07e5e39ef11f5366fadd2ad37478a925c2296b498f647553c099e4572de.png)\n\nLet's apply the extended transition function to our strings.\n\n- Consider the string $0001{:}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle δ(p, 0001)|= δ(δ(p, 0), 001); = δ(q, 001); = δ(δ(q, 0), 01); = δ(r, 01); = δ(δ(r, 0), 1); = δ(p, 1); = p\n$$![](images/5b/5bdbfd194560c41eb304d9bf489623ab0f82e1bb28b24cac9573f599afe017b2.png)\nThe process is terminated at the state $p$. Since $p$ is not a final (accepting) state, the string $0001$ is rejected.\n- Consider the string $1010{:}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle δ(p, 1010)|= δ(δ(p, 1), 010); = δ(p, 010); = δ(δ(p, 0), 10); = δ(q, 10); = δ(δ(q, 1), 0); = δ(q, 0); = r\n$$![](images/7d/7d75e94dd273fc37034baefc52234aad4056f528ec5824ddf850e729d44931c0.png)\nThe process is terminated at the state $r$. Since $r$ is a final (accepting) state, the string $1010$ is accepted.\n- Consider the string $0011{:}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle δ(p, 0011)|= δ(δ(p, 0), 011); = δ(q, 011); = δ(δ(q, 0), 11); = δ(r, 11); = δ(δ(r, 1), 1); = δ(q, 1); = q\n$$![](images/a4/a497ef18d08c2cbefcf7dc7164d3073f2b64851c1124fb4f22e573804570d6d2.png)\nThe process is terminated at the state $q$. Since $q$ is a final (accepting) state, the string $0011$ is accepted.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186203766 1074 "Recall that the one-factor ANOVA table for comparing $K$ groups in a sample of size $n$ gives the following data.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Source</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,\\textrm{SS}\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,df\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,\\textrm{MS}\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,F\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Between Groups</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{SSB}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$K-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{MSB}=\\dfrac{\\text{SSB}}{K-1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{\\text{MSB}}{\\text{MSW}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Within Groups</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{SSW}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$n-K$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{MSW}=\\dfrac{\\text{SSW}}{n-K}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Total</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{SST}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$n-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{SSW}$ is the sum of squares within groups,\n- $\\text{SSB}$ is the sum of squares between groups,\n- $\\text{SST}$ is the total sum of squares, and $\\text{SST}=\\text{SSW}+\\text{SSB}$.\n\nLet's go through the missing entries in the given table.\n\n- $\\text{SSB}$ can be computed using the above relation:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SSB} & =\\text{SST}-\\text{SSW} \\\\  & =7.6-4.15 \\\\  & =3.45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The degrees of freedom of $\\text{SSB}$ are\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d{f}_{B} & =K-1 \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The degrees of freedom of $\\text{SSW}$ are\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d{f}_{W} & =n-K \\\\  & =26-3 \\\\  & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The mean sum of squares between groups is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{MSB} & =\\frac{\\text{SSB}}{K-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3.45}{3-1} \\\\  & =1.725 \\\\  & \\approx 1.73.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The mean sum of squares within groups is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{MSW} & =\\frac{\\text{SSW}}{n-K} \\\\  & =\\frac{4.15}{26-3} \\\\  & =0.180\\hspace{0.167em}434… \\\\  & \\approx 0.18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Finally, the $F$ ratio is\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\text{MSB}}{\\text{MSW}} = \\dfrac{1.725}{0.180\\,434\\ldots} \\approx 9.56.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as follows.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Source</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,\\textrm{SS}\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,df\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,\\textrm{MS}\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,F\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Between</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}3.45}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1.73}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}9.56}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Within</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4.15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}23}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.18}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Total</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7.6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186203766 1074 "Recall that the one-factor ANOVA table for comparing $K$ groups in a sample of size $n$ gives the following data.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Source</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,\\textrm{SS}\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,df\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,\\textrm{MS}\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,F\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Between Groups</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{SSB}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$K-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{MSB}=\\dfrac{\\text{SSB}}{K-1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{\\text{MSB}}{\\text{MSW}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Within Groups</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{SSW}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$n-K$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{MSW}=\\dfrac{\\text{SSW}}{n-K}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Total</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{SST}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$n-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{SSW}$ is the sum of squares within groups,\n- $\\text{SSB}$ is the sum of squares between groups,\n- $\\text{SST}$ is the total sum of squares, and $\\text{SST}=\\text{SSW}+\\text{SSB}$.\n\nLet's go through the missing entries in the given table.\n\n- $\\text{SSB}$ can be computed using the above relation:\n$$\n\\displaystyle SSB|= SST - SSW; = 7.6 - 4.15; = 3.45\n$$\n- The degrees of freedom of $\\text{SSB}$ are\n$$\n\\displaystyle df_{B}|= K - 1; = 3 - 1; = 2.\n$$\n- The degrees of freedom of $\\text{SSW}$ are\n$$\n\\displaystyle df_{W}|= n - K; = 26 - 3; = 23.\n$$\n- The mean sum of squares between groups is\n$$\n\\displaystyle MSB|= \\frac{SSB}{K - 1}; = \\frac{3.45}{3 - 1}; = 1.725; \\approx 1.73.\n$$\n- The mean sum of squares within groups is\n$$\n\\displaystyle MSW|= \\frac{SSW}{n - K}; = \\frac{4.15}{26 - 3}; = 0.180434…; \\approx 0.18.\n$$\n- Finally, the $F$ ratio is\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\text{MSB}}{\\text{MSW}} = \\dfrac{1.725}{0.180\\,434\\ldots} \\approx 9.56.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as follows.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Source</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,\\textrm{SS}\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,df\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,\\textrm{MS}\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,F\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Between</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}3.45}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1.73}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}9.56}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Within</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4.15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}23}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.18}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Total</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7.6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186203775 1074 "![](images/de/def8939d5b6a390496bbf83b2b5d595980c27189c4cbfea3af1e492dba4688b9.png)\n\nA sketch of the given information is shown above, and the correct proof is below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$r \\perp s$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$m\\angle a= 90^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Definition of perpendicular lines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$\\angle a$ and $\\angle b$ are supplementary</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Linear pair postulate}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$m\\angle a+ m\\angle b=180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Definition of supplementary angles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$90^\\circ + m\\angle b = 180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Substitution</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$m\\angle b = 90^\\circ$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Addition principle}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">7</td>\n<td>$90^\\circ = m\\angle b$</td>\n<td>Symmetric property of equality</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">8</td>\n<td>$m\\angle a = m\\angle b$</td>\n<td>Transitive property of equality</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">9</td>\n<td>$\\angle a \\cong \\angle b$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent angles}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's examine the rows of the table with missing parts in turn.\n\n- Consider row 3. Notice that $\\angle a$ and $\\angle b$ form a linear pair. Therefore, by the linear pair postulate, these angles are supplementary.\n- Consider row 6. From row 5, we have that $90^\\circ + m\\angle b = 180^\\circ$. By the addition principle, we can subtract $90^\\circ$ from both sides of this equation:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{90}^{∘}+m\\mathrm{∠}b & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {90}^{∘}+m\\mathrm{∠}b-{90}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘}-{90}^{∘} \\\\ \\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{90}^{∘}}+m\\mathrm{∠}b-\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{90}^{∘}} & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}b & ={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Consider row 9. From row 8, $\\angle a$ and $\\angle b$ have the same measures. Therefore, by definition of congruent angles, these angles are congruent." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186203775 1074 "![](images/de/def8939d5b6a390496bbf83b2b5d595980c27189c4cbfea3af1e492dba4688b9.png)\n\nA sketch of the given information is shown above, and the correct proof is below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$r \\perp s$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$m\\angle a= 90^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Definition of perpendicular lines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$\\angle a$ and $\\angle b$ are supplementary</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Linear pair postulate}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$m\\angle a+ m\\angle b=180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Definition of supplementary angles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$90^\\circ + m\\angle b = 180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Substitution</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$m\\angle b = 90^\\circ$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Addition principle}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">7</td>\n<td>$90^\\circ = m\\angle b$</td>\n<td>Symmetric property of equality</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">8</td>\n<td>$m\\angle a = m\\angle b$</td>\n<td>Transitive property of equality</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">9</td>\n<td>$\\angle a \\cong \\angle b$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent angles}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's examine the rows of the table with missing parts in turn.\n\n- Consider row 3. Notice that $\\angle a$ and $\\angle b$ form a linear pair. Therefore, by the linear pair postulate, these angles are supplementary.\n- Consider row 6. From row 5, we have that $90^\\circ + m\\angle b = 180^\\circ$. By the addition principle, we can subtract $90^\\circ$ from both sides of this equation:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 90^{∘} + m∠b|= 180^{∘}; 90^{∘} + m∠b - 90^{∘}|= 180^{∘} - 90^{∘}; 90^{∘} + m∠b - 90^{∘}|= 90^{∘}; m∠b|= 90^{∘}\n$$\n- Consider row 9. From row 8, $\\angle a$ and $\\angle b$ have the same measures. Therefore, by definition of congruent angles, these angles are congruent." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186203778 1074 "The correct proof is below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$m\\angle DCB = 90^\\circ$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Given}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$\\angle DCB$ and $\\angle DCA$ are supplementary</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Linear pair postulate}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$m\\angle DCB +m\\angle DCA = 180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of supplementary angles}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$m\\angle DCA =180^\\circ - m\\angle DCB$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Addition principle}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$m\\angle DCA = 90^\\circ$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Substitution}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$m\\angle DCB = m\\angle DCA$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Transitive property of equality}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">7</td>\n<td>$\\angle DCB \\cong \\angle DCA$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent angles}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">8</td>\n<td>$\\overline{AC} \\cong \\overline{CB}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of perpendicular bisector}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">9</td>\n<td>$\\overline{CD} \\cong \\overline{CD}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Reflexive property of congruence}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">10</td>\n<td>$\\triangle{DCB} \\cong \\triangle{DCA}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{SAS criterion}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">11</td>\n<td>$\\overline{DB} \\cong \\overline{DA}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{CPCTC}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's examine each row in turn.\n\n1. We're given that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$. Then, by the definition of perpendicular lines, the angles formed by the intersection of $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ and $\\overline{AB}$ have a measure of $90^\\circ$. So, we have that $m\\angle{DCB} = 90^\\circ$.\n2. Notice that $\\angle{DCB}$ and $\\angle{DCA}$ form a linear pair. Therefore, these angles are supplementary by the linear pair postulate.\n3. From row 2, $\\angle{DCB}$ and $\\angle{DCA}$ are supplementary. Therefore, $m\\angle DCB +m\\angle DCA = 180^\\circ$ by the definition of supplementary angles.\n4. From row 3, $m\\angle DCB +m\\angle DCA = 180^\\circ$. By the addition principle, we can subtract $m\\angle{DCB}$ from both sides of this equation:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\\mathrm{∠}DCB+m\\mathrm{∠}DCA & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}DCB+m\\mathrm{∠}DCA-m\\mathrm{∠}DCB & ={180}^{∘}-m\\mathrm{∠}DCB \\\\ \\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{m\\mathrm{∠}DCB}+m\\mathrm{∠}DCA-\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{m\\mathrm{∠}DCB} & ={180}^{∘}-m\\mathrm{∠}DCB \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}DCA & ={180}^{∘}-m\\mathrm{∠}DCB\\end{aligned}\n$$\n5. From rows 1 and 4, $m\\angle DCB = 90^\\circ$ and $m\\angle DCA =180^\\circ - m\\angle DCB$. Therefore, by substituting the first into the second,\n$$\n\\displaystyle m\\angle DCA\n\n= 180^\\circ - 90^\\circ\n= 90^\\circ.\n$$\n6. From rows 1 and 5, we have $m\\angle DCB = 90^\\circ = m\\angle DCA$. Therefore, by the transitive property of equality,\n$m\\angle{DCB} = m\\angle{DCA}$.\n7. From row 6, $\\angle{DCB}$ and $m\\angle{DCA}$ have the same measure. Therefore, these angles are congruent by the definition of congruent angles.\n8. From the given information, $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$ and $C$ lies on $\\overline{AB}$. By the definition of a perpendicular bisector, we have\n$\\overline{AC} \\cong \\overline{CB}$.\n9. The reflexive property of congruence states that every segment is congruent to itself. Then, $\\overline{CD} \\cong \\overline{CD}$ by the reflexive property of congruence.\n10. From rows 8, 7, and 9, we have\n$\\overline{AC} \\cong \\overline{CB}, \\qquad \\angle DCB \\cong \\angle DCA, \\qquad \\overline{CD} \\cong \\overline{CD}$.\nTherefore, by the SAS criterion, $\\triangle{DCB} \\cong \\triangle{DCA}$.\n11. From row 10, $\\triangle{{\\color{red}D}C{\\color{red}B}} \\cong \\triangle{{\\color{red}D}C{\\color{red}A}}$. Then, $\\overline{DB} \\cong \\overline{DA}$ by CPCTC." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186203778 1074 "The correct proof is below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$m\\angle DCB = 90^\\circ$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Given}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$\\angle DCB$ and $\\angle DCA$ are supplementary</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Linear pair postulate}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$m\\angle DCB +m\\angle DCA = 180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of supplementary angles}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$m\\angle DCA =180^\\circ - m\\angle DCB$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Addition principle}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$m\\angle DCA = 90^\\circ$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Substitution}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$m\\angle DCB = m\\angle DCA$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Transitive property of equality}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">7</td>\n<td>$\\angle DCB \\cong \\angle DCA$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent angles}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">8</td>\n<td>$\\overline{AC} \\cong \\overline{CB}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of perpendicular bisector}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">9</td>\n<td>$\\overline{CD} \\cong \\overline{CD}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Reflexive property of congruence}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">10</td>\n<td>$\\triangle{DCB} \\cong \\triangle{DCA}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{SAS criterion}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">11</td>\n<td>$\\overline{DB} \\cong \\overline{DA}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{CPCTC}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's examine each row in turn.\n\n1. We're given that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$. Then, by the definition of perpendicular lines, the angles formed by the intersection of $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ and $\\overline{AB}$ have a measure of $90^\\circ$. So, we have that $m\\angle{DCB} = 90^\\circ$.\n2. Notice that $\\angle{DCB}$ and $\\angle{DCA}$ form a linear pair. Therefore, these angles are supplementary by the linear pair postulate.\n3. From row 2, $\\angle{DCB}$ and $\\angle{DCA}$ are supplementary. Therefore, $m\\angle DCB +m\\angle DCA = 180^\\circ$ by the definition of supplementary angles.\n4. From row 3, $m\\angle DCB +m\\angle DCA = 180^\\circ$. By the addition principle, we can subtract $m\\angle{DCB}$ from both sides of this equation:\n$$\n\\displaystyle m∠DCB + m∠DCA|= 180^{∘}; m∠DCB + m∠DCA - m∠DCB|= 180^{∘} - m∠DCB; m∠DCB + m∠DCA - m∠DCB|= 180^{∘} - m∠DCB; m∠DCA|= 180^{∘} - m∠DCB\n$$\n5. From rows 1 and 4, $m\\angle DCB = 90^\\circ$ and $m\\angle DCA =180^\\circ - m\\angle DCB$. Therefore, by substituting the first into the second,\n$$\n\\displaystyle m\\angle DCA\n\n= 180^\\circ - 90^\\circ\n= 90^\\circ.\n$$\n6. From rows 1 and 5, we have $m\\angle DCB = 90^\\circ = m\\angle DCA$. Therefore, by the transitive property of equality,\n$m\\angle{DCB} = m\\angle{DCA}$.\n7. From row 6, $\\angle{DCB}$ and $m\\angle{DCA}$ have the same measure. Therefore, these angles are congruent by the definition of congruent angles.\n8. From the given information, $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$ and $C$ lies on $\\overline{AB}$. By the definition of a perpendicular bisector, we have\n$\\overline{AC} \\cong \\overline{CB}$.\n9. The reflexive property of congruence states that every segment is congruent to itself. Then, $\\overline{CD} \\cong \\overline{CD}$ by the reflexive property of congruence.\n10. From rows 8, 7, and 9, we have\n$\\overline{AC} \\cong \\overline{CB}, \\qquad \\angle DCB \\cong \\angle DCA, \\qquad \\overline{CD} \\cong \\overline{CD}$.\nTherefore, by the SAS criterion, $\\triangle{DCB} \\cong \\triangle{DCA}$.\n11. From row 10, $\\triangle{{\\color{red}D}C{\\color{red}B}} \\cong \\triangle{{\\color{red}D}C{\\color{red}A}}$. Then, $\\overline{DB} \\cong \\overline{DA}$ by CPCTC." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186203781 1074 "The correct proof is shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$\\angle CHF$ and $\\angle FHD$ are supplementary</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Linear pair postulate}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$m\\angle CHF + m\\angle FHD = 180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Definition of supplementary angles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$m\\angle CHF = 180^\\circ - m\\angle FHD$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Addition principle}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$\\angle EGB$ and $\\angle BGH$ are supplementary</td>\n<td>Linear pair postulate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$m\\angle EGB + m\\angle BGH = 180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Definition of supplementary angles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$\\angle EGB \\cong \\angle CHF$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">7</td>\n<td>$m\\angle EGB = 180^\\circ - m\\angle FHD$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent angles}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">8</td>\n<td>$(180^\\circ - m\\angle FHD) + m\\angle BGH = 180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Substitution</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">9</td>\n<td>$m\\angle BGH = m\\angle FHD$</td>\n<td>Addition principle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">10</td>\n<td>$\\angle BGH \\cong \\angle FHD$</td>\n<td>Definition of congruent angles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">11</td>\n<td>$\\overleftrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overleftrightarrow{CD}$ are parallel</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Converse corresponding angles postulate}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's examine the rows of the table with missing parts in turn.\n\n- Consider row 1. Notice from the diagram that $\\angle CHF$ and $\\angle FHD$ form a linear pair. The linear pair postulate states that such angles must be supplementary. Therefore, $\\angle CHF$ and $\\angle FHD$ are supplementary angles.\n- Consider row 3. From row 2, we have $m\\angle CHF + m\\angle FHD = 180^\\circ$. By the addition principle, we can subtract $m\\angle FHD$ from both sides\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\\mathrm{∠}CHF+m\\mathrm{∠}FHD & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}CHF+m\\mathrm{∠}FHD-m\\mathrm{∠}FHD & ={180}^{∘}-m\\mathrm{∠}FHD \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}CHF+\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{m\\mathrm{∠}FHD}-\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{m\\mathrm{∠}FHD} & ={180}^{∘}-m\\mathrm{∠}FHD \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}CHF & ={180}^{∘}-m\\mathrm{∠}FHD.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Consider row 7. From row 3, we have that $m\\angle CHF = 180^\\circ - m\\angle FHD$, and from row 6, we have that $\\angle EGB \\cong \\angle CHF$. Therefore, by the definition of congruent angles, these angles have equal measure:\n$m \\angle EGB= 180^\\circ - m\\angle FHD$\n- Consider row 11. From row 10, we have that $\\angle BGH\\cong \\angle FHD$. By the converse corresponding angles postulate, if two lines and a transversal form corresponding angles that are congruent, then the lines are parallel. Notice that $\\angle BGH$ and $\\angle FHD$ are corresponding angles formed by the lines $\\overleftrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overleftrightarrow{CD}$ with the transversal $\\overleftrightarrow{EF}$. Therefore, $\\overleftrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overleftrightarrow{CD}$ are parallel." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186203781 1074 "The correct proof is shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$\\angle CHF$ and $\\angle FHD$ are supplementary</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Linear pair postulate}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$m\\angle CHF + m\\angle FHD = 180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Definition of supplementary angles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$m\\angle CHF = 180^\\circ - m\\angle FHD$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Addition principle}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$\\angle EGB$ and $\\angle BGH$ are supplementary</td>\n<td>Linear pair postulate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$m\\angle EGB + m\\angle BGH = 180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Definition of supplementary angles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$\\angle EGB \\cong \\angle CHF$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">7</td>\n<td>$m\\angle EGB = 180^\\circ - m\\angle FHD$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent angles}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">8</td>\n<td>$(180^\\circ - m\\angle FHD) + m\\angle BGH = 180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Substitution</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">9</td>\n<td>$m\\angle BGH = m\\angle FHD$</td>\n<td>Addition principle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">10</td>\n<td>$\\angle BGH \\cong \\angle FHD$</td>\n<td>Definition of congruent angles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">11</td>\n<td>$\\overleftrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overleftrightarrow{CD}$ are parallel</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Converse corresponding angles postulate}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's examine the rows of the table with missing parts in turn.\n\n- Consider row 1. Notice from the diagram that $\\angle CHF$ and $\\angle FHD$ form a linear pair. The linear pair postulate states that such angles must be supplementary. Therefore, $\\angle CHF$ and $\\angle FHD$ are supplementary angles.\n- Consider row 3. From row 2, we have $m\\angle CHF + m\\angle FHD = 180^\\circ$. By the addition principle, we can subtract $m\\angle FHD$ from both sides\n$$\n\\displaystyle m∠CHF + m∠FHD|= 180^{∘}; m∠CHF + m∠FHD - m∠FHD|= 180^{∘} - m∠FHD; m∠CHF + m∠FHD - m∠FHD|= 180^{∘} - m∠FHD; m∠CHF|= 180^{∘} - m∠FHD.\n$$\n- Consider row 7. From row 3, we have that $m\\angle CHF = 180^\\circ - m\\angle FHD$, and from row 6, we have that $\\angle EGB \\cong \\angle CHF$. Therefore, by the definition of congruent angles, these angles have equal measure:\n$m \\angle EGB= 180^\\circ - m\\angle FHD$\n- Consider row 11. From row 10, we have that $\\angle BGH\\cong \\angle FHD$. By the converse corresponding angles postulate, if two lines and a transversal form corresponding angles that are congruent, then the lines are parallel. Notice that $\\angle BGH$ and $\\angle FHD$ are corresponding angles formed by the lines $\\overleftrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overleftrightarrow{CD}$ with the transversal $\\overleftrightarrow{EF}$. Therefore, $\\overleftrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overleftrightarrow{CD}$ are parallel." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186203789 1074 "First, we find the coordinates of the point $J$ in terms of the coordinates of $S$ and $T{:}$\n\n> Since $\\overline{RJ}$ is a median, we have that $J$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{ST}$. So, if $(x_J,y_J)$ are the coordinates of $J$, by the\n> midpoint formula, we have\n> $$\n\\displaystyle x_{J} = \\frac{x_{S} + x_{T}}{2}, y_{J} = \\frac{y_{S} + y_{T}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we write the coordinates of the centroid using the formula for partitioning a line segment in a given ratio:\n\n> By the centroid ratio theorem, the point $G$ splits the median $\\overline{RJ}$ in the ratio $2:1$, starting from the point $R$. Now, by partitioning the segment $\\overline{RJ}$ in a given ratio, we obtain that\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{G} & =\\frac{{x}_{R}+2{x}_{J}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{R}+2\\cdot \\frac{{x}_{S}+{x}_{T}}{2}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{R}+{x}_{S}+{x}_{T}}{3}, \\\\ {y}_{G} & =\\frac{{y}_{R}+2{y}_{J}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}_{R}+2\\cdot \\frac{{y}_{S}+{y}_{T}}{2}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}_{R}+{y}_{S}+{y}_{T}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186203789 1074 "First, we find the coordinates of the point $J$ in terms of the coordinates of $S$ and $T{:}$\n\n> Since $\\overline{RJ}$ is a median, we have that $J$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{ST}$. So, if $(x_J,y_J)$ are the coordinates of $J$, by the\n> midpoint formula, we have\n> $$\n\\displaystyle x_{J} = \\frac{x_{S} + x_{T}}{2}, y_{J} = \\frac{y_{S} + y_{T}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we write the coordinates of the centroid using the formula for partitioning a line segment in a given ratio:\n\n> By the centroid ratio theorem, the point $G$ splits the median $\\overline{RJ}$ in the ratio $2:1$, starting from the point $R$. Now, by partitioning the segment $\\overline{RJ}$ in a given ratio, we obtain that\n> $$\n\\displaystyle x_{G}|= \\frac{x_{R} + 2x_{J}}{3}; = \\frac{x_{R} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{x_{S} + x_{T}}{2}}{3}; = \\frac{x_{R} + x_{S} + x_{T}}{3},; y_{G}|= \\frac{y_{R} + 2y_{J}}{3}; = \\frac{y_{R} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{y_{S} + y_{T}}{2}}{3}; = \\frac{y_{R} + y_{S} + y_{T}}{3}.\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186203791 1074 "The correct proof is shown below.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\dfrac{PA}{PR} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ | Given |\n| 2 | $B$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{PQ}$ | Given |\n| 3 | $PB = \\dfrac12{PQ}$ | Definition of a midpoint |\n| 4 | $\\dfrac{PB}{PQ} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Multiplication principle}}$ |\n| 5 | $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{PA}{PR} = \\dfrac{PB}{PQ}}\n$$ | Transitive property of equality |\n| 6 | $\\angle{RPQ} \\cong \\angle{APB}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Reflexive property of congruence}}$ |\n| 7 | $\\triangle{RQP} \\sim \\triangle{ABP}$ | SAS similarity criterion |\n| 8 | $\\angle PBA \\cong \\angle Q$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Corresponding angles in similar triangles}}$ |\n| 9 | $\\overline{BA} \\parallel \\overline{RQ}$ | Converse corresponding angles postulate |\n\nLet's examine the rows of the table with missing parts in turn.\n\n- Consider row 4. By the multiplication principle, we can divide the equation we obtained in row 3 by $PQ$, as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PB & =\\frac{1}{2}PQ \\\\ \\frac{PB}{PQ} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{PQ}{PQ} \\\\ \\frac{PB}{PQ} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{PQ}}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{PQ}} \\\\ \\frac{PB}{PQ} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Consider row 5. From rows 1 and 4, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{PA}{PR}}} = \\dfrac 12 \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad \\dfrac 12 = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{PB}{PQ}}}.\n$$\nTherefore, by the transitive property of equality, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{PA}{PR}}} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{PB}{PQ}}}.\n$$\n- Consider row 6. Notice that $\\angle RPQ$ and $\\angle APB$ represent the same angle. Therefore, by the reflexive property of congruence, we have $\\angle{RPQ} \\cong \\angle{APB}$.\n- Consider row 8. From row 7, we have\n$\\triangle{RQP} \\sim \\triangle{ABP}$.\nTherefore, we have that $\\angle PBA \\cong \\angle Q$ since they're corresponding angles in similar triangles." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186203791 1074 "The correct proof is shown below.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\dfrac{PA}{PR} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ | Given |\n| 2 | $B$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{PQ}$ | Given |\n| 3 | $PB = \\dfrac12{PQ}$ | Definition of a midpoint |\n| 4 | $\\dfrac{PB}{PQ} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Multiplication principle}}$ |\n| 5 | $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{PA}{PR} = \\dfrac{PB}{PQ}}\n$$ | Transitive property of equality |\n| 6 | $\\angle{RPQ} \\cong \\angle{APB}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Reflexive property of congruence}}$ |\n| 7 | $\\triangle{RQP} \\sim \\triangle{ABP}$ | SAS similarity criterion |\n| 8 | $\\angle PBA \\cong \\angle Q$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Corresponding angles in similar triangles}}$ |\n| 9 | $\\overline{BA} \\parallel \\overline{RQ}$ | Converse corresponding angles postulate |\n\nLet's examine the rows of the table with missing parts in turn.\n\n- Consider row 4. By the multiplication principle, we can divide the equation we obtained in row 3 by $PQ$, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle PB|= \\frac{1}{2}PQ; \\frac{PB}{PQ}|= \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{PQ}{PQ}; \\frac{PB}{PQ}|= \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{PQ}{PQ}; \\frac{PB}{PQ}|= \\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n- Consider row 5. From rows 1 and 4, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{PA}{PR}}} = \\dfrac 12 \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad \\dfrac 12 = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{PB}{PQ}}}.\n$$\nTherefore, by the transitive property of equality, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{PA}{PR}}} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{PB}{PQ}}}.\n$$\n- Consider row 6. Notice that $\\angle RPQ$ and $\\angle APB$ represent the same angle. Therefore, by the reflexive property of congruence, we have $\\angle{RPQ} \\cong \\angle{APB}$.\n- Consider row 8. From row 7, we have\n$\\triangle{RQP} \\sim \\triangle{ABP}$.\nTherefore, we have that $\\angle PBA \\cong \\angle Q$ since they're corresponding angles in similar triangles." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186203816 1074 "We have a sample with the following information:\n\n- The sample size is $n = 100$\n- The number of groups is $K = 8$\n\nLet's calculate the degrees of freedom for each of the sums of squares:\n\n- For the total sum of squares $(\\textrm{SST})$, the number of degrees of freedom is given by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d{f}_{T} & =n-1 \\\\  & =100-1 \\\\  & ={\\color{blue}{99}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the total sum of squares between the groups $(\\textrm{SSB})$, the number of degrees of freedom is given by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d{f}_{B} & =K-1 \\\\  & =8-1 \\\\  & ={\\color{blue}{7}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the total sum of squares within the groups $(\\textrm{SSW})$, the number of degrees of freedom is given by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d{f}_{W} & =n-K \\\\  & =100-8 \\\\  & ={\\color{blue}{92}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\ndf_T = df_B + df_W\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186203816 1074 "We have a sample with the following information:\n\n- The sample size is $n = 100$\n- The number of groups is $K = 8$\n\nLet's calculate the degrees of freedom for each of the sums of squares:\n\n- For the total sum of squares $(\\textrm{SST})$, the number of degrees of freedom is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle df_{T}|= n - 1; = 100 - 1; = 99.\n$$\n- For the total sum of squares between the groups $(\\textrm{SSB})$, the number of degrees of freedom is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle df_{B}|= K - 1; = 8 - 1; = 7.\n$$\n- For the total sum of squares within the groups $(\\textrm{SSW})$, the number of degrees of freedom is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle df_{W}|= n - K; = 100 - 8; = 92.\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\ndf_T = df_B + df_W\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186203817 1074 "The sum of squares within groups $(\\textrm{SSW})$ and sum of squares between groups $(\\textrm{SSB})$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SSW} = \\sum_{k=1}^K \\sum_{j=1}^{n_k} \\left(x_{jk} - \\overline{x}_k\\right)^2, \\qquad \\text{SSB} = \\sum_{k=1}^K n_k \\left(\\overline{x}_k - \\overline{x}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $K$ is the number of groups,\n- $n_k$ is the number of individuals in the $k$th group,\n- $x_{jk}$ is the $j$th data point from the $k$th group,\n- $\\overline{x}_{k}$ is the sample mean of the $k$th group,\n- $\\overline{x}$ is the grand mean.\n\nNow, if $s_k^2$ is the unbiased estimate of the population variance $\\sigma^2$ obtained from the data in group $k$ only, then\n\n$$\ns_k^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n_k-1}\\sum_{j=1}^{n_k}\\left(x_{jk} - \\overline{x}_k\\right)^2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (n_k-1)s_k^2 = \\sum_{j=1}^{n_k}\\left(x_{jk} - \\overline{x}_k\\right)^2\n$$\n\nThis means we can express $\\text{SSW}$ in the following simplified form:\n\n$$\n\\text{SSW} = \\sum_{k=1}^K (n_k - 1)s_k^2\n$$\n\nMoreover, the total sum of squares is\n\n$$\n\\text{SST} = (n-1)s^2\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $n$ is the grand sample size,\n- $s^2$ is the grand (unbiased) estimate of the population variance $\\sigma^2$.\n\nIn our case, since there are $3$ groups, we have $K=3$, and we can compute SSW as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSSW &= (20 - 1)(3.2)^{2} + (24 - 1)(3.4)^{2} + (20 - 1)(3.8)^{2} \\\\\n&= 194.56 + 265.88 + 274.36 \\\\\n&= 734.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\nn = 20+24+20 = 64\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\text{SST} = (64-1)\\cdot (15)=945\n$$\n\nNow, let's now examine our statements:\n\n- Computing the given ratio, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\text{SSW}}{\\text{SST}} = \\dfrac{734.8}{945}\\approx \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}78\\%}.\n$$\n- We're interested in determining the between-group variability. First, recall that\n$\\textrm{SSW} + \\textrm{SSB} = \\textrm{SST}$\nand therefore\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\textrm{SSW}}{\\textrm{SST}} + \\dfrac{\\textrm{SSB}}{\\textrm{SST}} = 1.\n$$\nTherefore,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{SSB}}{\\text{SST}} & =1-\\frac{\\text{SSW}}{\\text{SST}} \\\\  & \\approx 100\\mathrm{\\%}-78\\mathrm{\\%} \\\\  & =22\\mathrm{\\%} \\\\  & <40\\mathrm{\\%}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince a low proportion of the variability is *between* groups, relatively high variability is attributable to differences *within* the group means. In other words, this suggests the between-group variability is $\\bbox[3pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{low}}$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186203817 1074 "The sum of squares within groups $(\\textrm{SSW})$ and sum of squares between groups $(\\textrm{SSB})$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SSW} = \\sum_{k=1}^K \\sum_{j=1}^{n