f the given points is on the line $k$.\n\n- The slope of the line segment with endpoints at $(1,-2)$ and $(3,-1)$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{-1-(-2)}{3-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & \\ne 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore, $(1,-2)$ does not lie on the line $k$.\n- The slope of the line segment with endpoints at $(5,1)$ and $(3,-1)$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{-1-1}{3-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{-2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore, $(5,1)$ lies on the line $k$.\n\n- The slope of the line segment with endpoints at $(0,-1)$ and $(3,-1)$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{-1-(-1)}{3-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{3} \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & \\ne 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore, $(0,-1)$ does not lie on the line $k$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$(5,1)$ only.\"" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186208340 1074 "We are given that the circle has its center at $(1,1)$ and that line $k$ is tangent to this circle at the point $(3,-1)$. Therefore, the segment connecting the center $(1,1)$ with the point of tangency $(3,-1)$ is a circle's radius.\n\nThe slope of a line (or line segment) passing through points $(x_1,y_1)$ and $(x_2, y_2)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1}\n$$\n\nThen, the slope of the radius with endpoints at $(1,1)$ and $(3,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{-1 - 1}{3 - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-2}{2} \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that a line is tangent to a circle if and only if it's perpendicular to the radius drawn to the point of tangency. Consequently, $k$ is perpendicular to the radius at the point $(3, -1)$. Hence, the slope of $k$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of this radius, that is, the slope of $k$ is $1$.\n\nSince the slope of line $k$ is the same between any two points on line $k$, a point lies on line $k$ if the slope of the line segment connecting the point and $(3,-1)$ is $1$. Taking this into account, we proceed to verify which of the given points is on the line $k$.\n\n- The slope of the line segment with endpoints at $(1,-2)$ and $(3,-1)$ is\n$$\n\\displaystyle m|= (-1 - (-2))/(3 - 1); = \\frac{1}{2}; \\ne 1.\n$$\nTherefore, $(1,-2)$ does not lie on the line $k$.\n- The slope of the line segment with endpoints at $(5,1)$ and $(3,-1)$ is\n$$\n\\displaystyle m|= \\frac{-1 - 1}{3 - 5}; = \\frac{-2}{-2}; = 1.\n$$\nTherefore, $(5,1)$ lies on the line $k$.\n\n- The slope of the line segment with endpoints at $(0,-1)$ and $(3,-1)$ is\n$$\n\\displaystyle m|= (-1 - (-1))/(3 - 0); = \\frac{0}{3}; = 0; \\ne 1.\n$$\nTherefore, $(0,-1)$ does not lie on the line $k$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$(5,1)$ only.\"" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186208361 1074 "First, note that we can inscribe our triangle within the rectangle shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/b6/b6fd666615d54950f20c7b5ca219de1e3a1867f3fb989370ef4d9ff1e7e9f328.png)\n\nWe have the rectangle, whose area we denote by $A$, and four triangles:\n\n- $\\triangle ABC$, whose area $A_{\\triangle}$ we're required to determine,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}$ and area $A_1$,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{BC}$ and area $A_2$, and\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$ and area $A_3$.\n\nRecall that the area of a figure consisting of several smaller non-overlapping parts is equal to the sum of the areas of those parts. So, we have\n\n$$\nA = A_{\\triangle} + A_1 + A_2 + A_3\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = A - A_1 - A_2 - A_3\n$$\n\nLet's now compute all the areas on the right-hand side of this equation.\n\n- For the area of the rectangle, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\text{width}\\cdot \\text{height} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\text{leg}}_{1}\\cdot {\\text{leg}}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{BC}$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\text{leg}}_{1}\\cdot {\\text{leg}}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\text{leg}}_{1}\\cdot {\\text{leg}}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = 20 - 2 - 6 - 2.5 = 9.5\n$$\n\nsquare units." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186208361 1074 "First, note that we can inscribe our triangle within the rectangle shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/b6/b6fd666615d54950f20c7b5ca219de1e3a1867f3fb989370ef4d9ff1e7e9f328.png)\n\nWe have the rectangle, whose area we denote by $A$, and four triangles:\n\n- $\\triangle ABC$, whose area $A_{\\triangle}$ we're required to determine,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}$ and area $A_1$,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{BC}$ and area $A_2$, and\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$ and area $A_3$.\n\nRecall that the area of a figure consisting of several smaller non-overlapping parts is equal to the sum of the areas of those parts. So, we have\n\n$$\nA = A_{\\triangle} + A_1 + A_2 + A_3\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = A - A_1 - A_2 - A_3\n$$\n\nLet's now compute all the areas on the right-hand side of this equation.\n\n- For the area of the rectangle, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle A|= width \\cdot height; = 5 \\cdot 4; = 20.\n$$\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle A_{1}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot leg_{1} \\cdot leg_{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 1 \\cdot 4; = 2.\n$$\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{BC}$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle A_{2}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot leg_{1} \\cdot leg_{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3; = 6.\n$$\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle A_{3}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot leg_{1} \\cdot leg_{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 5 \\cdot 1; = 2.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = 20 - 2 - 6 - 2.5 = 9.5\n$$\n\nsquare units." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186208391 1074 "First, notice that the absolute value terms contain the same expression. So, by combining these like terms, the equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3|4-a|-7|4-a| & =-60 \\\\ -4|4-a| & =-60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, dividing both sides by $-4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\mid 4 - a \\mid = 15\n$$\n\nNext, we consider both possible cases for the absolute value.\n\n- If $\\mid 4 - a \\mid = 4 - a$, then\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|4-a| & =15 \\\\ 4-a & =15 \\\\ -a & =11 \\\\ a & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- If $\\mid 4 - a \\mid = -(4 - a)$, then\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|4-a| & =15 \\\\ -(4-a) & =15 \\\\ -4+a & =15 \\\\ a & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the question asks for the positive solution, the correct answer is\n\n$$\na=\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{19}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186208391 1074 "First, notice that the absolute value terms contain the same expression. So, by combining these like terms, the equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3|4-a|-7|4-a| & =-60 \\\\ -4|4-a| & =-60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, dividing both sides by $-4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\mid 4 - a \\mid = 15\n$$\n\nNext, we consider both possible cases for the absolute value.\n\n- If $\\mid 4 - a \\mid = 4 - a$, then\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mid 4 - a \\mid |= 15; 4 - a|= 15; - a|= 11; a|=-11.\n$$\n- If $\\mid 4 - a \\mid = -(4 - a)$, then\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mid 4 - a \\mid |= 15; - (4 - a)|= 15; - 4 + a|= 15; a|= 19.\n$$\n\nSince the question asks for the positive solution, the correct answer is\n\n$$\na=\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{19}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186208392 1074 "To solve this equation, we consider both possible cases for the absolute value.\n\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle \\left|\\dfrac{3}{5}x - \\dfrac{11}{6}\\right| = \\dfrac{3}{5}x - \\dfrac{11}{6},\n$$ then\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{3}{5}x-\\frac{11}{6}| & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}x-\\frac{11}{6} & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}x & =\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{11}{6} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}x & =\\frac{14}{6}+\\frac{11}{6} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}x & =\\frac{25}{6} \\\\ x & =\\frac{25}{6}\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{125}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle \\left|\\dfrac{3}{5}x - \\dfrac{11}{6}\\right| = -\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}x - \\dfrac{11}{6}\\right),\n$$ then\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{3}{5}x-\\frac{11}{6}| & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -(\\frac{3}{5}x-\\frac{11}{6}) & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}x+\\frac{11}{6} & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}x & =\\frac{7}{3}-\\frac{11}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}x & =\\frac{14}{6}-\\frac{11}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}x & =\\frac{3}{6} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{6}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{5}{3}\\right) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{15}{18} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{125}{18} + \\left(-\\dfrac{5}{6}\\right) = \\dfrac{125}{18} - \\dfrac{15}{18} = \\dfrac{110}{18} = \\dfrac{55}{9}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186208392 1074 "To solve this equation, we consider both possible cases for the absolute value.\n\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle \\left|\\dfrac{3}{5}x - \\dfrac{11}{6}\\right| = \\dfrac{3}{5}x - \\dfrac{11}{6},\n$$ then\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mid \\frac{3}{5}x - \\frac{11}{6} \\mid |= \\frac{7}{3}; \\frac{3}{5}x - \\frac{11}{6}|= \\frac{7}{3}; \\frac{3}{5}x|= \\frac{7}{3} + \\frac{11}{6}; \\frac{3}{5}x|= \\frac{14}{6} + \\frac{11}{6}; \\frac{3}{5}x|= \\frac{25}{6}; x|= \\frac{25}{6} \\cdot \\frac{5}{3}; x|= \\frac{125}{18}.\n$$\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle \\left|\\dfrac{3}{5}x - \\dfrac{11}{6}\\right| = -\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}x - \