t 5; = 30.\n$$\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle A_{1}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot leg_{1} \\cdot leg_{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 4 \\cdot 4; = 8.\n$$\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{BC}$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle A_{2}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot leg_{1} \\cdot leg_{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 5; = 5.\n$$\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle A_{3}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot leg_{1} \\cdot leg_{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 \\cdot 1; = 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = 30 - 8 - 5 - 3 = 14\n$$\n\nsquare units." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186214229 1064 "Often in algebra, we’re so used to solving for a variable that we overlook faster, simpler paths to an answer. In many problems, the quantity we’re asked to find already contains a piece of information we’ve been given. Recognizing this can save time, reduce the possibility of errors, and sharpen our pattern-recognition skills.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the equation\n\n$$\n5y = 32\n$$\n\nand we want to determine the value of\n\n$$\n5y - 6\n$$\n\nWhat is the fastest way to do this? Let's compare two different methods.\n\n- **Method 1 (Slow)**The standard approach is to solve for $y$ using the first equation, and then substitute that value into the expression we want to evaluate.First, we solve for the variable $y{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y & =32 \\\\ \\frac{5y}{5} & =\\frac{32}{5} \\\\ y & =\\frac{32}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThen, we evaluate $5y-6{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y-6 & =5\\cdot \\left(\\frac{32}{5}\\right)-6 \\\\  & =32-6 \\\\  & =26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- **Method 2 (Fast)**Notice that the term $\\color{blue}5y$ appears in *both* the equation *and* the expression we wish to evaluate. So, there's no need to solve for $y$. Instead, we can substitute ${\\color{blue}{5y}} = {\\color{red}{32}}$ directly into the expression ${\\color{blue}{5y}} - 6$, and evaluate:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\color{blue}{5y}}-6 & =({\\color{red}{32}})-6 \\\\  & =26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186214229 1064 "Often in algebra, we’re so used to solving for a variable that we overlook faster, simpler paths to an answer. In many problems, the quantity we’re asked to find already contains a piece of information we’ve been given. Recognizing this can save time, reduce the possibility of errors, and sharpen our pattern-recognition skills.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the equation\n\n$$\n5y = 32\n$$\n\nand we want to determine the value of\n\n$$\n5y - 6\n$$\n\nWhat is the fastest way to do this? Let's compare two different methods.\n\n- **Method 1 (Slow)**The standard approach is to solve for $y$ using the first equation, and then substitute that value into the expression we want to evaluate.First, we solve for the variable $y{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle 5y|= 32; \\frac{5y}{5}|= \\frac{32}{5}; y|= \\frac{32}{5}\n$$\nThen, we evaluate $5y-6{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle 5y - 6|= 5 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{32}{5}\\right) - 6; = 32 - 6; = 26\n$$\n- **Method 2 (Fast)**Notice that the term $\\color{blue}5y$ appears in *both* the equation *and* the expression we wish to evaluate. So, there's no need to solve for $y$. Instead, we can substitute ${\\color{blue}{5y}} = {\\color{red}{32}}$ directly into the expression ${\\color{blue}{5y}} - 6$, and evaluate:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 5y - 6|= (32) - 6; = 26\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186214249 1064 "In a previous lesson, we saw how to create and apply a conversion formula to convert quantities with mixed units, such as converting from \"kilometers per hour\" to \"meters per second.\"\n\nIn a separate lesson, we saw how to convert between different units of area, such as converting from \"square meters\" to \"square centimeters\" and vice versa.\n\nBy 