 $0.3$.\n- The range depends only on the difference between the largest and the smallest values. Since both of these values are shifted by the same amount, the range remains unchanged.\n- The standard deviation measures the spread about the mean. Since adding the same constant to every value (and thus to the mean) does not change the deviations from the mean, the standard deviation is unchanged.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186216993 1074 "When a fixed constant is added to every data value, each statistic responds as follows:\n\n- For $29$ *different* values, there is exactly one middle value (the $15$th value) of $3.4$. Adding $0.3$ to every value shifts the median to $3.7$. So, the median increases by $0.3$.\n- The mean depends on every value, so adding the same number to all values will increase the mean by the same number. If we denote the $29$ different weights by $w_1, w_2, \\ldots, w_{29}$ and add $0.3$ to each, then the new mean is:\n$$\n\\displaystyle ((w_{1} + 0.3) + (w_{2} + 0.3) + ⋯ + (w_{29} + 0.3))/(29)|= (0.3 \\cdot 29 + (w_{1} + w_{2} + ⋯ + w_{29}))/(29); = 0.3 + \\frac{w_{1} + w_{2} + ⋯ + w_{29}}{29}; = 0.3 + 3.4; = 3.7\n$$\nSo, the mean also increases by $0.3$.\n- The range depends only on the difference between the largest and the smallest values. Since both of these values are shifted by the same amount, the range remains unchanged.\n- The standard deviation measures the spread about the mean. Since adding the same constant to every value (and thus to the mean) does not change the deviations from the mean, the standard deviation is unchanged.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186216996 1074 "From the description, our rectangle has the following measurements:\n\n- The length is $l = x$ centimeters.\n- Let's denote the width by $w$. We are told that the length is $7$ centimeters less than three times its width:\n$x = 3w - 7$\nSolving for $w$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3w-7 \\\\ x+7 & =3w \\\\ \\frac{x+7}{3} & =w \\\\ w & =\\frac{1}{3}(x+7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the rectangle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(x) &= wl \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(x + 7) \\cdot x \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}x(x + 7)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186216996 1074 "From the description, our rectangle has the following measurements:\n\n- The length is $l = x$ centimeters.\n- Let's denote the width by $w$. We are told that the length is $7$ centimeters less than three times its width:\n$x = 3w - 7$\nSolving for $w$, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle x|= 3w - 7; x + 7|= 3w; \\frac{x + 7}{3}|= w; w|= \\frac{1}{3}(x + 7)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the rectangle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(x) &= wl \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(x + 7) \\cdot x \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}x(x + 7)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186216997 1074 "From the description, our right rectangular prism has the following measurements:\n\n- The height is $h = 7$ feet.\n- The length is $l = x$ feet.\n- Let the width be $w$. We are told that the length is $5$ feet more than $3$ times the width:\n$x = 3w + 5$\nSolving for $w$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3w+5 \\\\ x-5 & =3w \\\\ \\frac{x-5}{3} & =w \\\\ w & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the prism is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(x) &= lwh \\\\\n&= x \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(x - 5) \\cdot 7 \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{3}x(x - 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186216997 1074 "From the description, our right rectangular prism has the following measurements:\n\n- The height is $h = 7$ feet.\n- The length is $l = x$ feet.\n- Let the width be $w$. We are told that the length is $5$ feet more than $3$ times the width:\n$x = 3w + 5$\nSolving for $w$, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle x|= 3w + 5; x - 5|= 3w; \\frac{x - 5}{3}|= w; w|= \\frac{1}{3}(x - 5).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the prism is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(x) &= lwh \\\\\n&= x \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(x - 5) \\cdot 7 \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{3}x(x - 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186216998 1074 "From the description, our right cone has the following measurements:\n\n- The radius of the base is $r = x$ centimeters.\n- Let's denote the height by $h$. We're told that the base radius is $4$ centimeters less than one-third the height. So, we have\n$x = \\frac{1}{3}h-4$.\nSolving for $h$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{3}h-4 \\\\ x+4 & =\\frac{1}{3}h \\\\ 3(x+4) & =h \\\\ h & =3(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the cone is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(x) &= \\frac{1}{3}πr^{2}h \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}π \\cdot x^{2} \\cdot 3(x + 4) \\\\\n&= πx^{2}(x + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186216998 1074 "From the description, our right cone has the following measurements:\n\n- The radius of the base is $r = x$ centimeters.\n- Let's denote the height by $h$. We're told that the base radius is $4$ centimeters less than one-third the height. So, we have\n$x = \\frac{1}{3}h-4$.\nSolving for $h$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle x|= \\frac{1}{3}h - 4; x + 4|= \\frac{1}{3}h; 3(x + 4)|= h; h|= 3(x + 4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the cone is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(x) &= \\frac{1}{3}πr^{2}h \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}π \\cdot x^{2} \\cdot 3(x + 4) \\\\\n&= πx^{2}(x + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186217606 1071 "A cylinder has a height of $h$ and a base radius of $r$. The second cylinder has an identical base but is two times higher. The two cylinders are joined together by gluing one of their bases together. Which of the following gives the surface area of the combined solid?\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the surface area $S$ of a cylinder is given by*\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\cdot (\\text{base area})+(\\text{lateral surface area}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\pi {r}^{2}+2\\pi rh\\end{aligned}\n$$\n*where $r$ is the base radius and $h$ is its height.*" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186217606 1071 "A cylinder has a height of $h$ and a base radius of $r$. The second cylinder has an identical base but is two times higher. The two cylinders are joined together by gluing one of their bases together. Which of the following gives the surface area of the combined solid?\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the surface area $S$ of a cylinder is given by*\n$$\n\\displaystyle S|= 2 \\cdot (base area) + (lateral surface area); = 2 \\cdot πr^{2} + 2πrh\n$$\n*where $r$ is the base radius and $h$ is its height.*" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186217608 1071 "Two identical cylinders have a height of $10$ centimeters and a base radius of $5$ centimeters. The cylinders are joined together by gluing one of their bases together. Find the surface area, in square centimeters, of the combined solid.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the surface area $S$ of a cylinder is given by*\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\cdot (\\text{base area})+(\\text{lateral surface area}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\pi {r}^{2}+2\\pi rh\\end{aligned}\n$$\n*where $r$ is the base radius and $h$ is its height.*\n\nThe surface area of the combined solid is\n{{field-1}}\n$cm^{2}$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186217608 1071 "Two identical cylinders have a height of $10$ centimeters and a base radius of $5$ centimeters. The cylinders are joined together by gluing one of their bases together. Find the surface area, in square centimeters, of the combined solid.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the surface area $S$ of a cylinder is given by*\n$$\n\\displaystyle S|= 2 \\cdot (base area) + (lateral surface area); = 2 \\cdot πr^{2} + 2πrh\n$$\n*where $r$ is the base radius and $h$ is its height.*\n\nThe surface area of the combined solid is\n{{field-1}}\n$cm^{2}$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186217610 1