ume (in milliliters) of the first and second solutions, respectively.\n\nLet's establish the linear equations that model the given situation.\n\n- The total volume was $90.0\\,\\text{mL}$, so\n$x + y = 90.0$.\n- We are given that the total salt content is $25.0\\%$ of $90.0\\,\\text{mL}$. Therefore,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25.0\\mathrm{\\%}\\times 90.0\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{mL} & =0.25\\times 90.0\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{mL} \\\\  & =22.50\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{mL}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWe are also told that the first solution was $10.0\\%$ salt and the second was $40.0\\%$ salt. Therefore, we get the equation\n$0.10x + 0.40y = 22.50$.\n\nPutting the equations together, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y = 90.0 \\\\ 0.10 x + 0.40 y = 22.50\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using the substitution method. We solve for $y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + y &= 90.0 \\\\\ny &= 90.0 - x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute\n\n$$\ny = 90.0 - x\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.10x + 0.40y &= 22.50 \\\\\n0.10x + 0.40(90.0 - x) &= 22.50 \\\\\n0.10x + 36.0 - 0.40x &= 22.50 \\\\\n- 0.30x &= -13.50 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{-13.50}{-0.30} \\\\\nx &= 45.0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the first solution was $10.0\\%$ salt by volume, the volume of salt in the first solution used was\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.10x &= 0.10(45.0) \\\\\n&= 4.5mL\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186221014 1074 "Let $x$ and $y$ be the volume (in milliliters) of the first and second solutions, respectively.\n\nLet's establish the linear equations that model the given situation.\n\n- The total volume was $90.0\\,\\text{mL}$, so\n$x + y = 90.0$.\n- We are given that the total salt content is $25.0\\%$ of $90.0\\,\\text{mL}$. Therefore,\n$$\n\\displaystyle 25.0\\% \\cdot 90.0mL|= 0.25 \\cdot 90.0mL; = 22.50mL.\n$$\nWe are also told th