\n\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or $m=-1$, and $m=-1$ is a negative integer. ${\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186225046 1074 "Recall that by the factor theorem, if $(sx-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$, then\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{r}{s}\n$$\n\nis a root of the polynomial.\nThus, since $2x -5m$ is a factor of our polynomial $p(x)$, we must have\n\n$$\np\\left(\\dfrac{5m}{2}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options in turn.\n\n- Option I, $p(x) = 4x^{2}+2x+18m$, is not valid. Here, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle p\\left(\\frac{3c}{2}\\right)|= 4\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right) + 18m; = 25m^{2} + 5m + 18m; = 25m^{2} + 23m; = m(25m + 23).\n$$\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or $m=-\\dfrac{23}{25}$, neither of which is a negative integer. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n*Option II,*\n\n$$\np(x)=4x^{2}+2x+10m\n$$\n\nis not valid. Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np\\left(\\frac{3c}{2}\\right) &= 4\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right) + 10m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 5m + 10m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 15m \\\\\n&= 5m(5m + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or\n\n$$\nm=-\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nneither of which is a negative integer. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n*Option III,*\n\n$$\np(x)=4x^{2}+2x+20m\n$$\n\nis valid. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np\\left(\\frac{3c}{2}\\right) &= 4\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right) + 20m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 5m + 20m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 25m \\\\\n&= 25m(m + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or $m=-1$, and $m=-1$ is a negative integer. ${\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186225071 1074 "First, we notice that the function has three distinct roots, $x = -4$, $x = 0$, and $x = 3$.\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- The graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x = -4$. Therefore, this root is simple (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n- The graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x = 0$. Therefore, this root has even multiplicity.\n- The graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x = 3$. Therefore, this root is simple (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\nTherefore, the equation we need to find has the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = a(x + 4)x^n(x - 3)\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an even natural number.\n\nOf the given options, the following are similar to the form described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^{2}(x - 3), \\\\\nf(x) &= -\\frac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^{2}(x - 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we can notice that the value of the function at $x=1$ is negative, that is, $f(1) \\lt 0$.\n\n- For $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^2(x - 3)$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =\\frac{1}{3}(1+4)(1)^{2}(1-3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{3} \\\\  & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For $f(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^2(x - 3)$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-\\frac{1}{3}(1+4)(1)^{2}(1-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3} \\\\  & ≮0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we discard\n\n$$\nf(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^2(x - 3)\n$$\n\nConsequently, the equation that could represent the graph shown is\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^2(x - 3)\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186225071 1074 "First, we notice that the function has three distinct roots, $x = -4$, $x = 0$, and $x = 3$.\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- The graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x = -4$. Therefore, this root is simple (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n- The graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x = 0$. Therefore, this root has even multiplicity.\n- The graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x = 3$. Therefore, this root is simple (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\nTherefore, the equation we need to find has the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = a(x + 4)x^n(x - 3)\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an even natural number.\n\nOf the given options, the following are similar to the form described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^{2}(x - 3), \\\\\nf(x) &= -\\frac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^{2}(x - 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we can notice that the value of the function at $x=1$ is negative, that is, $f(1) \\lt 0$.\n\n- For $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^2(x - 3)$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(1)|= \\frac{1}{3}(1 + 4)(1)^{2}(1 - 3); =-\\frac{10}{3}; < 0.\n$$\n- For $f(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^2(x - 3)$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(1)|=-\\frac{1}{3}(1 + 4)(1)^{2}(1 - 3); = \\frac{10}{3}; ≮0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we discard\n\n$$\nf(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^2(x - 3)\n$$\n\nConsequently, the equation that could represent the graph shown is\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^2(x - 3)\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186225072 1074 "First, notice that the discriminant of the factor $x^2+x+2$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal D = 1^2-4(1)(2) = -7 < 0\n$$\n\nso our cubic cannot be factored any further over the real numbers.\n\nNow, we sketch the graph of the cubic. From the equation, we conclude the following:\n\n- The graph has an $x$-intercept at $x=3$.\n- $y \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to \\infty$ and $y \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to -\\infty$.\n- The $y$-intercept is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0-3)({0}^{2}+0+2) \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (2) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding partial graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/6e/6efc85f87a2ca97602de742653ccfe08b08cd409a35bca97b5dd3470984f71f0.png)\n\nWe are interested in the points $(a,b)$ whose $x$-coordinate $a$ satisfies\n\n$$\n1 \\leq a \\leq 3\n$$\n\nThe corresponding interval on the $x$-axis is highlighted on the graph.\nNotice that in this interval, the graph of the cubic lies below the $x$-axis. So, we must have\n\n$$\nb \\leq 0\n$$\n\nAmong the given options, only option III does **NOT** satisfy this condition." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186225072 1074 "First, notice that the discriminant of the factor $x^2+x+2$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal D = 1^2-4(1)(2) = -7 < 0\n$$\n\nso our cubic cannot be factored any further over the real numbers.\n\nNow, we sketch the graph of the cubic. From the equation, we conclude the following:\n\n- The graph has an $x$-intercept at $x=3$.\n- $y \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to \\infty$ and $y \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to -\\infty$.\n- The $y$-intercept is\n$$\n\\displaystyle y|= (0 - 3)(0^{2} + 0 + 2); = (-3) \\cdot (2); =-6.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding partial graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/6e/6efc85f87a2ca97602de742653ccfe08b08cd409a35bca97b5dd3470984f71f0.png)\n\nWe are interested in the points $(a,b)$ whose $x$-coordinate $a$ satisfies\n\n$$\n1 \\leq a \\leq 3\n$$\n\nThe corresponding interval on the $x$-axis is highlighted on the graph.\nNotice that in this interval, the graph of the cubic lies below the $x$-axis. So, we must have\n\n$$\nb \\leq 0\n$$\n\nAmong the given options, only option III does **NOT** satisfy this condition." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186225074 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\nz(w) = \\mid 25 - 5w \\mid\n$$\n\nand asked to find the value of $k$ for which $z(k) = 5k$. Therefore, we need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\mid 25 - 5k \\mid = 5k\n$$\n\nWe now split into two cases based on the definition of absolute value.\n\n- First, we consider the case where $25 - 5k \\ge 0$. Then, we have\n$\\mid 25 - 5k \\mid = 25 - 5k$.\nTherefore, our absolute value equation becomes\n$25 - 5k = 5k$.\nSolving the above equation for $k$, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25-5k & =5k \\\\ 25 & =10k \\\\ k & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nNow, we check whether this value of $k$ satisfies the condition for the first case:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25-5\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right) & =25-\\frac{25}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{2} \\\\  & \\ge 0\\hspace{1em}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, $k = \\dfrac{5}{2}$ is a valid solution.\n- Now, we consider the case where $25 - 5k < 0$. Then, we have\n$\\mid 25 - 5k \\mid = -(25 - 5k)$.\nThis equation becomes:\n$$\n\\displaystyle -25 + 5k = 5k \\Rightarrow -25 = 0,\n$$\nwhich is not true. Therefore, no solution arises from this case.\n\nThus, the only value that satisfies the equation $z(k) = 5k$ is\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186225074 1074 "We are given the equation\n\n$$\nz(w) = \\mid 25 - 5w \\mid\n$$\n\nand asked to find the value of $k$ for which $z(k) = 5k$. Therefore, we need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\mid 25 - 5k \\mid = 5k\n$$\n\nWe now split into two cases based on the definition of absolute value.\n\n- First, we consider the case where $25 - 5k \\ge 0$. Then, we have\n$\\mid 25 - 5k \\mid = 25 - 5k$.\nTherefore, our absolute value equation becomes\n$25 - 5k = 5k$.\nSolving the above equation for $k$, we obtain\n$$\n\\displaystyle 25 - 5k|= 5k; 25|= 10k; k|= \\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\nNow, we check whether this value of $k$ satisfies the condition for the first case:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 25 - 5\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)|= 25 - \\frac{25}{2}; = \\frac{25}{2}; \\ge 0✓\n$$\nSo, $k = \\dfrac{5}{2}$ is a valid solution.\n- Now, we consider the case where $25 - 5k < 0$. Then, we have\n$\\mid 25 - 5k \\mid = -(25 - 5k)$.\nThis equation becomes:\n$$\n\\displaystyle -25 + 5k = 5k \\Rightarrow -25 = 0,\n$$\nwhich is not true. Therefore, no solution arises from this case.\n\nThus, the only value that satisfies the equation $z(k) = 5k$ is\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186228748 1074 "We proceed to calculate the mean, median, and mode separately.\n\n- The mean (or average) is calculated by dividing the sum of all the data values by the number of values.Therefore, for our data set, the mean is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{mean} & =\\frac{7(2)+8(3)+9(6)+10(5)+11(3)+15(1)}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{14+24+54+50+33+15}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{190}{20} \\\\  & =9.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The median is the middle value of a data set when the values are arranged in order from smallest to greatest.Since there are $20$ rent amounts ordered, the median is the average of the two middle values, the $10$th and $11$th, which for these data are both $9$. Then, the median is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{median} & =\\frac{9+9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{2} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The mode is the value that occurs most often in the data set.For the data shown, we have\n$\\textrm{mode} = 9$.\n\nTherefore, the correct order is\n\n$$\n\\text{mode} = \\text{median} < \\text{mean}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186228748 1074 "We proceed to calculate the mean, median, and mode separately.\n\n- The mean (or average) is calculated by dividing the sum of all the data values by the number of values.Therefore, for our data set, the mean is\n$$\n\\displaystyle mean|= (7(2) + 8(3) + 9(6) + 10(5) + 11(3) + 15(1))/(20); = \\frac{14 + 24 + 54 + 50 + 33 + 15}{20}; = \\frac{190}{20}; = 9.5.\n$$\n- The median is the middle value of a data set when the values are arranged in order from smallest to greatest.Since there are $20$ rent amounts ordered, the median is the average of the two middle values, the $10$th and $11$th, which for these data are both $9$. Then, the median is\n$$\n\\displaystyle median|= \\frac{9 + 9}{2}; = \\frac{18}{2}; = 9.\n$$\n- The mode is the value that occurs most often in the data set.For the data shown, we have\n$\\textrm{mode} = 9$.\n\nTherefore, the correct order is\n\n$$\n\\text{mode} = \\text{median} < \\text{mean}\n$$" 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186228752 1074 "The mean (or average) is calculated by dividing the sum of all the data values by the number of values.\n\nWe proceed to determine the mean of the data sets from each of the given frequency tables.\n\n- For the table\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-t