ue by the same constant scales the mean by the same factor.If we denote the $19$ different totals by $t_1, t_2, \\ldots, t_{19}$ and multiply each by $2$, then the new mean is:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2t_{1} + 2t_{2} + ⋯ + 2t_{19}}{19}|= (2 \\cdot (t_{1} + t_{2} + ⋯ + t_{19}))/(19); = 2 \\cdot 50; = 100\n$$\nSo, the mean also changes.\n- The range depends only on the difference between the largest and the smallest values. Multiplying both by the same positive constant multiplies that difference by the same constant. Thus, the range is scaled by $2$, so it changes.\n- The standard deviation measures the spread about the mean. Since every value (and thus the mean) is multiplied by $2$, each deviation from the mean is also multiplied by $2$; hence the variance scales by $4$ and the standard deviation by $2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None\"." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186228852 1074 "Let's compare the medians and ranges of data sets $C$ and $D{:}$\n\n- Medians:There are $19$ values, so the median of $C$ is the $10$th value when ordered. Counting frequencies across $9,10,11,12,13$ gives cumulative counts $3,7,12,16,19$, so the $10$th value lies at\n$\\text{Median}(C)=11$.After subtracting $12$ from each value in data set $C$, the median of $D$ will still be the $10$th value, but shifted down by $12{:}$\n$\\text{Median}(D)=11-12=-1$.Thus, the median of data set $D$ is smaller than the median of data set $C$.\n- Ranges: The range of data set $C{:}$\n$\\text{Range}(C)=\\max(C)-\\min(C)=13-9=4$.After subtracting $12$ from each value in data set $C$, the range of data set $D{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Range}(D) & =(max(C)-12)-(min(C)-12) \\\\  & =max(C)-12-min(C)+12 \\\\  & =max(C)-min(C) \\\\  & =\\text{Range}(C).\\end{aligned}\n$$Thus, the ranges are equal.\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is “The median of data set $D$ is smaller than the median of data set $C$, and the range of data set $D$ is equal to the range of data set $C$.”" 