us, the factored form of the expression is $4(8x+3)$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186231337 1074 "We factor in two steps:\n\n1. Separate the greatest common factor from each of the terms (ignoring any negative signs). In this case, $\\textrm{GCF}(63x,36) = 9$, so:\n$63x - 36|=; (9 \\cdot 7x) - (9 \\cdot 4)$\n2. Place the common factor in front of the parentheses, leaving whatever remains inside the parentheses:\n$(9 \\cdot 7x) - (9 \\cdot 4)|=; 9(7x - 4)$\n\nThus, the factored form of the expression is $9(7x-4)$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186231337 1074 "We factor in two steps:\n\n1. Separate the greatest common factor from each of the terms (ignoring any negative signs). In this case, $\\textrm{GCF}(63x,36) = 9$, so:\n$\\begin{aligned}63x-36 &= (9\\cdot 7x)-(9\\cdot 4)\\end{aligned}$\n2. Place the common factor in front of the parentheses, leaving whatever remains inside the parentheses:\n$\\begin{aligned}(9\\cdot 7x)-(9\\cdot 4) &= 9(7x-4)\\end{aligned}$\n\nThus, the factored form of the expression is $9(7x-4)$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186231338 1074 "We factor in two steps:\n\n1. Separate the greatest common factor from each of the terms (ignoring any negative signs). In this case, $\\textrm{GCF}(4x,20y,12) = 4$, so\n$4x + 20y - 12|=; (4 \\cdot x) + (4 \\cdot 5y) - (4 \\cdot 3)$.\n2. Place the common factor in front of the parentheses, leaving whatever remains inside the parentheses:\n$(4 \\cdot x) + (4 \\cdot 5y) - (4 \\cdot 3)|=; 4(x + 5y - 3)$\n\nThus, the factored form of the expression is $4(x + 5y - 3)$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 false] [17592186231338 1074 "We factor in two steps:\n\n1. Separate the greatest common factor from each of the terms (ignoring any negative signs). In this case, $\\textrm{GCF}(4x,20y,12) = 4$, so\n$\\begin{aligned}4x+20y-12 &= (4\\cdot x)+(4\\cdot 5y)-(4\\cdot 3).\\end{aligned}$.\n2. Place the common factor in front of the parentheses, leaving whatever remains inside the parentheses:\n$\\begin{aligned}(4\\cdot x)+(4\\cdot 5y)-(4\\cdot 3) &= 4(x+5y-3)\\end{aligned}$\n\nThus, the factored form of the expression is $4(x + 5y - 3)$." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186231342 1074 "To determine which of the expressions are equivalent to $4(3x-7)$, we first collect like terms and then factor each result:\n\n- We factor out the greatest common factor in expression I. Since $\\text{GCF}(12x,28)=4$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-28 &= (4\\cdot 3x)-(4\\cdot 7) \\\\ &= 4\\cdot (3x-7) && .\\hspace{2em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, expression I *is* equivalent to $4(3x-7)$.\n- There are two like terms in expression II, $5x$ and $-2x$. Combining these terms, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-7-2x &= 5x-2x-7 \\\\ &= (5-2)x-7 \\\\ &= 3x-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nNow, we factor out the greatest common factor. Since $\\text{GCF}(3x,7)=1$, there is nothing to factor out.\n$3x - 7 \\qquad{\\color{red}\\times}$\nSo, expression II *is not* equivalent to $4(3x-7)$.\n- There are two like terms in expression III, $6x$ and $6x$. Combining these terms, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+6x-32 &= (6+6)x-32 \\\\ &= 12x-32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nNow, we factor out the greatest common factor. Since $\\text{GCF}(12x,32)=4$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-32 &= (4\\cdot 3x)-(4\\cdot 8) \\\\ &= 4\\cdot (3x-8) && .\\hspace{2em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, expression III *is not* equivalent to $4(3x-7)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139541978 17592186576411 true] [17592186231342 1074 "To determine which of the expressions are equivalent to $4(3x-7)$, we first collect like terms and then factor each result:\n\n- We factor out the greatest common factor in expression I. Since $\\text{GCF}(12x,28)=4$, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle 12x - 28|=; (4 \\cdot 3x) - (4 \\cdot 7)|=; 4 \\cdot (3x - 7) \\mid.✓\n$$\nSo, expression I *is* equivalent to $4(3x-7)$.\n- There are two like terms in expression II, $5x$ and $-2x$. Combini